(function(){
  var chunk = [{"id":922,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"nk must be connected to the same secure online platform which\n enables electronic presentations and electronic document\n examinations.\n\n Rules: (eUCP): In year 2002, the Banking Commission created\n supplementary rules to UCP500, which enabled the presentation of\n electronic records alone or in combination with paper documents.\n\n The supplementary rules are known as the “Supplement to the\n Uniform Customs and Practice for Documentary Credits for Electronic\n Presentation,” or simply “eUCP“.\n\n eUCP came into effect on April 1, 2002 and was revised in 2007\n following the replacement of UCP500 with UCP 600.\n\n1.7 STEP-BY-STEP ELECTRONIC LETTER OF CREDIT\n TRANSACTION PROCESS\n 1. Electronic letter of credit transaction starts with the signature\n of the sales contract between the exporter and the importer\n through a secure online platform.\n 2. The applicant (importer) applies to the issuing bank"},{"id":923,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"mporter\n through a secure online platform.\n 2. The applicant (importer) applies to the issuing bank for the\n opening of an electronic letter of credit which should be subject\n to latest version of eUCP rules.\n 3. The issuing bank issues its electronic letter of credit and sends\n the credit to the advising bank through a multi-bank platform.\n 4. The beneficiary (exporter) of the electronic letter of credit\n receives the authenticated copy of the e-credit through the\n secure online multi-bank platform.\n\n 19\n\f5. At the same time, the applicant (importer) applies to the\n transport company to make sure that an electronic bill of lading\n will be issued instead of a regular paper-based bill of lading.\n6. The beneficiary makes the electronic presentation to the\n advising bank. Electronic presentation contains electronic\n documents, including a digitally signed e-bill of lading.\n7. The advising b"},{"id":924,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ion contains electronic\n documents, including a digitally signed e-bill of lading.\n7. The advising bank checks the documents. If documents require\n no correction, then the advising bank makes the electronic\n presentation to the issuing bank.\n8. The issuing bank checks the documents. If documents are\n found to be compliant, then the issuing bank honours its letter\n of credit.\n9. The issuing bank sends e-documents to the applicant.\n\nBenefits of Electronic Letters of Credit\n• Cost reduction through operational efficiency:\n Traditional paper documents have much higher operational\n costs compared to electronic documentation.\n• International courier costs: In each set of documents you\n submit to your bank under a paper-based letter of credit, you\n should be paying 75-100 USD. You could save this amount with\n electronic L/Cs.\n• Domestic Document Gathering Costs: Most of the export\n and import d"},{"id":925,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"amount with\n electronic L/Cs.\n• Domestic Document Gathering Costs: Most of the export\n and import documents are created quite far away from the\n manufacturing companies, as production plants are located\n away from the city centres, where the land prices are low.\n Some documents are created at the custom offices which are\n located just inside the port of discharge. Collecting different\n paper-based documents from different locations may cost a\n considerable amount of money, especially when the exporters\n are racing for a timely presentation of the letters of credit.\n• Financial Advantages: Financial gains as a result of using\n electronic letters of credit could be summed up under the\n following points:\n -Reaching to the Payments Faster via Online Presentations:\n Under paper-based presentations, the beneficiaries could\n\n 20\n\f collect all required letters of credit documents within 4-5 day"},{"id":926,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ions, the beneficiaries could\n\n 20\n\f collect all required letters of credit documents within 4-5 days\n after the date of shipment. Documents could reach to the\n issuing banks or confirming banks via expedited courier\n services in 3-7 days under normal conditions. In conclusion, it\n is fair to expect under an at-sight letter of credit, which allows\n paper-based presentations, that the beneficiary could access\n the payment within 12-20 days after the date of shipment. On\n the other hand, the beneficiary may be able to access the\n payment under an electronic letter of credit, which is available\n at sight within 3-4 days after the date of shipment.\n • Benefits of Reduced Risk Levels: Electronic letters of credit\n could eliminate, or at least reduce, various risks of the\n exporters and importers as defined below:\n ✓ Electronic Documents can be corrected easily, which\n makes them less risky in"},{"id":927,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"s as defined below:\n ✓ Electronic Documents can be corrected easily, which\n makes them less risky in terms of refusals by the issuing\n banks: Correction on electronic documents is easier and\n faster than paper-based documents. As a result,\n exporters’ presentation refusal risks are reduced\n significantly.\n ✓ Documents reach to Issuing Bank Earlier: On some\n occasions, the distance between the exporter’s and the\n importer’s countries is quite long that paper documents\n could reach the issuing bank long after the vessel has\n arrived at the port of destination. Such a situation could\n create some problems that may involve extra risks and\n costs to both exporters and importers as highlighted\n below:\n ➢ Demurrage charges;\n ➢ Risk and costs associated with issuance of a Letter\n of Indemnity.\n\n1.8 HOW TO OPEN A LETTER OF CREDIT\n\n Who Opens the Letter of Credit?\n The issuing banks open the lette"},{"id":928,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"8 HOW TO OPEN A LETTER OF CREDIT\n\n Who Opens the Letter of Credit?\n The issuing banks open the letters of credit according to the\n instructions they have received from the importers. We can say that\n\n 21\n\fletters of credit are opened by importers’ banks in favour of the\nexporters according to the instructions they have received from the\nimporters. Importers define the terms and conditions of the letters of\ncredit.\n\nStep-by-Step Procedures for Opening a Letter of Credit\n\n Step 1: Signing of the Sales Contract\n Step 2: Letter of Credit Application\n Step 3: Completing the Letter of Credit Application Form\n Step 4: Evaluation of the Application\n Step 5: Approval of the Application and Issuance\n\nStep 1: Signing of the Sales Contract: In letters of credit\nterminology, the importer is called the applicant. The applicant plays\na key role in a letter of credit transaction from the beginning to th"},{"id":929,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"applicant. The applicant plays\na key role in a letter of credit transaction from the beginning to the\nend.\n\nThe importer finds a supplier and then signs a sales contract with the\nsupplier. On the sales contract, the importer has to indicate the terms\nand conditions of the sale, including, but not limited to, the delivery\nterms, description of goods, delivery date, price and quantity of the\ngoods, package type, insurance coverage, legal details, etc. Please\nnote that a pro forma invoice could be used instead of a sales contract.\n\nStep 2: Letter of Credit Application: After the signing of the sales\ncontract, the importer applies to his bank to have the letter of credit\nissued in favour of the exporter.\nThe importer has to make sure that the letter of credit application is\nin accordance with the terms of the sales agreement.\n\nStep 3: Completing the Letter of Credit Application Form: After\nt"},{"id":930,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"the terms of the sales agreement.\n\nStep 3: Completing the Letter of Credit Application Form: After\nthe importer applies to his bank for issuance of an import letter of\ncredit, the issuing bank requests him to fill an application form, which\nis called “Letter of Credit Application Form” or “Documentary Letter of\n\n 22\n\fCredit – Application Form”. The letter of credit application form could\nbe in electronic or hard copy format.\n\nIn general, the application forms are similar to each other, whether\noffline or online, due to the fact that they follow the swift message\nbody of issuance of letter of credit.\n\nBanks use MT 700 - Issue of a Documentary Credit swift message -\nwhen issuing a letter of credit.\n\nThe issuing bank may request the importer to attach the pro forma\ninvoice or sales contract to the application form in order to ensure that\nthe letter of credit application form reflects the co"},{"id":931,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"o the application form in order to ensure that\nthe letter of credit application form reflects the correct data stated on\nthe pro forma invoice.\n\nStep 4: Evaluation of the Application: At this stage, the issuing\nbank evaluates the letter of credit application.\n\nIf the issuing bank decides to issue the letter of credit, then the issuing\nbank takes action to secure its payment from the importer.\n\nStep 5: Approval of the Application and Issuance: The issuing\nbank creates the letter of credit and sends it to the importer for\napproval. At that point, it would be a good idea to have the draft L/C\nchecked by the exporter. This will eliminate costly possibly future\namendments.\n\nThe draft letter of credit will be also in swift message format and\nreflect all the details of the final letter of credit.\n\nOnce the draft letter of credit is confirmed by the exporter, the\nimporter can give final approval"},{"id":932,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"Once the draft letter of credit is confirmed by the exporter, the\nimporter can give final approval to the issuing bank for the issuance\nof the LC.\n\nHow to Cancel a Letter of Credit\n\n 23\n\fAccording to the letter of credit rules, a letter of credit should be\nissued in an irrevocable form; as a result, it cannot be cancelled\nwithout the written consent of the beneficiary.\n\nDefinition of Irrevocability Concept and Its Effects on\nCancellation of Letters of Credit\nAccording to letter of credit rules, a credit can neither be amended\nnor cancelled without the agreement of the issuing bank, the\nconfirming bank, if any, and the beneficiary.\n\nA letter of credit is a conditional payment method, which means that\nthe payment can be made only against a complying presentation; but\nthe terms and conditions of the credit cannot be amended or else\ncannot be cancelled by the issuing bank without the conse"},{"id":933,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ns of the credit cannot be amended or else\ncannot be cancelled by the issuing bank without the consent of the\nbeneficiary. As a result, issuing banks cannot cancel letters of credit\nby themselves alone.\n\nIn order to cancel a letter of credit, an issuing bank has to receive a\nwritten declaration from the beneficiary certifying that the letter of\ncredit will not be utilised.\n\nIf the letter of credit has been confirmed by another bank, the\nconfirming bank’s written declaration should also be received before\nthe cancellation.\n\nThere are two ways to cancel a letter of credit.\nActually, the first scenario should be treated as a “termination” rather\nthan a “cancellation”.\n\nCancellation of a letter of credit which is not utilised by the\nbeneficiary within the validity period:\nEvery letter of credit must have an expiry date and place.\nIf the beneficiary elects not to use the letter of credit with"},{"id":934,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"t must have an expiry date and place.\nIf the beneficiary elects not to use the letter of credit within the validity\nperiod, which starts with the issuance of the letter of credit and ends\nwith the expiry date, the letter of credit will be no longer valid.\n\n 24\n\fThis type of cancellation is referred to as termination of letter of credit\nby the beneficiary.\n\nWhat are the factors that may force the beneficiary to\nterminate the letter of credit?\n\n✓ The unit prices may have increased too much since the opening\n of the letter of credit and the beneficiary may perceive the\n transaction not profitable any more.\n✓ The beneficiary may find the terms and conditions of the letter\n of credit unattractive.\n✓ The beneficiary may not be able to find the required finance to\n perform under the letter of credit.\n\nCancellation of a letter of credit by the beneficiary’s written\ndeclaration within the validit"},{"id":935,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"dit.\n\nCancellation of a letter of credit by the beneficiary’s written\ndeclaration within the validity period:\nAs explained above, an irrevocable letter of credit cannot be cancelled\nwithout the written consent of the beneficiary and the confirming\nbank, if any.\n\nAs a result, a letter of credit cancellation process should be started\nwith the beneficiary’s written declaration.\n\nThis can be done by a letter which is issued by the beneficiary and\nsent directly to the issuing bank, or else a swift message sent by the\nadvising bank to the issuing bank.\n\nCase Study: Can the Issuing Bank Cancel the L/C by Itself on the\nGrounds of Bad Quality of Goods?\n\nSummary\nA letter of credit was issued by National Import Bank in Saudi Arabia\nfor USD5,500,000 allowing partial shipments.\n\nIt was planned under the sales contract that the goods will be loaded\nvia 5 partial shipments, with each equal to USD1,100,"},{"id":936,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"e sales contract that the goods will be loaded\nvia 5 partial shipments, with each equal to USD1,100,000.\n\n 25\n\fThe letter of credit was available with a nominated bank located in\nGermany for payment. The nominated bank in Germany just advised\nthe credit without adding its confirmation.\n\nAdditionally, the nominated bank did not make any payments to the\nbeneficiary. Its role was rather an advising bank’s role under this\ntransaction.\n\nThe beneficiary made the 1st shipment and received the payment\nfrom the issuing bank.\n\nOn the 2nd shipment, the beneficiary did not get any payment from\nthe issuing bank, although there was no advice of refusal received.\n\nLater on, the beneficiary received the following message from the\nissuing bank via the nominated bank: “Goods are defective, as stated\nby the buyer, the said L/C is cancelled.”\n\nAnswer: The letter of credit cannot be cancelled by the issuing"},{"id":937,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"buyer, the said L/C is cancelled.”\n\nAnswer: The letter of credit cannot be cancelled by the issuing bank\nby itself on the grounds of bad quality of goods. Only a court order\ncould stop the issuing bank from paying for the complying documents.\n\nParties to Letters of Credit\nThe main parties to a typical letter of credit transaction are: the\napplicant, the beneficiary, the issuing bank, the confirming bank, the\nnominated bank, the advising bank and the reimbursing bank.\n\n 26\n\fApplicant\nThe applicant is the buyer/importer of the goods or services supplied\nby the seller. A letter of credit is opened by the issuing bank as per\nthe applicant’s request. He is sometimes called the opener or the\naccreditor.\n\nBeneficiary\nThe beneficiary is the seller/exporter of the goods or the provider of\nthe services in a standard commercial letter of credit transaction. A\nletter of credit is opened by the issui"},{"id":938,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ces in a standard commercial letter of credit transaction. A\nletter of credit is opened by the issuing bank in favour of the\nbeneficiary.\nThe beneficiary will be eligible to receive payment under both\ncommercial and standby letters of credit, as long as they make\ncompliant presentations.\n\nIssuing Bank\nThe issuing bank is the bank that issues a letter of credit at the request\nof an applicant or on its own behalf. The issuing bank undertakes to\nhonour a compliant presentation of the beneficiary without recourse\nto him, which means that the issuing bank must pay the letter of credit\namount to the beneficiary, if a complying presentation has been made.\n\nNominated Bank\nA nominated bank is the bank with which the credit is available.\nNominated banks play a key role in determining whether or not the\ndocuments are presented within the allowed period or not.\n\nAdvising Bank\nThe advising bank is th"},{"id":939,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"the\ndocuments are presented within the allowed period or not.\n\nAdvising Bank\nThe advising bank is the bank that advises the credit at the request\nof the issuing bank. An advising bank that is not a confirming bank\nadvises the credit and any amendment without any obligation to\nhonour.\n\nBy advising the credit or amendment, the advising bank signifies that\nit has satisfied itself as to the apparent authenticity of the credit or\n\n 27\n\famendment, and that the advice accurately reflects the terms and\nconditions of the credit.\n\nConfirming Bank\nThe confirming bank is the bank that adds its confirmation to a credit\nupon the issuing bank’s authorisation or request. A confirming bank\nmay or may not add its confirmation to a letter of credit. This decision\nis up to the confirming bank only.\n\nHowever, once it adds its confirmation to the credit, the confirming\nbank is irrevocably bound to honour or n"},{"id":940,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"once it adds its confirmation to the credit, the confirming\nbank is irrevocably bound to honour or negotiate as of the time it adds\nits confirmation to the credit. Even if the issuing bank fails to honour,\nthe confirming bank must pay to the beneficiary.\n\nReimbursing Bank\nThe reimbursing bank is the bank instructed and/or authorised to\nprovide reimbursement pursuant to a reimbursement authorisation\nissued by the issuing bank to the nominated or confirming bank.\nThe reimbursing bank is the settlement bank between the issuing\nbank and the nominated bank or the confirming bank.\n\nIf a letter of credit currency is USD, the reimbursing bank is usually\nlocated in the US. If, however, the letter of credit currency is EUR, the\nreimbursing bank is generally located in Germany.\n\nWhat Does Reimbursement Mean?\nReimbursement is defined as compensation paid to someone for an\nexpense.\n\nIn order to be re"},{"id":941,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"nt Mean?\nReimbursement is defined as compensation paid to someone for an\nexpense.\n\nIn order to be reimbursed by another person or organisation, first of\nall, you have to make some expenses and then you will be qualified\nfor a reimbursement.\n\nThe same logic applies to reimbursement as a trade finance term.\n\n 28\n\fA confirming bank or a nominated bank first honours or negotiates\nagainst a complying presentation under a letter of credit and then it\nwill be reimbursed by the reimbursing banks in accordance with the\nissuing bank’s authorisation.\n\nReimbursing Bank Interpretation\nThe reimbursing bank is the bank that, at the request of the issuing\nbank, is authorised to pay, or accept and pay a time draft under a\ndocumentary credit in accordance with UCP 600 Article 13; or, if\nincorporated, the ICC Uniform Rules for Bank-to-Bank\nReimbursements under Documentary Credits (URR 725). (1)\n\nWhat is th"},{"id":942,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"C Uniform Rules for Bank-to-Bank\nReimbursements under Documentary Credits (URR 725). (1)\n\nWhat is the Function of a Reimbursing Bank under a Letter of\nCredit?\nThere are two possible reasons that make a reimbursing bank\nnecessary under a documentary credit transaction:\n\ni. The first reason may be that the confirming bank does not trust\n the issuing bank in terms of risk issues, and so demands more\n security. In this situation, the presence of the reimbursing bank\n is a requirement for the confirmation.\n\n Without receiving a reimbursement undertaking from the\n reimbursing bank, the confirming bank may elect not to add its\n confirmation to the credit.\n\n Note: Please keep in mind that the confirmation process is a\n commercial decision and no bank is forced to add its\n confirmation to any letter of credit.\n\nii. The second reason could be that the issuing bank and the\n nominated bank (in case"},{"id":943,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"r of credit.\n\nii. The second reason could be that the issuing bank and the\n nominated bank (in case of a confirmed letter of credit, the\n confirming bank) do not have an account relationship; as a\n result, they may be requiring a third bank’s service for the\n settlement.\n\n 29\n\f What are the Rules Covering Bank-to-Bank Reimbursements\n under Letters of Credit?\n There are two options for the reimbursement rules:\n\n(i) Reimbursements must be governed either by URR 725 or UCP 600\n Article 13 for the letters of credit which are opened according to\n latest documentary credit rules.\n\n URR 725 – The Uniform Rules for Bank-to-Bank Reimbursements\n under Documentary Credits – ICC Publication No. 725: This is a\n small-sized booklet published by ICC that specifically governs the\n bank-to-bank reimbursements. If the issuing bank would like to\n make the reimbursement subject to URR 725, then it must make"},{"id":944,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"nts. If the issuing bank would like to\n make the reimbursement subject to URR 725, then it must make\n a reference on the swift message when issuing a letter of credit.\n\n(ii) If the issuing bank puts a reference to MT700 swift message under\n field “40E- Applicable Rules” as “UCPURR LATEST VERSION,” that\n means the letter of credit is subject to the latest version of letter\n of credit rules (UCP 600) and the latest version of bank-to-bank\n reimbursements rules (URR 725).\n\n Note: Additionally, the reimbursement authorisation should\n expressly indicate that it is subject to URR 725 – The Uniform Rules\n for Bank-to-Bank Reimbursements under Documentary Credits.\n\n UCP 600 Article 13: If a credit does not state that reimbursement\n is subject to the ICC rules for bank-to-bank reimbursements, the\n UCP 600 Article 13 applies.\n\n Some important definitions from URR 725 – The Uniform\n Rules for Bank-"},{"id":945,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"UCP 600 Article 13 applies.\n\n Some important definitions from URR 725 – The Uniform\n Rules for Bank-to-Bank Reimbursements under Documentary\n Credits\n\n “Claiming bank” means a bank that honours or negotiates a credit and\n presents a reimbursement claim to the reimbursing bank. “Claiming\n bank” includes a bank authorised to present a reimbursement claim\n\n 30\n\fto the reimbursing bank on behalf of the bank that honours or\nnegotiates.\n\n“Issuing bank” means the bank that has issued a credit and the\nreimbursement authorisation under that credit.\n\n“Reimbursing bank” means the bank instructed or authorised to\nprovide reimbursement pursuant to a reimbursement authorisation\nissued by the issuing bank.\n\n“Reimbursement Authorisation” means an instruction or authorisation,\nindependent of the credit, issued by an issuing bank to a reimbursing\nbank to reimburse a claiming bank or, if so requested by th"},{"id":946,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"sued by an issuing bank to a reimbursing\nbank to reimburse a claiming bank or, if so requested by the issuing\nbank, to accept and pay a time draft drawn on the reimbursing bank.\n\n“Reimbursement Undertaking” means a separate irrevocable\nundertaking of the reimbursing bank, issued upon the authorisation\nor request of the issuing bank, to the claiming bank named in the\nreimbursement authorisation, to honour that bank’s reimbursement\nclaim, provided the terms and conditions of the reimbursement\nundertaking have been complied with.\n\n“Reimbursement Claim” means a request for reimbursement from the\nclaiming bank to the reimbursing bank.\n\nReimbursement Transaction under a Letter of Credit:\n1. The issuing bank issues the letter of credit and transmits it to\n the nominated bank via swift message.\n2. The issuing bank gives reimbursement authorisation to the\n reimbursing bank.\n3. The reimbursing ban"},{"id":947,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"The issuing bank gives reimbursement authorisation to the\n reimbursing bank.\n3. The reimbursing bank issues its reimbursement undertaking\n and transmits it to the nominated bank via swift message.\n4. The nominated bank advises the letter of credit to the\n beneficiary. (Please keep in mind that step 3 and step 4 may\n change their sequence.)\n5. The beneficiary presents documents to the nominated bank.\n\n 31\n\f 6. The nominated bank checks the documents and negotiates\n upon a complying presentation.\n 7. The nominated bank sends its reimbursement claim to the\n reimbursing bank. The reimbursing bank reimburses the\n nominated bank according to terms and conditions of the\n reimbursement undertaking.\n 8. The nominated bank sends documents to the issuing bank.\n 9. At the final stage, the issuing bank and the reimbursing bank\n arrange settlement between themselves. The issuing bank gets\n the letter"},{"id":948,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"k and the reimbursing bank\n arrange settlement between themselves. The issuing bank gets\n the letter of credit amount from the applicant and releases the\n original shipment documents.\n\n1.9 WORKABILITY OF THE CREDIT\n How Does a Negotiable Letter of Credit Work?\n\n What Does Negotiation Mean?\n Negotiation means the purchase by the nominated bank of drafts\n (drawn on a bank other than the nominated bank) and/or documents\n under a complying presentation, by advancing or agreeing to advance\n funds to the beneficiary on or before the banking day on which\n reimbursement is due to the nominated bank.\n\n What Are the Benefits of a Negotiable Letter of Credit to the\n Exporters?\n Exporters can reach the payment sooner with negotiable letters of\n credit, while offering usance terms to the importers.\n\n With the help of the negotiable letters of credit, exporters can balance\n their cash flows, and be ab"},{"id":949,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ith the help of the negotiable letters of credit, exporters can balance\n their cash flows, and be able to propose competitive payment terms\n to the importers.\n\n Who Should Pay Negotiation Fees?\n Negotiation fees are generally covered by the exporters, although this\n is contrary to the letter of credit rules.\n\n How to Understand if a Letter of Credit is Negotiable or Not?\n\n 32\n\fIn order to understand if a letter of credit is negotiable or not, you\nneed to look at field“41A-Available with/by” field in an MT700 swift\nmessage.\n\nIf a letter of credit is negotiable, it must be mentioned under field 41A\nthat the letter of credit is available by negotiation.\n\nHow Does a Negotiable Letter of Credit Work?\n\nStep 1: The exporter and importer enter into a sales contract by\n agreeing on the terms and conditions of the business\n transaction.\n\nStep 2: The importer contacts the issuing bank for the issua"},{"id":950,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"nditions of the business\n transaction.\n\nStep 2: The importer contacts the issuing bank for the issuance\n of the negotiable letter of credit.\n\nStep 3: The issuing bank issues the negotiable letter of credit in\n swift format and sends it to the nominated bank, which\n is also the negotiating bank and the advising bank.\n\nStep 4: The negotiating bank advises the letter of credit to the\n exporter. The exporter checks the letter of credit\n conditions; if they are acceptable to the exporter, he\n starts production of the goods.\n\nStep 5: The exporter ships the goods within the validity of the\n letter of credit and not later than the latest date of\n shipment indicated in the L/C.\n\nStep 6: The exporter presents the documents to the negotiating\n bank within the presentation period allowed under the\n letter of credit. Remember that, if presented documents\n contain a transport document, presentation mu"},{"id":951,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"tter of credit. Remember that, if presented documents\n contain a transport document, presentation must be\n completed within 21 days after date of shipment.\n\n 33\n\f Step 7: The negotiating bank checks the documents presented\n by the exporter and, if it is determined that they are\n compliant, the negotiating bank advances cash to the\n exporter. The “negotiation” is effectively the purchase\n of documents from the exporter at a discount.\n\n Step 8: The negotiating bank presents the documents to the\n issuing bank.\n\n Step 9: The issuing bank checks the documents and, if\n compliant, accepts them to be paid to the negotiating\n bank at maturity. At the same time, the issuing bank\n gets in touch with the importer and delivers documents\n to him according to the financial agreement between the\n issuing bank and the importer.\n\n1.10 GOVERNING LAWS WITH RESPECT TO LETTER OF CREDIT\n Uniform Customs and Pr"},{"id":952,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"bank and the importer.\n\n1.10 GOVERNING LAWS WITH RESPECT TO LETTER OF CREDIT\n Uniform Customs and Practice for Documentary Credit (UCP)\n • UCP is a set of internationally accepted rules and regulations\n that covers the liabilities and duties of all parties to the credit.\n • It is a publication of the International Chamber of Commerce\n (ICC), and published from time to time.\n • The latest publication is the 2007 Revision, ICC Publication No.\n 600.\n • All bank authorities on advice of documentary credits, will state\n that the credit is subject to UCP 600.\n • The following rules apply in order of importance to\n documentary letters of credit:\n (i) National laws of the parties to the credit.\n (ii) Legal and enforceable agreement between parties.\n (iii) UCP.\n\n 34\n\fUniform Customs and Practice for Documentary Credits\n(UCP)\n\nICC Uniform Customs and Practice for Documentary Credits\n2007 Revision"},{"id":953,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"r Documentary Credits\n(UCP)\n\nICC Uniform Customs and Practice for Documentary Credits\n2007 Revision UCP 600\n\nArticle 1: Application of UCP\nThe Uniform Customs and Practice for Documentary Credits, 2007\nRevision, ICC Publication No. 600 (“UCP”) are rules that apply to any\ndocumentary credit (“credit”) (including, to the extent to which they\nmay be applicable, any standby letter of credit) when the text of the\ncredit expressly indicates that it is subject to these rules. They are\nbinding on all parties thereto, unless expressly modified or excluded\nby the credit.\n\nArticle 2 Definitions\nFor the purpose of these rules:\nAdvising bank means the bank that advises the credit at the request\nof the issuing bank.\n\nApplicant means the party on whose request the credit is issued.\n\nBanking day means a day on which a bank is regularly open at the\nplace at which an act, subject to these rules is to be p"},{"id":954,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ay on which a bank is regularly open at the\nplace at which an act, subject to these rules is to be performed.\n\nBeneficiary means the party in whose favour a credit is issued.\n\nComplying presentation means a presentation that is in\naccordance with the terms and conditions of the credit for the\napplicable provisions of the rules for international standard for\nbanking practice.\n\nConfirmation means a definite undertaking of the confirming bank,\nin addition to that of the issuing bank, to honour or negotiate a\ncomplying presentation.\n\n 35\n\fConfirming bank means the bank that adds its confirmation to a\ncredit upon the issuing bank’s authorisation or request.\n\nCredit means any arrangement, however named or described, that is\nirrevocable and thereby constitutes a definite undertaking of the\nissuing bank to honour a complying presentation.\n\nHonour means:\n(a) to pay at sight if the credit is avail"},{"id":955,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"g bank to honour a complying presentation.\n\nHonour means:\n(a) to pay at sight if the credit is available by sight payment.\n(b) to incur a deferred payment undertaking and pay at maturity if\n the credit is available by deferred payment.\n(c) to accept a bill of exchange (“draft”) drawn by the beneficiary\n and pay at maturity if the credit is available by acceptance.\n\nIssuing bank means the bank that issues a credit at the request of\nan applicant or on its own behalf.\n\nNegotiation means the purchase by the nominated bank of drafts\n(drawn on a bank other than the nominated bank) and/or documents\nunder a complying presentation, by advancing or agreeing to advance\nfunds to the beneficiary on or before the banking day on which\nreimbursement is due to the nominated bank.\n\nNominated bank means the bank with which the credit is available.\n\nPresentation means either the delivery of documents under"},{"id":956,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"bank with which the credit is available.\n\nPresentation means either the delivery of documents under a credit\nto the issuing bank or nominated bank, or the documents so delivered.\n\nPresenter means a beneficiary, bank or other party that makes a\npresentation.\n\nArticle 3: Interpretations\nFor the purpose of these rules:\nWhere applicable, words in the singular include the plural and in the\nplural include the singular.\n\n 36\n\fA credit is irrevocable even if there is no indication to that effect.\nA document may be signed by handwriting, facsimile signature,\nperforated signature, stamp, symbol or any other mechanical or\nelectronic method of authentication.\n\nA requirement for a document to be legalised, viewed, certified or\nsimilar will be satisfied by any signature, mark, stamp or label on the\ndocument which appears to satisfy that requirement.\n\nBranches of a bank in different countries are consi"},{"id":957,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ment which appears to satisfy that requirement.\n\nBranches of a bank in different countries are considered to be\nseparate banks.\n\nTerms such as \"first class\", \"well known\", \"qualified\", \"independent\",\n\"official\", \"competent\" or \"local\" used to describe the issuer of a\ndocument allow any issuer except the beneficiary to issue that\ndocument.\n\nUnless required to be used in a document, words such as \"prompt\",\n\"immediately\" or \"as soon as possible\" will be disregarded.\n\nThe expression \"on or about\" or any similar one will be interpreted as\na stipulation that an event is to occur during a period of five calendar\ndays before or until five calendar days after the specified date, both\nstart and end dates are included.\n\nThe words \"to\", \"until\", \"till\", “from” and “between” when used to\ndetermine a period of shipment include the date or dates mentioned,\nand the words “before” and \"after\" exclude the"},{"id":958,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"riod of shipment include the date or dates mentioned,\nand the words “before” and \"after\" exclude the date mentioned.\n\nThe words “from” and \"after\" when used to determine a maturity date\nexclude the date mentioned.\n\nThe terms \"first half\" and \"second half\" of a month shall be construed\nrespectively as the 1st to the 15th and the 16th to the last day of the\nmonth, all dates inclusive.\n\n 37\n\fThe terms \"beginning\", \"middle\" and \"end\" of a month shall be\nconstrued respectively as the 1st to the 10th, the 11th to the 20th and\nthe 21st to the last day of the month, all dates inclusive.\n\nArticle 4: Credits v. Contracts\n(a) A credit, by its nature, is a separate transaction from the sale\n or other contract on which it may be based. Banks are in no\n way concerned with or bound by such contract, even if any\n reference whatsoever to it is included in the credit.\n Consequently, the undertaking of a b"},{"id":959,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"if any\n reference whatsoever to it is included in the credit.\n Consequently, the undertaking of a bank to honour, to\n negotiate or to fulfil any other obligation under the credit is not\n subject to claims or defences by the applicant resulting from its\n relationships with the issuing bank or the beneficiary. A\n beneficiary can, in no case, avail itself of the contractual\n relationships existing between banks or between the applicant\n and the issuing bank.\n(b) An issuing bank should discourage any attempt by the applicant\n to include, as an integral part of the credit, copies of the\n underlying contract, pro forma invoice and the like.\n\nArticle 5: Documents v. Goods, Services or Performance\nBanks deal with documents and not with goods, services or\nperformance to which the documents may relate.\n\nArticle 6: Availability, Expiry Date and Place for Presentation\n(a) A credit must state the ba"},{"id":960,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ate.\n\nArticle 6: Availability, Expiry Date and Place for Presentation\n(a) A credit must state the bank with whom it is available. In a\n freely available credit, any bank is a nominated bank. If a credit\n does not state with whom it is available, it is only deemed to\n be available with the issuing bank. A credit available with a\n nominated bank is also available with the issuing bank.\n(b) A credit must state whether it is available by sight payment,\n deferred payment, acceptance or negotiation.\n(c) A credit must not be made available by a draft drawn on the\n applicant.\n\n 38\n\f(d) A credit must state an expiry date for presentation. An expiry\n date stated for honour or negotiation will be deemed to be an\n expiry date for presentation.\n(e) Except as provided in sub-article 29 (a), a presentation by or\n on behalf of the beneficiary must be made on or before the\n expiry date.\n\nArticle 7: Issui"},{"id":961,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"on by or\n on behalf of the beneficiary must be made on or before the\n expiry date.\n\nArticle 7: Issuing Bank Undertaking\n(a) Provided that the stipulated documents are presented to the\n nominated bank or to the issuing bank, and that the terms and\n conditions of the credit are complied with, the issuing bank\n must honour:\n\n i. If the credit is available by sight payment, deferred\n payment or acceptance with the issuing bank;\n ii. If the credit is available by sight payment with a\n nominated bank and that nominated bank does not pay;\n iii. Deferred payment with a nominated bank and that\n nominated bank does not incur its deferred payment\n undertaking or, having incurred its deferred payment\n undertaking, does not pay at maturity;\n iv. Acceptance with a nominated bank and that nominated\n bank does not accept a draft drawn on it or, having\n accepted a draft drawn on it, does not pay at matur"},{"id":962,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"does not accept a draft drawn on it or, having\n accepted a draft drawn on it, does not pay at maturity;\n v. Negotiation with a nominated bank and that nominated\n bank does not negotiate;\n\n(b) An issuing bank is irrevocably bound to honour as of the time\n it issues the credit.\n\n(c) An issuing bank undertakes to reimburse a nominated bank\n that has honoured or negotiated a complying presentation and\n forwarded the documents to the issuing bank. Reimbursement\n for the amount of a complying presentation under a credit\n available by acceptance or deferred payment is due at maturity,\n whether or not the nominated bank prepaid or purchased\n\n 39\n\f before maturity. An issuing bank's undertaking to reimburse a\n nominated bank is independent of the issuing bank’s\n undertaking to the beneficiary.\n\nArticle 8: Confirming Bank Undertaking\n(a) Provided that the stipulated documents are presented to the"},{"id":963,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ticle 8: Confirming Bank Undertaking\n(a) Provided that the stipulated documents are presented to the\n confirming bank or to any other nominated bank, and that the\n terms and conditions of the credit are complied with, the\n confirming bank must:\n i. Honour, if the credit is available by sight payment,\n deferred payment or acceptance with the confirming\n bank;\n ii. Have sight payment with another nominated bank and\n that nominated bank does not pay;\n iii. Have deferred payment with another nominated bank\n and that nominated bank does not incur its deferred\n payment undertaking or, having incurred its deferred\n payment undertaking, does not pay at maturity;\n iv. Have acceptance with another nominated bank and that\n nominated bank does not accept a draft drawn on it or,\n having accepted a draft drawn on it, does not pay at\n maturity; or negotiate,\n v. If the credit is available by negotiatio"},{"id":964,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"t drawn on it, does not pay at\n maturity; or negotiate,\n v. If the credit is available by negotiation with the\n confirming bank, or negotiation with another nominated\n bank and that nominated bank does not negotiate.\n vi. Negotiate, without recourse, if the credit is available by\n negotiation with the confirming bank.\n(b) A confirming bank is irrevocably bound to honour or negotiate\n as of the time it adds its confirmation to the credit.\n(c) A confirming bank undertakes to reimburse another nominated\n bank that has honoured or negotiated a complying\n presentation and forwarded the documents to the confirming\n bank. Reimbursement for the amount of a complying\n presentation under a credit available by acceptance or deferred\n payment is due at maturity, whether or not another nominated\n bank prepaid or purchased before maturity. A confirming\n\n 40\n\f bank's undertaking to reimburse another no"},{"id":965,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"prepaid or purchased before maturity. A confirming\n\n 40\n\f bank's undertaking to reimburse another nominated bank is\n independent of the confirming bank’s undertaking to the\n beneficiary.\n(d) If a bank is authorised or requested by the issuing bank to\n confirm a credit but is not prepared to do so, it must inform the\n issuing bank without delay and may advise the credit without\n confirmation.\n\nArticle 9: Advising of Credits and Amendments\n(a) A credit and any amendment may be advised to a beneficiary\n through an advising bank. An advising bank that is not a\n confirming bank advises the credit and any amendment without\n any undertaking to honour or negotiate.\n(b) By advising the credit or amendment, the advising bank\n signifies that it has satisfied itself as to the apparent\n authenticity of the credit or amendment, and that the advice\n accurately reflects the terms and conditions of the c"},{"id":966,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"the credit or amendment, and that the advice\n accurately reflects the terms and conditions of the credit or\n amendment received.\n(c) An advising bank may utilise the services of another bank\n (“second advising bank”) to advise the credit and any\n amendment to the beneficiary. By advising the credit or\n amendment, the second advising bank signifies that it has\n satisfied itself as to the apparent authenticity of the advice it\n has received and that the advice accurately reflects the terms\n and conditions of the credit or amendment received.\n(d) A bank utilising the services of an advising bank or second\n advising bank to advise a credit must use the same bank to\n advise any amendment thereto.\n(e) If a bank is requested to advise a credit or amendment but\n elects not to do so, it must so inform, without delay, the bank\n from which the credit, amendment or advice has been received.\n(f) If"},{"id":967,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"form, without delay, the bank\n from which the credit, amendment or advice has been received.\n(f) If a bank is requested to advise a credit or amendment but\n cannot satisfy itself as to the apparent authenticity of the\n credit, the amendment or the advice, it must so inform, without\n delay, the bank from which the instructions appear to have\n been received. If the advising bank or second advising bank\n\n 41\n\f elects nonetheless to advise the credit or amendment, it must\n inform the beneficiary or second advising bank that it has not\n been able to satisfy itself as to the apparent authenticity of the\n credit, the amendment or the advice.\n\nArticle 10: Amendments\n(a) Except as otherwise provided by Article 38, a credit can neither\n be amended nor cancelled without the agreement of the\n issuing bank, the confirming bank, if any, and the beneficiary.\n(b) An issuing bank is irrevocably bound by"},{"id":968,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"bank, the confirming bank, if any, and the beneficiary.\n(b) An issuing bank is irrevocably bound by an amendment as of\n the time it issues the amendment. A confirming bank may\n extend its confirmation to an amendment and will be\n irrevocably bound as of the time it advises the amendment. A\n confirming bank may, however, choose to advise an\n amendment without extending its confirmation and, if so, it\n must inform the issuing bank without delay and also copy the\n beneficiary in its advice.\n(c) The terms and conditions of the original credit (or a credit\n incorporating previously accepted amendments) will remain in\n force for the beneficiary until the beneficiary communicates its\n acceptance of the amendment to the bank that advised such\n amendment. The beneficiary should give notification of\n acceptance or rejection of an amendment. If the beneficiary\n fails to give such notification, a pr"},{"id":969,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"acceptance or rejection of an amendment. If the beneficiary\n fails to give such notification, a presentation that complies with\n the credit and any not-yet-accepted amendment will be\n deemed to be a notification of acceptance by the beneficiary of\n such amendment. As of that moment, the credit will be\n amended.\n(d) A bank that advises an amendment should inform the bank\n from which it received the amendment of any notification of\n acceptance or rejection.\n(e) Partial acceptance of an amendment is not allowed and will be\n deemed to be a notification of rejection of the amendment.\n(f) If a time limit for the acceptance or rejection of an amendment\n is contained in the amendment or advice of amendment, the\n\n 42\n\f time limit shall be deemed not stated and should be\n disregarded.\n\nArticle 11:Teletransmitted and Pre-Advised Credits and\nAmendments\n(a) An authenticated teletransmission of a cr"},{"id":970,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"Teletransmitted and Pre-Advised Credits and\nAmendments\n(a) An authenticated teletransmission of a credit or amendment\n will be deemed to be the operative credit or amendment, and\n any subsequent mail confirmation shall be disregarded. If a\n teletransmission states \"full details to follow\" (or words of\n similar effect), or states that the mail confirmation is to be the\n operative credit or amendment, then, the teletransmission will\n not be deemed to be the operative credit or amendment. The\n issuing bank must then issue the operative credit or\n amendment without delay in terms not inconsistent with the\n teletransmission.\n(b) A preliminary advice of the issuance of a credit or amendment\n (“pre-advice”) shall only be sent if the issuing bank is prepared\n to issue the operative credit or amendment. An issuing bank\n that sends a pre-advice is irrevocably committed to issue the\n operative cred"},{"id":971,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ment. An issuing bank\n that sends a pre-advice is irrevocably committed to issue the\n operative credit or amendment, without delay, in terms not\n inconsistent with the pre-advice.\n\nArticle 12: Nomination\n(a) Unless a nominated bank is the confirming bank, an\n authorisation to honour or negotiate does not impose any\n obligation on that nominated bank to honour or negotiate,\n except when expressly agreed to by that nominated bank and\n so communicated to the beneficiary.\n\n 43\n\f(b) By nominating a bank to accept a draft or incur a deferred\n payment undertaking, an issuing bank authorises that\n nominated bank to prepay or purchase a draft accepted or a\n deferred payment undertaking incurred by that nominated\n bank.\n(c) Receipt or examination and forwarding of documents by a\n nominated bank that is not a confirming bank does not make\n that nominated bank liable to honour or negotiate, nor does"},{"id":972,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"is not a confirming bank does not make\n that nominated bank liable to honour or negotiate, nor does it\n constitute honour or negotiation.\n\nArticle 13: Bank-to-Bank Reimbursement Arrangements\n(a) If a credit states that reimbursement is to be obtained by a\n nominated bank (\"claiming bank\") claiming on another party\n (\"reimbursing bank\"), the credit must state if the\n reimbursement is subject to the ICC rules for bank-to-bank\n reimbursements in effect on the date of issuance of the credit.\n\n(b) If a credit does not state that reimbursement is subject to the\n ICC rules for bank-to-bank reimbursements, the following shall\n apply:\n i. An issuing bank must provide a reimbursing bank with a\n reimbursement authorisation that conforms to the\n availability stated in the credit. The reimbursement\n authorisation should not be subject to an expiry date.\n ii. A claiming bank shall not be required to"},{"id":973,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"uthorisation should not be subject to an expiry date.\n ii. A claiming bank shall not be required to supply a\n reimbursing bank with a certificate of compliance with\n the terms and conditions of the credit.\n iii. An issuing bank will be responsible for any loss of\n interest, together with any expenses incurred, if\n reimbursement is not provided on first demand by a\n reimbursing bank in accordance with the terms and\n conditions of the credit.\n iv. A reimbursing bank's charges are for the account of the\n issuing bank. However, if the charges are for the\n account of the beneficiary, it is the responsibility of an\n issuing bank to so indicate in the credit and in the\n\n 44\n\f reimbursement authorisation. If a reimbursing bank's\n charges are for the account of the beneficiary, they shall\n be deducted from the amount due to a claiming bank\n when reimbursement is made. If no reimbursement is\n made"},{"id":974,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ted from the amount due to a claiming bank\n when reimbursement is made. If no reimbursement is\n made, the reimbursing bank's charges remain the\n obligation of the issuing bank.\n\n(c) An issuing bank is not relieved of any of its obligations to\n provide reimbursement if reimbursement is not made by a\n reimbursing bank on first demand.\n\nArticle 14: Standard for Examination of Documents\n(a) A nominated bank, acting on its nomination, a confirming bank,\n if any, and the issuing bank must examine a presentation to\n determine, on the basis of the documents alone, whether or\n not the documents appear on their face value to constitute a\n complying presentation.\n(b) A nominated bank, acting on its nomination, a confirming bank,\n if any, and the issuing bank shall each have a maximum of five\n banking days following the day of presentation to determine if\n a presentation is complying. This period is"},{"id":975,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"days following the day of presentation to determine if\n a presentation is complying. This period is not curtailed or\n otherwise affected by the occurrence on or after the date of\n presentation of any expiry date or last day for presentation.\n(c) A presentation, including one or more original transport\n documents subject to Articles 19, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24 or 25, must\n be made by or on behalf of the beneficiary not later than 21\n calendar days after the date of shipment as described in these\n rules; but, in any event, not later than the expiry date of the\n credit.\n(d) Data in a document, when read in context with the credit, the\n document itself and international standard banking practice,\n need not be identical to, but must not conflict with, data in that\n document, any other stipulated document or the credit.\n(e) In documents other than the commercial invoice, the\n description of the go"},{"id":976,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"cument or the credit.\n(e) In documents other than the commercial invoice, the\n description of the goods, services or performance, if stated,\n\n 45\n\f may be in general terms not conflicting with their description\n in the credit.\n(f) If a credit requires presentation of a document other than a\n transport document, insurance document or commercial\n invoice, without stipulating by whom the document is to be\n issued or its data content, banks will accept the document as\n presented if its content appears to fulfil the function of the\n required document and otherwise complies with sub-article\n 14(d).\n(g) A document presented but not required by the credit will be\n disregarded and may be returned to the presenter.\n(h) If a credit contains a condition without stipulating the\n document to indicate compliance with the condition, banks will\n deem such condition as not stated and will disregard it.\n(i"},{"id":977,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"mpliance with the condition, banks will\n deem such condition as not stated and will disregard it.\n(i) A document may be dated prior to the issuance date of the\n credit, but must not be dated later than its date of presentation.\n(j) When the addresses of the beneficiary and the applicant\n appear in any stipulated document, they need not be the same\n as those stated in the credit or in any other stipulated\n document, but must be within the same country as the\n respective addresses mentioned in the credit. Contact details\n (telefax, telephone, email and the like) stated as part of the\n beneficiary’s and the applicant’s address will be disregarded.\n However, when the address and contact details of the applicant\n appear as part of the consignee or notify party details on a\n transport document subject to Articles 19, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24\n or 25, they must be as stated in the credit.\n(k) The ship"},{"id":978,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ubject to Articles 19, 20, 21, 22, 23, 24\n or 25, they must be as stated in the credit.\n(k) The shipper or consignor of the goods indicated on any\n document need not be the beneficiary of the credit.\n(l) A transport document may be issued by any party other than a\n carrier, owner, master or charterer, provided that the transport\n document meets the requirements of Articles 19, 20, 21, 22,\n 23 or 24 of these rules.\n\nArticle 15: Complying Presentation\n\n 46\n\f(a) When an issuing bank determines that a presentation is\n complying, it must honour.\n(b) When a confirming bank determines that a presentation is\n complying, it must honour or negotiate and forward the\n documents to the issuing bank.\n(c) When a nominated bank determines that a presentation is\n complying and honours or negotiates, it must forward the\n documents to the confirming bank or issuing bank.\n\nArticle 16: Discrepant Documents,"},{"id":979,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"t forward the\n documents to the confirming bank or issuing bank.\n\nArticle 16: Discrepant Documents, Waiver and Notice\n(a) When a nominated bank, acting on its nomination, a confirming\n bank, if any, or the issuing bank determines that a presentation\n does not comply, it may refuse to honour or negotiate.\n(b) When an issuing bank determines that a presentation does not\n comply, it may in its sole judgement approach the applicant for\n a waiver of the discrepancies. This does not, however, extend\n the period mentioned in sub-article 14 (b).\n(c) When a nominated bank, acting on its nomination, a confirming\n bank, if any, or the issuing bank decides to refuse to honour or\n negotiate, it must give a single notice to that effect to the\n presenter.\n The notice must state:\n i. that the bank is refusing to honour or negotiate; and\n ii. each discrepancy in respect of which the bank refuses\n to hono"},{"id":980,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"sing to honour or negotiate; and\n ii. each discrepancy in respect of which the bank refuses\n to honour or negotiate; and\n iii. (a) that the bank is holding the documents pending\n further instructions from the presenter; or\n (b) that the issuing bank is holding the documents\n until it receives a waiver from the applicant and\n agrees to accept it, or receives further\n instructions from the presenter prior to agreeing\n to accept a waiver; or\n (c) that the bank is returning the documents; or\n (d) that the bank is acting in accordance with\n instructions previously received from the\n presenter.\n\n 47\n\f(d) The notice required in sub-article 16 (c) must be given by\n telecommunication or, if that is not possible, by other\n expeditious means not later than the close of the fifth banking\n day following the day of presentation.\n(e) A nominated bank, acting on its nomination, a confirming bank,\n if an"},{"id":981,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"g the day of presentation.\n(e) A nominated bank, acting on its nomination, a confirming bank,\n if any, or the issuing bank may, after providing notice required\n by sub-article 16 (c) (iii) (a) or(b), return the documents to the\n presenter at any time.\n(f) If an issuing bank or a confirming bank fails to act in\n accordance with the provisions of this article, it shall be\n precluded from claiming that the documents do not constitute\n a complying presentation.\n(g) When an issuing bank refuses to honour or a confirming bank\n refuses to honour or negotiate, and has given notice to that\n effect in accordance with this article, it shall then be entitled to\n claim a refund, with interest, of any reimbursement made.\n\nArticle 17: Original Documents and Copies\n(a) At least one original of each document stipulated in the credit\n must be presented.\n(b) A bank shall treat, as an original, any document"},{"id":982,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"nt stipulated in the credit\n must be presented.\n(b) A bank shall treat, as an original, any document bearing an\n apparently original signature, mark, stamp, or label of the\n issuer of the document; unless the document itself indicates\n that it is not an original.\n(c) Unless a document indicates otherwise, a bank will also accept\n a document as original if it:\n (i) Appears to be written, typed, perforated or stamped by\n the document issuer’s hand; or\n (ii) Appears to be on the document issuer’s original\n stationery; or\n (iii) States that it is original, unless the statement appears\n not to apply to the document presented.\n(d) If a credit requires presentation of copies of documents,\n presentation of either originals or copies is permitted.\n(e) If a credit requires presentation of multiple documents by using\n terms such as \"in duplicate\", \"in twofold\" or \"in two copies\", this\n\n 48\n\f will b"},{"id":983,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ocuments by using\n terms such as \"in duplicate\", \"in twofold\" or \"in two copies\", this\n\n 48\n\f will be satisfied by the presentation of at least one original and\n the remaining number in copies, except when the document\n itself indicates otherwise.\n\nArticle 18: Commercial Invoice\n(a) A commercial invoice:\n i. Must appear to have been issued by the beneficiary\n (except as provided in Article 38);\n ii. Must be made out in the name of the applicant (except\n as provided in sub-article 38 (g));\n iii. Must be made out in the same currency as the credit;\n and\n iv. Need not be signed.\n(b) A nominated bank, acting on its nomination, a confirming bank,\n if any, or the issuing bank may accept a commercial invoice\n issued for an amount in excess of the amount permitted by the\n credit, and its decision will be binding upon all parties, provided\n the bank in question has not honoured or negotiated for"},{"id":984,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"will be binding upon all parties, provided\n the bank in question has not honoured or negotiated for an\n amount in excess of that permitted by the credit.\n(c) The description of the goods, services or performance in a\n commercial invoice must correspond with that appearing in the\n credit.\n\nArticle 19: Transport Document Covering at Least Two\nDifferent Modes of Transport\n(a) A transport document covering at least two different modes of\n transport (multimodal or combined transport document),\n however named, must appear to:\n i. Indicate the name of the carrier and be signed by:\n The carrier or a named agent for or on behalf of the\n carrier, or\n The master, or a named agent, for or on behalf of the\n master.\n Any signature by the carrier, master or agent must be\n identified as that of the carrier, master or agent.\n\n 49\n\f Any signature by an agent must indicate whether the\n agent has signed for"},{"id":985,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"r, master or agent.\n\n 49\n\f Any signature by an agent must indicate whether the\n agent has signed for or on behalf of the carrier or for or\n on behalf of the master.\n\nii. Indicate that the goods have been dispatched, taken in\n charge or shipped on board at the place stated in the\n credit, by:\n Pre-printed wording, or a stamp or notation, indicating\n the date on which the goods have been dispatched,\n taken in charge or shipped on board.\n The date of issuance of the transport document will be\n deemed to be the date of dispatch, taking in charge or\n shipped on board, and the date of shipment. However,\n if the transport document indicates, by stamp or\n notation, a date of dispatch, taking in charge or shipped\n on board, this date will be deemed to be the date of\n shipment.\niii. Indicate the place of dispatch, taking in charge or\n shipment and the place of final destination stated in the\n cred"},{"id":986,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ce of dispatch, taking in charge or\n shipment and the place of final destination stated in the\n credit, even if:\n a. The transport document states, in addition, a\n different place of dispatch, taking in charge or\n shipment or place of final destination, or\n b. The transport document contains the indication\n \"intended\" or similar qualification in relation to\n the vessel, port of loading or port of discharge.\niv. Be the sole original transport document or, if issued in\n more than one original, be the full set as indicated on\n the transport document.\nv. Contain terms and conditions of carriage or make\n reference to another source containing the terms and\n conditions of carriage (short form or blank back\n transport document). Contents of terms and conditions\n of carriage will not be examined.\nvi. Contain no indication that it is subject to a charter party.\n\n 50\n\f(b) For the purpose of this a"},{"id":987,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"vi. Contain no indication that it is subject to a charter party.\n\n 50\n\f(b) For the purpose of this article, transshipment means unloading\n from one means of conveyance and reloading to another\n means of conveyance (whether or not in different modes of\n transport) during the carriage from the place of dispatch,\n taking in charge or shipment to the place of final destination\n stated in the credit.\n(c) i. A transport document may indicate that the goods will or\n may be transshipped, provided that the entire carriage is\n covered by one and the same transport document.\n ii. A transport document indicating that transshipment will\n or may take place is acceptable, even if the credit\n prohibits transshipment.\n\nArticle 20: Bill of Lading\n(a) A bill of lading, however named, must appear to:\n i. Indicate the name of the carrier and be signed by:\n\n The carrier or a named agent for or on behalf of th"},{"id":988,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"cate the name of the carrier and be signed by:\n\n The carrier or a named agent for or on behalf of the\n carrier, or\n The master or a named agent, for or on behalf of the\n master.\n Any signature by the carrier, master or agent must be\n identified as that of the carrier, master or agent.\n Any signature by an agent must indicate whether the\n agent has signed for or on behalf of the carrier or for or\n on behalf of the master.\n\n ii. Indicate that the goods have been shipped on board a\n named vessel at the port of loading stated in the credit\n by:\n Pre-printed wording or an on-board notation indicating\n the date on which the goods have been shipped on\n board.\n The date of issuance of the bill of lading will be deemed\n to be the date of shipment unless the bill of lading\n contains an on-board notation indicating the date of\n\n 51\n\f shipment, in which case, the date stated in the on-board\n notatio"},{"id":989,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"tion indicating the date of\n\n 51\n\f shipment, in which case, the date stated in the on-board\n notation will be deemed to be the date of shipment.\n If the bill of lading contains the indication \"intended\n vessel\" or similar qualification in relation to the name of\n the vessel, an on-board notation indicating the date of\n shipment and the name of the actual vessel is required.\n\n ii. Indicate shipment from the port of loading to the port of\n discharge stated in the credit.\n If the bill of lading does not indicate the port of loading\n stated in the credit as the port of loading, or if it contains\n the indication “intended” or similar qualification in\n relation to the port of loading, an on- board notation\n indicating the port of loading as stated in the credit, the\n date of shipment and the name of the vessel are\n required. This provision applies even when loading on\n board or shipment on a n"},{"id":990,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"e of the vessel are\n required. This provision applies even when loading on\n board or shipment on a named vessel is indicated by\n pre-printed wording on the bill of lading.\n\n iv. Be the sole original bill of lading or, if issued in more\n than one original, be the full set as indicated on the bill\n of lading.\n v. Contain terms and conditions of carriage or make\n reference to another source containing the terms and\n conditions of carriage (short form or blank back bill of\n lading). Contents of terms and conditions of carriage will\n not be examined.\n vi. Contain no indication that it is subject to a charter party.\n(b) For the purpose of this article, transshipment means unloading\n from one vessel and reloading to another vessel during the\n carriage from the port of loading to the port of discharge stated\n in the credit.\n(c) i. A bill of lading may indicate that the goods will or may\n be tran"},{"id":991,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"arge stated\n in the credit.\n(c) i. A bill of lading may indicate that the goods will or may\n be transhipped, provided that the entire carriage is\n covered by one and the same bill of lading.\n\n 52\n\f ii. A bill of lading indicating that transshipment will or may\n take place is acceptable, even if the credit prohibits\n transshipment, if the goods have been shipped in a\n container, trailer or LASH barge as evidenced by the bill\n of lading.\n(d) Clauses in a bill of lading stating that the carrier reserves the\n right to transship will be disregarded.\n\nArticle 21: Non-Negotiable Sea Waybill\n(a) A non-negotiable sea waybill, however named, must appear to:\n i. Indicate the name of the carrier and be signed by:\n The carrier or a named agent for or on behalf of the\n carrier, or\n The master or a named agent, for or on behalf of the\n master.\n Any signature by the carrier, master or agent must be\n ide"},{"id":992,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"agent, for or on behalf of the\n master.\n Any signature by the carrier, master or agent must be\n identified as that of the carrier, master or agent.\n Any signature by an agent must indicate whether the\n agent has signed for or on behalf of the carrier or for or\n on behalf of the master.\n ii. Indicate that the goods have been shipped on board a\n named vessel at the port of loading stated in the credit\n by:\n Pre-printed wording, or\n A non-board notation indicating the date on which the\n goods have been shipped on board.\n The date of issuance of the non-negotiable sea waybill\n will be deemed to be the date of shipment unless the\n non-negotiable sea waybill contains an on-board\n notation indicating the date of shipment, in which case\n the date stated in the on-board notation will be deemed\n to be the date of shipment.\n If the non-negotiable sea waybill contains the indication\n \"intended vess"},{"id":993,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"be the date of shipment.\n If the non-negotiable sea waybill contains the indication\n \"intended vessel\" or similar qualification in relation to\n the name of the vessel, an on-board notation indicating\n\n 53\n\f the date of shipment and the name of the actual vessel\n is required.\n iii. Indicate shipment from the port of loading to the port\n of discharge stated in the credit. If the non-negotiable\n sea waybill does not indicate the port of loading stated\n in the credit as the port of loading, or if it contains the\n indication “intended” or similar qualification in relation\n to the port of loading, an on-board notation indicating\n the port of loading as stated in the credit, the date of\n shipment and the name of the vessel is required. This\n provision applies even when loading on board or\n shipment on a named vessel is indicated by pre-printed\n wording on the non-negotiable sea waybill.\n iv. B"},{"id":994,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ent on a named vessel is indicated by pre-printed\n wording on the non-negotiable sea waybill.\n iv. Be the sole original non-negotiable sea waybill or, if\n issued in more than one original, be the full set as\n indicated on the non-negotiable sea waybill.\n v. Contain terms and conditions of carriage or make\n reference to another source containing the terms and\n conditions of carriage (short form or blank back non-\n negotiable sea waybill). Contents of terms and\n conditions of carriage will not be examined.\n vi. Contain no indication that it is subject to a charter party.\n(b) For the purpose of this article, transshipment means unloading\n from one vessel and reloading to another vessel during the\n carriage from the port of loading to the port of discharge stated\n in the credit.\n(c) i. A non-negotiable sea waybill may indicate that the\n goods will or may be transshipped, provided that the\n e"},{"id":995,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"egotiable sea waybill may indicate that the\n goods will or may be transshipped, provided that the\n entire carriage is covered by one and the same non-\n negotiable sea waybill.\n ii. A non-negotiable sea waybill indicating that trans-\n shipment will or may take place is acceptable, even if\n the credit prohibits transshipment, if the goods have\n been shipped in a container, trailer or LASH barge as\n evidenced by the non-negotiable sea waybill.\n\n 54\n\f(d) Clauses in a non-negotiable sea waybill stating that the carrier\n reserves the right to transship will be disregarded.\n\nArticle 22: Charter Party Bill of Lading\n(a) A bill of lading, however named, containing an indication that\n it is subject to a charter party (charter party bill of lading), must\n appear to:\n i. be signed by:\n The master or a named agent for or on behalf of the\n master, or\n The owner or a named agent for or on behalf of the"},{"id":996,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"named agent for or on behalf of the\n master, or\n The owner or a named agent for or on behalf of the\n owner, or\n The charterer or a named agent for or on behalf of the\n charterer.\n Any signature by the master, owner, charterer or agent\n must be identified as that of the master, owner,\n charterer or agent.\n Any signature by an agent must indicate whether the\n agent has signed for or on behalf of the master, owner\n or charterer.\n An agent signing for or on behalf of the owner or\n charterer must indicate the name of the owner or\n charterer.\n ii. Indicate that the goods have been shipped on board a\n named vessel at the port of loading stated in the credit\n by:\n Pre-printed wording, or an on-board notation indicating\n the date on which the goods have been shipped on\n board.\n The date of issuance of the charter party bill of lading\n will be deemed to be the date of shipment unless the\n charter"},{"id":997,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"e of the charter party bill of lading\n will be deemed to be the date of shipment unless the\n charter party bill of lading contains an on-board notation\n indicating the date of shipment, in which case the date\n stated in the on-board notation will be deemed to be the\n date of shipment.\n\n 55\n\f iii. Indicate shipment from the port of loading to the port\n of discharge stated in the credit. The port of discharge\n may also be shown as a range of ports or a geographical\n area, as stated in the credit.\n iv. Be the sole original charter party bill of lading or, if\n issued in more than one original, be the full set as\n indicated on the charter party bill of lading.\n(b) A bank will not examine charter party contracts, even if they\n are required to be presented by the terms of the credit.\n\nArticle 23: Air Transport Document\n(a) An air transport document, however named, must appear to:\n i. Indicate t"},{"id":998,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"Air Transport Document\n(a) An air transport document, however named, must appear to:\n i. Indicate the name of the carrier and be signed by:\n The carrier or a named agent for or on behalf of the\n carrier.\n Any signature by the carrier or agent must be identified\n as that of the carrier or agent.\n Any signature by an agent must indicate that the agent\n has signed for or on behalf of the carrier.\n ii. Indicate that the goods have been accepted for carriage.\n iii. Indicate the date of issuance. This date will be deemed\n to be the date of shipment unless the air transport\n document contains a specific notation of the actual date\n of shipment, in which case the date stated in the\n notation will be deemed to be the date of shipment.\n Any other information appearing on the air transport\n document relative to the flight number and date will not\n be considered in determining the date of shipment."},{"id":999,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"relative to the flight number and date will not\n be considered in determining the date of shipment.\n iv. Indicate the airport of departure and the airport of\n destination stated in the credit.\n v. Be the original for the consignor or shipper, even if the\n credit stipulates a full set of originals.\n vi. Contain terms and conditions of carriage or make\n reference to another source containing the terms and\n conditions of carriage. Contents of terms and conditions\n of carriage will not be examined.\n\n 56\n\f(b) For the purpose of this article, transshipment means unloading\n from one aircraft and reloading to another aircraft during the\n carriage from the airport of departure to the airport of\n destination stated in the credit.\n(c) i. An air transport document may indicate that the goods\n will or may be transshipped, provided that the entire\n carriage is covered by one and the same air transpor"},{"id":1000,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"may be transshipped, provided that the entire\n carriage is covered by one and the same air transport\n document.\n ii. An air transport document indicating that transshipment\n will or may take place is acceptable, even if the credit\n prohibits transshipment.\n\nArticle 24: Road, Rail or Inland Waterway Transport\nDocuments\n(a) A road, rail or inland waterway transport document, however\n named, must appear to:\n i. Indicate the name of the carrier and:\n Be signed by the carrier or a named agent for or on\n behalf of the carrier,\n Or indicate receipt of the goods by signature, stamp or\n notation by the carrier or a named agent for or on behalf\n of the carrier.\n Any signature, stamp or notation of receipt of the goods\n by the carrier or\n Agent must be identified as that of the carrier or agent.\n Signature, stamp or notation of receipt of the goods by\n the agent must indicate that the agent has si"},{"id":1001,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"gnature, stamp or notation of receipt of the goods by\n the agent must indicate that the agent has signed or\n acted for or on behalf of the carrier.\n A rail transport document does not identify the carrier,\n any or stamp of the railway company will be accepted\n as evidence of the document being signed by the carrier.\n ii. Indicate the date of shipment or the date the goods\n have been received for shipment, dispatch or carriage\n at the place stated in the credit. Unless the transport\n document contains a dated reception stamp, an\n indication of the date of receipt or a date of shipment,\n\n 57\n\f the date of issuance of the transport document will be\n deemed to be the date of shipment.\n iii. Indicate the place of shipment and the place of\n destination stated in the credit.\n(b) i. A road transport document must appear to be the\n original for the consignor or shipper, or bear no marking\n indica"},{"id":1002,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"rt document must appear to be the\n original for the consignor or shipper, or bear no marking\n indicating for whom the document has been prepared.\n ii. A rail transport document marked “duplicate” will be\n accepted as an original.\n iii. A rail or inland waterway transport document will be\n accepted as an original, whether marked as an original\n or not.\n(c) In the absence of an indication on the transport document as\n to the number of originals issued, the number presented will\n be deemed to constitute a full set.\n(d) For the purpose of this article, transshipment means unloading\n from one means of conveyance and reloading to another\n means of conveyance, within the same mode of transport,\n during the carriage from the place of shipment, dispatch or\n carriage to the place of destination stated in the credit.\n(e) i. A road, rail or inland waterway transport document may\n indicate that the g"},{"id":1003,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ed in the credit.\n(e) i. A road, rail or inland waterway transport document may\n indicate that the goods will or may be transshipped,\n provided that the entire carriage is covered by one and\n the same transport document.\n ii. A road, rail or inland waterway transport document\n indicating that transshipment will or may take place if\n acceptable, even if the credit prohibits transshipment.\n\n 58\n\fArticle 25: Courier Receipt, Post Receipt or Certificate of\nPosting\n(a) A courier receipt, however named, evidencing receipt of goods\n for transport, must appear to:\n i. Indicate the name of the courier service and be stamped\n or signed by the named courier service at the place from\n which the credit states the goods are to be shipped; and\n ii. Indicate a date of pick-up or of receipt or wording to this\n effect. This date will be deemed to be the date of\n shipment.\n\n(b) A requirement that courier c"},{"id":1004,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"his\n effect. This date will be deemed to be the date of\n shipment.\n\n(b) A requirement that courier charges are to be paid or prepaid\n may be satisfied by a transport document issued by a courier\n service evidencing that courier charges are for the account of\n a party other than the consignee.\n(c) A post receipt or certificate of posting, however named,\n evidencing receipt of goods for transport, must appear to be\n stamped or signed and dated at the place from which the credit\n states the goods are to be shipped. This date will be deemed\n to be the date of shipment.\n\nArticle 26: \"On Deck\", \"Shipper's Load and Count\", “Said by\nShipper to Contain” and “Charges Additional to Freight”\n(a) A transport document must not indicate that the goods are or\n will be loaded on deck. A clause on a transport document\n stating that the goods may be loaded on deck is acceptable.\n(b) A transport document be"},{"id":1005,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"rt document\n stating that the goods may be loaded on deck is acceptable.\n(b) A transport document bearing a clause such as \"shipper's load\n and count\" and \"said by shipper to contain\" is acceptable.\n(c) A transport document may bear a reference, by stamp or\n otherwise, to charge additional to the freight.\n\nArticle 27: Clean Transport Document\nA bank will only accept a clean transport document. A clean transport\ndocument is one bearing no clause or notation expressly declaring a\ndefective condition of the goods or their packaging. The word “clean”\n\n 59\n\fneed not appear on a transport document, even if a credit has a\nrequirement for that transport document to be “clean on-board”.\n\nArticle 28: Insurance Document and Coverage\n(a) An insurance document, such as an insurance policy, an\n insurance certificate or a declaration under an open cover,\n must appear to be issued and signed by an insur"},{"id":1006,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"e certificate or a declaration under an open cover,\n must appear to be issued and signed by an insurance company,\n an underwriter, their agents or their proxies.\n Any signature by an agent or proxy must indicate whether the\n agent or proxy has signed for or on behalf of the insurance\n company or underwriter.\n(b) When the insurance document indicates that it has been issued\n in more than one original, all originals must be presented.\n(c) Cover notes will not be accepted.\n(d) An insurance policy is acceptable in lieu of an insurance\n certificate or a declaration under an open cover.\n(e) The date of the insurance document must not be later than the\n date of shipment, unless it appears from the insurance\n document that the cover is effective from a date not later than\n the date of shipment.\n(f) i. The insurance document must indicate the amount of\n insurance coverage and be in the same curre"},{"id":1007,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":") i. The insurance document must indicate the amount of\n insurance coverage and be in the same currency as the\n credit.\n ii. A requirement in the credit for insurance coverage to be\n for a percentage of the value of the goods, of the invoice\n value or similar, is deemed to be the minimum amount\n of coverage required.\n If there is no indication in the credit of the insurance\n coverage required, the amount of insurance coverage\n must be at least 110% of the CIF or CIP value of the\n goods.\n When the CIF or CIP value cannot be determined from\n the documents, the amount of insurance coverage must\n be calculated on the basis of the amount for which\n honour or negotiation is requested or the gross value of\n the goods as shown on the invoice, whichever is greater.\n\n 60\n\f iii. The insurance document must indicate that risks are\n covered at least between the place of taking in charge\n or shipment"},{"id":1008,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"t must indicate that risks are\n covered at least between the place of taking in charge\n or shipment and the place of discharge or final\n destination as stated in the credit.\n(g) A credit should state the type of insurance required and, if any,\n the additional risks to be covered. An insurance document will\n be accepted without regard to any risks that are not covered if\n the credit uses imprecise terms such as “usual risks” or\n “customary risks”.\n(h) When a credit requires insurance against “all risks” and an\n insurance document is presented containing any “all risks”\n notation or clause, whether or not bearing the heading “all\n risks”, the insurance document will be accepted without regard\n to any risks stated to be excluded.\n(i) An insurance document may contain reference to any exclusion\n clause.\n(j) An insurance document may indicate that the cover is subject\n to a franchise or exces"},{"id":1009,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"n\n clause.\n(j) An insurance document may indicate that the cover is subject\n to a franchise or excess (deductible).\n\nArticle 29: Extension of Expiry Date or Last Day for\nPresentation\n(a) If the expiry date of a credit or the last day for presentation\n falls on a day when the bank to which presentation is to be\n made is closed for reasons other than those referred to in\n Article 36, the expiry date or the last day for presentation, as\n the case may be, will be extended to the first following banking\n day.\n(b) If presentation is made on the first following banking day, a\n nominated bank must provide the issuing bank or confirming\n bank with a statement on its covering schedule that the\n presentation was made within the time limits extended in\n accordance with sub-article 29 (a).\n(c) The latest date for shipment will not be extended as a result of\n sub-article 29 (a).\n\n 61\n\fArticle 30: Tole"},{"id":1010,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"st date for shipment will not be extended as a result of\n sub-article 29 (a).\n\n 61\n\fArticle 30: Tolerance in Credit Amount, Quantity and Unit\nPrices\n(a) The words \"about\" or \"approximately\" used in connection with\n the amount of the credit, the quantity or the unit price stated\n in the credit, are to be construed as allowing a tolerance not to\n exceed 10% more or 10% less than the amount, the quantity\n or the unit price to which they refer.\n(b) A tolerance not to exceed 5% more or 5% less than the\n quantity of the goods is allowed, provided the credit does not\n state the quantity in terms of a stipulated number of packing\n units or individual items and the total amount of the drawings\n does not exceed the amount of the credit.\n(c) Even when partial shipments are not allowed, a tolerance not\n to exceed 5% less than the amount of the credit is allowed,\n provided that the quantity of the go"},{"id":1011,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"t\n to exceed 5% less than the amount of the credit is allowed,\n provided that the quantity of the goods, if stated in the credit,\n is shipped in full and a unit price, if stated in the credit, is not\n reduced or that sub-article 30 (b) is not applicable. This\n tolerance does not apply when the credit stipulates a specific\n tolerance or uses the expressions referred to in sub-article\n 30(a).\n\nArticle 31: Partial Drawings or Shipments\n(a) Partial drawings or shipments are allowed.\n(b) A presentation consisting of more than one set of transport\n documents evidencing shipment commencing on the same\n means of conveyance and for the same journey, provided they\n indicate the same destination, will not be regarded as covering\n a partial shipment, even if they indicate different dates of\n shipment or different ports of loading, places of taking in\n charge or dispatch. If the presentation consists"},{"id":1012,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"or different ports of loading, places of taking in\n charge or dispatch. If the presentation consists of more than\n one set of transport documents, the latest date of shipment as\n evidenced on any of the sets of transport documents will be\n regarded as the date of shipment.\n\n A presentation consisting of one or more sets of transport\n documents evidencing shipment on more than one means of\n\n 62\n\f conveyance within the same mode of transport will be regarded\n as covering a partial shipment, even if the means of\n conveyance leave on the same day for the same destination.\n(c) A presentation consisting of more than one courier receipt, post\n receipt or certificate of posting will not be regarded as a partial\n shipment if the courier receipts, post receipts or certificates of\n posting appear to have been stamped or signed by the same\n courier or postal service at the same place and date and fo"},{"id":1013,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"have been stamped or signed by the same\n courier or postal service at the same place and date and for the\n same destination.\n\nArticle 32: Instalmental Drawings or Shipments\nIf a drawing or shipment by instalments within given periods is\nstipulated in the credit and any instalment is not drawn or shipped\nwithin the period allowed for that instalment, the credit ceases to be\navailable for that and any subsequent instalment.\n\nArticle 33: Hours of Presentation\nA bank has no obligation to accept a presentation outside of its\nbanking hours.\n\nArticle 34: Disclaimer on Effectiveness of Documents\nA bank assumes no liability or responsibility for the form, sufficiency,\naccuracy, genuineness, falsification or legal effect of any document,\nor for the general or particular conditions stipulated in a document or\nsuperimposed thereon; nor does it assume any liability or\nresponsibility for the descripti"},{"id":1014,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"cument or\nsuperimposed thereon; nor does it assume any liability or\nresponsibility for the description, quantity, weight, quality, condition,\npacking, delivery, value or existence of the goods, services or other\nperformance represented by any document, or for the good faith or\nacts or omissions, solvency, performance or standing of the consignor,\nthe carrier, the forwarder, the consignee or the insurer of the goods\nor any other person.\n\n 63\n\fArticle 35: Disclaimer on Transmission and Translation\nA bank assumes no liability or responsibility for the consequences\narising out of delay, loss in transit, mutilation or other errors arising\nin the transmission of any messages or delivery of letters or\ndocuments, when such messages, letters or documents are\ntransmitted or sent according to the requirements stated in the credit,\nor when the bank may have taken the initiative in the choice of the"},{"id":1015,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"uirements stated in the credit,\nor when the bank may have taken the initiative in the choice of the\ndelivery service in the absence of such instructions in the credit.\n\nIf a nominated bank determines that a presentation is complying and\nforwards the documents to the issuing bank or confirming bank,\nwhether or not the nominated bank has honoured or negotiated, an\nissuing bank or confirming bank must honour or negotiate, or\nreimburse that nominated bank, even when the documents have been\nlost in transit between the nominated bank and the issuing bank or\nconfirming bank, or between the confirming bank and the issuing\nbank.\n\nA bank assumes no liability or responsibility for errors in translation or\ninterpretation of technical terms and may transmit credit terms\nwithout translating them.\n\nArticle 36: Force Majeure\nA bank assumes no liability or responsibility for the consequences\narising out"},{"id":1016,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"le 36: Force Majeure\nA bank assumes no liability or responsibility for the consequences\narising out of the interruption of its business by Acts of God, riots, civil\ncommotions, insurrections, wars, acts of terrorism, or by any strikes\nor lockouts or any other causes beyond its control.\nA bank will not, upon resumption of its business, honour or negotiate\nunder a credit that expired during such interruption of its business.\n\nArticle 37: Disclaimer for Acts of an Instructed Party\n(a) A bank utilising the services of another bank for the purpose of\n giving effect to the instructions of the applicant does so for the\n account and at the risk of the applicant.\n(b) An issuing bank or advising bank assumes no liability or\n responsibility should the instructions it transmits to another\n\n 64\n\f bank not be carried out, even if it has taken the initiative in the\n choice of that other bank.\n(c) A ban"},{"id":1017,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"not be carried out, even if it has taken the initiative in the\n choice of that other bank.\n(c) A bank instructing another bank to perform services is liable for\n any commissions, fees, costs or expenses (“charges”) incurred\n by that bank in connection with its instructions.\n If a credit states that charges are for the account of the\n beneficiary and charges cannot be collected or deducted from\n proceeds, the issuing bank remains liable for payment of\n charges.\n A credit or amendment should not stipulate that the advice to\n a beneficiary is conditional upon the receipt by the advising\n bank or second advising bank of its charges.\n(d) The applicant shall be bound by and liable to indemnify a bank\n against all obligations and responsibilities imposed by foreign\n laws and usages.\n\nArticle 38: Transferable Credits\n(a) A bank is under no obligation to transfer a credit except to the\n extent an"},{"id":1018,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"Transferable Credits\n(a) A bank is under no obligation to transfer a credit except to the\n extent and in the manner expressly consented to by that bank.\n(b) For the purpose of this article:\n Transferable credit means a credit that specifically states it is\n “transferable”. A transferable credit may be made available in\n whole or in part to another beneficiary (“second beneficiary”)\n at the request of the beneficiary (“first beneficiary”).\n Transferring bank means a nominated bank that transfers the\n credit or, in a credit available with any bank, a bank that is\n specifically authorised by the issuing bank to transfer and that\n transfers the credit. An issuing bank may be a transferring\n bank.\n Transferred credit means a credit that has been made available\n by the transferring bank to a second beneficiary.\n(c) Unless otherwise agreed at the time of transfer, all charges\n (such as commissi"},{"id":1019,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ond beneficiary.\n(c) Unless otherwise agreed at the time of transfer, all charges\n (such as commissions, fees, costs or expenses) incurred in\n respect of a transfer must be paid by the first beneficiary.\n(d) A credit may be transferred in part to more than one second\n beneficiary provided partial drawing or shipments are allowed.\n\n 65\n\f A transferred credit cannot be transferred at the request of a\n second beneficiary to any subsequent beneficiary. The first\n beneficiary is not considered to be a subsequent beneficiary.\n(e) Any request for transfer must indicate if and under what\n conditions amendments may be advised to the second\n beneficiary. The transferred credit must clearly indicate those\n conditions.\n(f) If a credit is transferred to more than one second beneficiary,\n rejection of an amendment by one or more second beneficiary\n does not invalidate the acceptance by any other secon"},{"id":1020,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"n amendment by one or more second beneficiary\n does not invalidate the acceptance by any other second\n beneficiary, with respect to which the transferred credit will be\n amended accordingly. For any second beneficiary that rejected\n the amendment, the transferred credit will remain unamended.\n(g) The transferred credit must accurately reflect the terms and\n conditions of the credit, including confirmation, if any, with the\n exception of:\n • The amount of the credit,\n • Any unit price stated therein,\n • The expiry date,\n • The period for presentation, or\n • The latest shipment date or given period for shipment,\n\n Any or all of which may be reduced or curtailed.\n The percentage for which insurance cover must be effected\n may be increased to provide the amount of cover stipulated in\n the credit or these articles.\n The name of the first beneficiary may be substituted for that of\n the applica"},{"id":1021,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"it or these articles.\n The name of the first beneficiary may be substituted for that of\n the applicant in the credit.\n If the name of the applicant is specifically required by the credit\n to appear in any document other than the invoice, such\n requirement must be reflected in the transferred credit.\n(h) The first beneficiary has the right to substitute its own invoice\n and draft, if any, for those of a second beneficiary for an\n amount not in excess of that stipulated in the credit; and, upon\n such substitution, the first beneficiary can draw under the\n\n 66\n\f credit for the difference, if any, between its invoice and the\n invoice of a second beneficiary.\n(i) If the first beneficiary is to present its own invoice and draft, if\n any, but fails to do so on first demand, or if the invoices\n presented by the first beneficiary create discrepancies that did\n not exist in the presentation made b"},{"id":1022,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"esented by the first beneficiary create discrepancies that did\n not exist in the presentation made by the second beneficiary\n and the first beneficiary fails to correct them on first demand,\n the transferring bank has the right to present the documents\n as received from the second beneficiary to the issuing bank,\n without further responsibility to the first beneficiary.\n(j) The first beneficiary may, in its request for transfer, indicate\n that honour or negotiation is to be effected to a second\n beneficiary at the place to which the credit has been\n transferred, up to and including the expiry date of the credit.\n This is without prejudice to the right of the first beneficiary in\n accordance with sub-article 38 (h).\n(k) Presentation of documents by or on behalf of a second\n beneficiary must be made to the transferring bank.\n\nArticle 39: Assignment of Proceeds\nThe fact that a credit is not"},{"id":1023,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"be made to the transferring bank.\n\nArticle 39: Assignment of Proceeds\nThe fact that a credit is not stated to be transferable shall not affect\nthe right of the beneficiary to assign any proceeds to which it may be\nor may become entitled under the credit, in accordance with the\nprovisions of applicable law. This article applies only to the assignment\nof proceeds and not to the assignment of the right to perform under\nthe credit.\n\n 67\n\f REVISION QUESTIONS AND ANSWERS\n\n Essay Questions\n\nQuestion 1\nThe following have arisen in respect of a confirmed letter of credit\ntransaction.\nRequired:\nExamine each case independently and resolve the issues accordingly\na. At the request of the importer, the issuing bank initiated an\n amendment to the letter of credit by sending a mail to the confirming\n bank. The confirming bank merely advised the amendment to the\n beneficiary but opted not to add its con"},{"id":1024,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"k. The confirming bank merely advised the amendment to the\n beneficiary but opted not to add its confirmation to the amendment.\n State, with reasons, whether or not the amendment was valid.\n\nb. The quantity specified in the credit was 50,000 tons. However,\n documents presented for negotiation showed 46,000 tons. Justify\n your acceptance or rejection of the documents in your capacity as a\n presenting bank.\nc. The letter of credit amount was US$198,000 but the documents\n presented showed an invoice amount of US$231,840. Justify your\n acceptance or rejection of the documents in your capacity as a\n presenting bank.\nd. Among the documents presented for negotiation by the seller was a\n document marked “Bill of Lading’. That document did not resemble\n the type of bill of lading you are used to, as the usual full details of\n contract of carriage were absent, but rather made reference to\n another"},{"id":1025,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"s the usual full details of\n contract of carriage were absent, but rather made reference to\n another document where the full terms and conditions of carriage\n could be read and examined.\n i. Between the issuing bank and the confirming bank, who is\n under obligation to examine the additional document referred\n to in order to ascertain the terms and conditions of carriage?\n ii. As the negotiating bank, would you accept the documents for\n the purpose of negotiation or insist on replacement with the\n usual bill of lading?\n\n 68\n\fAnswer\na - The amendment was valid.\n - A confirming bank may choose to advise on amendment\n without extending its confirmation (Article 10 UCP).\n\nb. Reject document.\n - Variation in quantity was 8%.\n - Only 5% variation in quantity is allowed.\nc. Accept documents.\n 10% variation in price allowed (Article 30 UCP).\nd. None of the banks is under obligation to examine the"},{"id":1026,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"variation in price allowed (Article 30 UCP).\nd. None of the banks is under obligation to examine the documents\n - Short form bill of lading is acceptable.\n - Banks will not examine the terms and conditions of carriage\n beyond seeing that the bill of lading is written in purpose to be (Article\n 20 UCP).\nii. Accept the document.\n - The type of bill of lading is called short form.\n - Short form bill of lading is acceptable.\n - No need to invest on any replacement.\n\nQuestion 2\nSome years back, the Nigerian Government, through the Central Bank of\nNigeria (CBN), abolished the use of Bills for Collection and Open Account\nmethods of payment for settlement under International Trade.\nRequired:\n(a) As a Nigerian importer, what method of payment is acceptable to the\n Nigerian Government?\n(b) What are the advantages and disadvantages of the method referred\n to above?\n\nAnswer\na. Documentary Letters of"},{"id":1027,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"the advantages and disadvantages of the method referred\n to above?\n\nAnswer\na. Documentary Letters of Credit.\nb. Advantages and Disadvantages of confirmed irrevocable letter of\n credit\n\n 69\n\f Advantages Disadvantages\n 1 The Importer can obtain Banks deal with documents and not goods.\n help and expert advice The issuing bank is committed to effecting\n from his bankers as the payment where the exporter complies with\n issuing bank. the terms and conditions of the credit,\n regardless of the condition of the goods.\n 2 The exporter will only be It is more expensive to the importer.\n paid upon presentation of\n specified documents in\n accordance with the\n terms and conditions of\n the letter of credit\n 3 The importer may still It is a bit complex and requires strict\n secure a period of credit guidance from the bank.\n under the letter of credit.\n 4 The importer can insist on Where the letter of cre"},{"id":1028,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ce from the bank.\n under the letter of credit.\n 4 The importer can insist on Where the letter of credit demands for\n shipment being made at immediate payment (sight bill), the\n a specified period of time. importer may suffer cash flow squeeze.\n 5 The importer attracts The importer’s borrowing limit with the\n more confidence from the bank may be affected, especially where he\n exporter. needs finance from the bank to make\n payment under the letter of credit.\n\nQuestion 3\nYour customer, BestWears and Company, imports wool and mohair from the\nFar East and South America. Purchases have usually been on account basis,\nbut one of your customer's merchant suppliers now needs assistance to\nfinance his purchases.\n\nYou have been asked to support your customer by arranging one instrument,\nwhich will satisfy the merchant's requirements and allow series of shipments\nand drawings during the forthcoming i"},{"id":1029,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"isfy the merchant's requirements and allow series of shipments\nand drawings during the forthcoming importing season.\n\n 70\n\fRequired:\nWrite brief notes on:\n(a) The basic instrument (and how it operates) which would assist the\n supplier and satisfy your customer's needs.\n(b) Any disadvantages in using the instrument in (a) above which the\n customer must consider.\n(c) Your explanation of the international rules and practices which are\n relevant to the basic instrument you have defined in (a) above.\n Discuss the protection afforded to the buyer and the seller, in respect\n of drawings relating to partial shipments.\n\nAnswer\n(a) The basic instrument that would assist the supplier and satisfy the\n customer's needs is an Irrevocable Red Clause letter of credit. This is\n a type of credit incorporating a clause, traditionally in red, authorising\n the advising/confirming bank to advance to the expor"},{"id":1030,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ng a clause, traditionally in red, authorising\n the advising/confirming bank to advance to the exporter a specified\n percentage of the credit value even before shipment and presentation\n of stipulated documents. A red clause letter of credit can be\n clean/unconditional or documentary/secured/conditional. It is\n unconditional when the exporter is merely expected to give a written\n statement that any fund obtained will be utilised for the purpose\n meant. It is conditional when the exporter is expected to give a written\n pledge that the stipulated documents of title will be presented, and in\n addition shows evidence of title to the related goods before he obtains\n funds.\n\n Relying on the issuing bank's undertaking, the advising/confirming\n bank will give facilities, whether by way of an overdraft, advance or\n otherwise to the exporter. The issuing bank is bound to reimburse the\n advising/co"},{"id":1031,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"raft, advance or\n otherwise to the exporter. The issuing bank is bound to reimburse the\n advising/confirming bank, even though the exporter defaults.\n Depending on the agreement of the parties, the advance may be made\n clean or as discussed above. Furthermore, based on the special nature\n of the credit, normal and necessary documents such as title\n documents, transport documents, financial documents and the third\n party documents would be called for.\n\n 71\n\f(b) The advances/facilities extended to the exporter are to pay tax,\n deductible from the proceeds of sale which is to accrue to his\n presentation of the stipulated documents. Failure of the exporter to\n make shipment and thus present documents for payment will mean\n his inability to repay the loan. The advising/confirming bank must be\n reimbursed by the issuing bank and the latter will definitely pass over\n this liability to the custo"},{"id":1032,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"reimbursed by the issuing bank and the latter will definitely pass over\n this liability to the customer in form of debit into his account.\n Furthermore, if the advance/facility has been made unsecured, the\n risks become outrageous and may be worse than the situation is an\n open account trading.\n\n(c) The international rules relevant to the basic instrument identified\n under (c) above is the Uniform Custom and Practice for Documenter\n Credit ICC Publication No. 600. The UCP contains series of rates\n guiding the issuance and operation of documentary letters of credit,\n thereby making attempts to standardise terms and conditions under\n which credits are issued.\n\n Article 31 of the UCP covers partial drawings as well as partial\n shipment. The Article provides that partial drawing and/or partial\n shipments are allowed unless the credit stipulates otherwise. The\n Article goes further to say tha"},{"id":1033,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"shipments are allowed unless the credit stipulates otherwise. The\n Article goes further to say that where shipments are made at different\n times, at different ports on the same vessel and on the same voyage,\n such shipment will be regarded as a single shipment, even though a\n number of bills of lading are presented showing shipment on board\n on different dates. This provision enables exporters to enjoy cheaper\n freight charges associated with such shipments. Another protection is\n that partial drawings will only be granted in a case where partial\n shipments are allowed and made.\n\nQuestion 4\nMr. Goriola, the Import Manager of one of your valued customers, called at\nyour office today and complained about the features of the document\nreceived from your bank under the confirmed irrevocable documentary\nletters of credit opened in favour of their foreign suppliers.\n\n 72\n\fThe document compris"},{"id":1034,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ocumentary\nletters of credit opened in favour of their foreign suppliers.\n\n 72\n\fThe document comprised copies made by carbonised facsimile and other\nreprographic means EXCEPT for the Bill of Lading. He also observed that\nsome of the documents were not signed by the foreign exporter and\ntherefore not properly drawn in accordance with the terms and conditions of\nthe documentary letters of credit.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Justify your actions in accepting these documents with reference to\n the relevant provisions of UCP 600.\n(b) What protection, if any, is available to the bank?\n(c) What would be the bank’s position if some of these documents were\n dated before the due date on the letters of credit?\n\nAnswer\na.\n- The bank’s position is justified in view of the provision of Article 17 of\n UCP 600.\n- Unless otherwise stipulated in the credit, banks will also accept as\n original documents produced or app"},{"id":1035,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ss otherwise stipulated in the credit, banks will also accept as\n original documents produced or appearing to have been produced by\n reprographics, automated or computerised system- Article 17.\n- Banks will accept such documents where they are produced as such\n as carbon copies- Article 17(b)i.\n- Unless otherwise stipulated in the credit, banks will accept as copies,\n (a) document(s) either labelled copy or not marked as original- a copy\n need not be signed. Article 17 (c).\n- Credits that require multiple documents such as duplicate, two-fold,\n two copies, except where the document itself indicates otherwise.\n Article 17(e).\n- Unless otherwise stipulated in the credit, a condition under a credit\n calling for a document to be authenticated, validated, and legalised,\n by a signature, mark, stamp, or label on such document that, on its\n face value, appears to satisfy the above conditions."},{"id":1036,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"tamp, or label on such document that, on its\n face value, appears to satisfy the above conditions.\n\n 73\n\f(b)\n- Banks enjoy the provisions of the Uniform Customs and Practice\n published by the ICC, the current edition being No. 600, unless there\n exists any contrary condition to the credit.\n- Documents appearing on their face value to have been issued by\n another party are acceptable in international trade, provided they are\n issued in consonance with UCP 600 Article 38.\n\nc.\n- The bank’s position as regards date is covered by the provisions of\n Article 29.\n- Unless otherwise stipulated in the credit, banks will accept a document\n bearing a date of issuance prior to that of the credit, subject to such\n document being presented within the time limit set on the credit.\n- The case will be different if the credit disallows the practice and the\n parties agree to this.\n\nQuestion 5\nHosanna Oils P"},{"id":1037,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"rent if the credit disallows the practice and the\n parties agree to this.\n\nQuestion 5\nHosanna Oils Plc is your major customer and major player in the export\nmarket, mainly in red palm and vegetable oils. Your bank has advised and\nconfirmed an irrevocable documentary letter of credit on behalf of the issuing\nbank. The following information relates to the documentary letter of credit:\n\nIssuing Bank - Commerce and Credit Bank, Ontario, Canada.\nAccreditor - Millennium Foods Limited.\nBeneficiary - Hosanna Oils Plc.\nType & Value - Documentary letters of credit available by negotiation\n valued at US$ 1.5m quarterly.\nConsignment - 250 metric tons of red palm oil to be shipped in 20x20\n tankers before August 20; part-shipment not allowed.\n L/C expiry date September 16.\n\nHosanna Oil Plc. wrote a letter to you on September 6, intimating you that\nthey had not acted on their copy of the credit advice"},{"id":1038,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ter to you on September 6, intimating you that\nthey had not acted on their copy of the credit advice, which was sent to you\nbecause the accreditor merely informed them by facsimile message and that\nthey were awaiting the original advice copy through courier. When you cross-\n\n 74\n\fchecked the file, you discovered the credit advice was sent: the same advice\nto you.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Briefly define revolving documentary letters of credit as to time.\n(b) Explain to Hosanna Oils Plc., with reference to the relevant\n provisions of UCP 600, the meaning of documentary letters of credit\n available by negotiation.\n(c) State the relevant provision of the UCP 600 on Teletransmitted and\n Pre-Advised Credits so as to educate Hosanna Oils Plc on their\n complaints.\n\nAnswer\n(a) Revolving documentary letters of credit as to time is when the original\n amount of a credit is reinstalled after the expiration of"},{"id":1039,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"f credit as to time is when the original\n amount of a credit is reinstalled after the expiration of any given\n period of time, which could be monthly, quarterly or annually. The\n arrangement is used where a contract could extend over a fairly long\n period of time and is of a repetitive nature. It removes the\n cumbersome effects of establishing new documentary credit each\n time a transaction occurs.\n\n(b) According to Article 2(a) of UCP 600, all credits must clearly indicate\n whether they are available by sight payment, by deferred payment,\n by acceptance or by negotiation. Negotiation, according to Article 2 of\n UCP 600, means the giving of value of Draft(s) and/or document(s)\n but the bank is authorised to negotiate. Mere examination of\n documents without giving of value does not constitute a negotiation.\n\n(c) Teletransmitted and Pre-Advice Credits Article 11 (a-c)\n (i) When an issuing"},{"id":1040,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"te a negotiation.\n\n(c) Teletransmitted and Pre-Advice Credits Article 11 (a-c)\n (i) When an issuing bank instructs an advising bank by an\n authenticated Teletransmission to advise a credit or an\n amendment to a credit, the Teletransmission will be deemed to\n be an operative credit instrument or the operative amendment,\n and no mail confirmation should be sent. Should a mail\n confirmation nevertheless be sent, it will have no effect and\n the advising bank will have no obligation to check such mail\n\n 75\n\f confirmation against the operative credit instrument or the\n operative amendment received by Teletransmission. Article 11\n (a).\n (ii) If the Teletransmission states \"full details to follow\" (or words\n of similar effect) or states that the mail confirmation is to be\n the operative credit instrument or the operative amendment,\n then the Teletransmission will not be deemed to be the\n operati"},{"id":1041,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"trument or the operative amendment,\n then the Teletransmission will not be deemed to be the\n operative credit instrument or the operative amendment. The\n issuing bank must forward the operative credit instrument or\n the operative amendment to such advising bank without delay.\n Article 1 l(a) ii.\n\n A preliminary advice of the issuance or amendment of an\n irrevocable credit shall only be given by an issuing bank if such\n bank is prepared to issue the operative credit instrument or the\n operative amendment thereof. Unless otherwise stated in such\n preliminary advice, the issuing bank, having given such pre-\n advice, shall be irrevocably committed to issuing or amending\n the credit, in terms not consistent with the pre-advice without\n delay. Article 11.\n\nQuestion 6\n(a) An irrevocable credit constitutes a \"definite undertaking of the issuing\n bank, provided that the stipulated documents are p"},{"id":1042,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"nstitutes a \"definite undertaking of the issuing\n bank, provided that the stipulated documents are presented to the\n nominated bank or to the issuing bank,\" and that the terms and\n conditions of the credit are complied with. What are the terms and\n conditions validating the liability of the issuing and confirming banks,\n under the Uniform Customs and Practice as regards:\n (i) Payment? (Give 2 points)\n (ii) Acceptance? (Give 2 points)\n (iii) Negotiation? (Give 2 points)\n (iv) Confirmation? (Give 2 points]\n (v) Reimbursement? (Give 2 points]\n\n(b) (i) What is a revolving documentary letter of credit as to time?\n\n 76\n\f (ii) State the conditions under which a revocable credit will\n become irrevocable.\n\nAnswer\n(a) Article 8 states the liability of the issuing and confirming banks as\n follows:\n (i) Payment\n - If the credit calls for sight payment, to pay at sight.\n - If the credit calls for dif"},{"id":1043,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"i) Payment\n - If the credit calls for sight payment, to pay at sight.\n - If the credit calls for differed payment, to pay at\n maturity.\n (ii) Acceptance\n If the credit provides for acceptance: by the confirming\n bank, to accept at maturity draft(s) drawn by the\n beneficiary on the confirming bank and pay them at\n maturity. Or\n\n By another drawee bank, to accept and pay at maturity\n draft[s] drawn by the beneficiary on the confirming bank,\n in the event the drawee bank stipulated in the credit does\n not accept draft(s) accepted but not paid by such drawee\n bank at maturity.\n (iii) Negotiation\n If the credit provides for negotiation, to pay without recourse\n to drawer and/or bona fide holders, drafts drawn by the\n beneficiary and/or documents presented under the credit.\n (iv) Confirmation\n A confirmation of an irrevocable credit by another bank (the\n confirming bank], upon the authorisatio"},{"id":1044,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"confirmation of an irrevocable credit by another bank (the\n confirming bank], upon the authorisation or request of the\n issuing bank, constitutes a definite undertaking of the\n confirming bank, in addition to that of the issuing bank,\n provided that the stipulated documents are presented to the\n confirming bank or to any other nominated bank, and that the\n terms and conditions on the credit are complied with.\n\n 77\n\f - If the credit provides for sight payment, to pay at sight.\n - If the credit provides for deferred payment, to pay on\n the maturity date(s); the determination is in accordance\n with the stipulation of the credit.\n - If the credit provides for acceptance by the confirming\n bank, to accept drafts drawn by the beneficiary on the\n confirming bank.\n - If the draft is drawn on another drawee bank, to accept\n and pay at maturity, drafts drawn by the beneficiary on\n the confirming"},{"id":1045,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"her drawee bank, to accept\n and pay at maturity, drafts drawn by the beneficiary on\n the confirming bank on the terms the drawee bank\n stipulated in the credit does not accept, draft(s]\n accepted but not paid by such.\n - If the credit provides for negotiation, to negotiate\n without recourse to the drawer(s) and/or legitimate\n holders, drafts drawn by the beneficiary and/or\n documents presented under the credit.\n (v) Reimbursement\n If another bank is authorised or requested by the issuing bank\n to add its confirmation to a credit but is not prepared to do so,\n it must inform the issuing bank without delay unless the issuing\n bank specifies otherwise in its authorisation or request; to add\n confirmation, the advising bank may advise the creditor to the\n beneficiary without confirmation.\n If the confirming bank, it may or may not extend its\n confirmation to any amendment and must inform the"},{"id":1046,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"the confirming bank, it may or may not extend its\n confirmation to any amendment and must inform the issuing\n bank without delay.\n\n(b) (i) A revolving documentary letter of credit as to time is a type of\n credit authorising the beneficiary to ship goods of a specified\n amount, quantity and standard as may have been agreed or\n the credit as evidenced by the relative exchange control\n documents, to the accreditor on a regular basis, usually\n monthly, quarterly or any other stipulated time.\n (ii) A revocable credit is that whose terms and conditions can be\n amended or altered by any of the parties, particularly the\n accreditor, without the prior consent of others. A revocable\n\n 78\n\f credit becomes irrevocable where any of the parties,\n particularly the beneficiary or the confirming/advising banker,\n has already incurred a cost on it, in which case the accreditor\n would be made to pay for su"},{"id":1047,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"anker,\n has already incurred a cost on it, in which case the accreditor\n would be made to pay for such up to the time of cancellation\n or amendment.\n\n MULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTIONS (MCQs)\n\n1. A letter of credit employed where the identity of the original supplier\n of goods cannot be disclosed is known as:\n (a) Standby Letter of Credit.\n (b) Transferable Letter of Credit.\n (c) Transit Letter of Credit.\n (d) Back-to-Back Letter of Credit.\n\n2. All EXCEPT one of the following types of letters of credit are not\n comparable to a bank guarantee:\n (a) Revolving Credit\n (b) Back-to-Back Credit\n (c) Standby Credit\n (d) Confirmed Credit\n\n3. A letter of credit that allows the seller to receive up to 25% of the\n documentary credit amount as an advance payment before the\n fulfilment of all the conditions stated in the documentary credit is\n known as:\n\n (a) Green Clause Credit.\n (b) Yellow Clause Credit."},{"id":1048,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ated in the documentary credit is\n known as:\n\n (a) Green Clause Credit.\n (b) Yellow Clause Credit.\n (c) Purple Clause Credit.\n (d) Red Clause Credit.\n\n4. One of the following is the main objective of Letter of Credit (LC):\n (a) To reduce exchange risk.\n (b) To reduce credit risk.\n\n 79\n\f(c) To reduce financial risk.\n(d) To reduce economic risk\n\n 80\n\f5. In an LC transaction, which bank is authorised to honour the payment\n claim in settlement of negotiation/acceptance/payment lodged with it\n by the negotiating bank?\n (a) Reimbursing Bank\n (b) Opening Bank\n (c) Advising Bank\n (d) Foreign Bank\n\n6. A letter of credit which contains a clause authorising the nominated\n bank to make advances to the seller against security before\n shipment/presentation of documents is known as:\n a) Purple clause letter of credit.\n b) Yellow clause letter of credit.\n c) Green clause letter of credit.\n d) Red clause"},{"id":1049,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ter of credit.\n b) Yellow clause letter of credit.\n c) Green clause letter of credit.\n d) Red clause letter of credit.\n\n7. All EXCEPT one of the following are the factors that may force a\n beneficiary to terminate a letter of credit:\n (a) The unit prices may have increased too much since the opening\n of the letter of credit.\n (b) The beneficiary may find the terms and conditions of letter of\n credit not doable.\n (c) Beneficiary may not be able to find the required finance to\n perform under the letter of credit.\n (d) The advising bank may not be willing to advise the credit.\n\n8. A confirming bank is under obligation to add its confirmation to a letter\n of credit. True/False?\n\n9. Letter of credit is a conditional payment undertaking of the issuing\n bank. The condition is known as:\n (a) Obligatory presentation.\n (b) Complying presentation.\n (c) Advising presentation.\n (d) Reimbursing presen"},{"id":1050,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"atory presentation.\n (b) Complying presentation.\n (c) Advising presentation.\n (d) Reimbursing presentation.\n\n 81\n\f10. A revolving letter of credit where a fixed amount is replenished every\n time just after it is utilised by the beneficiary within the overall validity\n of the letter of credit is known as:\n (a) Non-Cumulative Revolving Letter of Credit.\n (b) Revolution Based on Value.\n (c) Cumulative Revolving Letter of Credit.\n (d) Revolving Cumulative Letter of Credit.\n\nAnswers\n(1) d\n(2) c\n(3) Red clause letter of credit\n(4) b\n(5) a\n(6) c\n(7) d\n(8) False\n(9) b\n(10) b\n\n 82\n\f REFERENCES\n\nCharles, W.L. (2005). International Business: Competing in the Global\n Marketplace. The McGraw Hill Companies Inc., USA.\n\nClark, E. (2002). International Finance, Thomson, High Holborn House,\n London.\n\nOdekunle, T. (2015). Concepts and Theories of International Finance,\n Jaytees Publishers.\n\nPaul, R.R. (20"},{"id":1051,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"nle, T. (2015). Concepts and Theories of International Finance,\n Jaytees Publishers.\n\nPaul, R.R. (2004). Money, Banking and International Trade. Kalyani\n Publishers, New Delhi.\n\nPugel, U. (2015). International Economics. McGraw Education,(16th Edition).\n\n 83\n\f Methods and Terms of\n Payments\n\nLearning Outcomes:\nAt the end of this Chapter, the reader should be able to understand the\nfollowing methods of payments in international trade and their features:\n• Open Account;\n• Payment in Advance;\n• International Collections; and\n• Bank Payment Obligations.\n\n2.1 INTRODUCTION\n Methods of payment refer to mediums through which international\n trade transactions are effected since the importer wants safe delivery\n while the exporter wants safe and efficient means of payment. Both\n the importer and exporter need to agree on the means by which their\n transactions will be effected. To succeed in today’"},{"id":1052,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"orter need to agree on the means by which their\n transactions will be effected. To succeed in today’s global marketplace\n and win sales against foreign competitors, exporters must offer their\n customers attractive sales terms supported by appropriate payment\n methods. Because getting paid in full and on time is the ultimate goal\n for each export sale, an appropriate payment method must be chosen\n carefully to minimise the payment risk while also accommodating the\n needs of the buyer. There are different methods of payment for\n international transactions, but the underlisted are the major ones:\n ▪ Open account;\n ▪ Advance payment;\n ▪ Documentary letters of credit;\n ▪ Documentary collection; and\n ▪ Bank payment obligations.\n\fThe order of attractiveness The order of attractiveness\nfrom the exporter’s point of from the importer’s point of\nview is: view is:\n1. Payment in advance 1. Open accou"},{"id":1053,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"porter’s point of from the importer’s point of\nview is: view is:\n1. Payment in advance 1. Open account\n2. Documentary credit 2. Documentary collection\n3. Documentary collection 3. Documentary credit\n4. Open account 4. Payment in advance\n\n2.2 OPEN ACCOUNT\n An open account transaction is a sale where the goods are shipped\n and delivered before payment is due, which is usually in 30-90 days.\n Obviously, this is the most advantageous option to the importer in\n terms of cash flow and cost; but it is consequently the highest risk\n option for an exporter. Because of intense competition in export\n markets, foreign buyers often press exporters for open account terms,\n since the extension credit by the seller to the buyer is more common\n abroad. Therefore, exporters who are reluctant to extend credit may\n lose sales to their competitors. However, the exporter can offer\n competitive open account te"},{"id":1054,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"t may\n lose sales to their competitors. However, the exporter can offer\n competitive open account terms while substantially mitigating the risk\n of non-payment by using one or more of the appropriate trade finance\n techniques, such as export credit insurance.\n\n Advantages to the Importer\n (a) It is the safest.\n (b) It increases turnover.\n (c) It increases cash flow position.\n\n Disadvantages to the Importer\n (a) It is prone to exposure to the risk of foreign exchange\n fluctuation.\n (b) Liabilities could be too much if the trade is not well planned.\n (c) Substandard goods may be shipped.\n\n 85\n\fMethods and Terms of Payments\n\n Advantages to the Disadvantages to the Exporter\n Exporter\n 1. It increases market 1. Buyer’s default can cripple his\n network. business.\n 2. Profit margin can be 2. Not suitable where both parties\n increased if properly and are located in unfriendly nations.\n carefully"},{"id":1055,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"suitable where both parties\n increased if properly and are located in unfriendly nations.\n carefully planned\n 3. It is less cumbersome. 3. Risk of exchange rates fluctuation\n could be higher.\n\n2.3 ADVANCE PAYMENT\n Advance payment, which is also known as cash in advance, is a\n payment method in international trade in which an order is not\n processed until full payment is received by the supplier in advance.\n Sometimes cash in advance is called cash with order.\n\n Advance payment poses the highest risk to the importer, the lowest\n risk to the exporter. One of the main characteristics of advance\n payment is that the full order amount will be paid by the importer to\n the exporter prior to the transfer of ownership of the goods.\n\n It is used mostly for goods made to custom, or where it is a seller's\n market. In most cases, exporters demand significant advance\n payments on order confirmation so"},{"id":1056,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"er's\n market. In most cases, exporters demand significant advance\n payments on order confirmation so as to protect themselves against\n order cancellations. Some common phrases stipulated in sales\n contracts to this effect are as follows:\n • 50% of pro forma invoice total will be paid on order\n confirmation; remaining 50% will be paid 1 week before\n shipment.\n • 30% of pro forma invoice total will be paid 1 week after order\n confirmation; remaining 70% will be paid against copy of\n shipment documents sent via e-mail or fax.\n\n Cash in advance payment is the most secure payment method in\n international trade for exporters, because it eliminates non-payment\n risk, especially when money transfer is done via wire transfer.\n\n 86\n\fBy paying total value of the goods before shipment, importers have\nto bear some risks such as:\n• Non-shipment risks;\n• The risk of low-quality goods;\n• Non-compliance"},{"id":1057,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"to bear some risks such as:\n• Non-shipment risks;\n• The risk of low-quality goods;\n• Non-compliance risks; and\n• Custom clearance risks.\n\nFor this reason, exporters and importers have to identify cash-in-\nadvance payment details on the pro forma invoice in order to prevent\nmisunderstandings and possible mistakes.\n\nPayment Settlement Methods for Advance Payment\nWire Transfer: Wire transfer is the most secure and preferred cash-\nadvance payment settlement method used in international trade\ntransactions.\n\nWire transfer can either be sent via online banking or traditional\nbanking applications. In order to complete an international wire\ntransfer correctly, the following details must be well specified:\n1. Currency of the wire transfer and wire transfer total.\n2. Name of the account holder.\n3. Full Name and address of the bank.\n4. Bank Branch.\n5. Account number.\n6. Swift Code: SWIFT is the shor"},{"id":1058,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ll Name and address of the bank.\n4. Bank Branch.\n5. Account number.\n6. Swift Code: SWIFT is the short form of “Society for Worldwide\n Interbank Financial Telecommunication”. SWIFT is an industry-\n owned co-operative supplying secure, standardised messaging\n services and interface software to nearly 8,100 financial\n institutions in 207 countries and territories. SWIFT members\n include banks, broker-dealers and investment managers. While\n not always required, a SWIFT Code (also known as BIC Code)\n may be required by some banks for the completion of wire\n transfers.\n7. IBAN Number: (if applicable) The International Bank Account\n Number (IBAN) is the international standard for identifying\n international bank accounts across national borders. The IBAN\n\n 87\n\fMethods and Terms of Payments\n\n is comprised of a maximum of 27 alphanumeric characters\n within Europe and a maximum of 34 outside of Eur"},{"id":1059,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"mprised of a maximum of 27 alphanumeric characters\n within Europe and a maximum of 34 outside of Europe (German\n IBAN: 22 characters). At present, the United States does not\n participate in IBAN. Therefore, US banks do not have an IBAN\n number.\n\n Credit Card: For small-scale orders, credit cards could be used as a\n viable cash-in-advance payment method in international trade.\n As international credit card transactions are typically placed via online,\n telephone, or fax methods that facilitate fraudulent transactions,\n proper precautions should be taken to determine the validity of\n transactions before the goods are shipped.\n Although exporters must endure the fees charged by credit card\n companies, this option may help the business grow because of its\n convenience.\n\n Advantages to the Exporter\n (a) It is the safest.\n (b) It is suitable where parties are located in an unfriendly\n environm"},{"id":1060,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"rter\n (a) It is the safest.\n (b) It is suitable where parties are located in an unfriendly\n environment.\n (c) It reduces exchange rate fluctuation risk.\n\n Disadvantages to the Exporter\n (a) It may reduce turnover.\n (b) It may reduce profit.\n (c) It may erode goodwill.\n\n Advantages to the Importer Disadvantages to the\n Importer\n 1. It is not as expensive as 1. Not suitable for parties\n documentary letter of located in less friendly\n credit. nations.\n 2. Allows for proper planning. 2. The insolvency of the\n exporter could lead to bad\n debt.\n\n 88\n\f (See Chapter one For DOCUMENTARY LETTERS OF CREDIT)\n\n2.4 DOCUMENTARY COLLECTION\n Documentary Collection is an international trade procedure in which a\n bank in the importer’s country acts on behalf of an exporter for\n collecting and remitting payment for a shipment.\n\n The exporter presents the shipping and collection documents to his or\n her bank"},{"id":1061,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"nt for a shipment.\n\n The exporter presents the shipping and collection documents to his or\n her bank (in own country) which sends them to its correspondent bank\n in the importer’s country.\n\n The foreign bank (called the presenting bank) hands over shipping\n and title documents (required for taking delivery of the shipment) to\n the importer in exchange for cash payment (in case of ‘documents\n against payment‘ instructions) or a firm commitment to pay on a fixed\n date (in case of ‘documents against acceptance‘ instructions).\n\n A documentary collection (D/C) is a transaction whereby the exporter\n entrusts the collection of a payment to the remitting bank (exporter’s\n bank), which sends documents to a collecting bank (importer’s bank),\n along with instructions for payment.\n\n Funds are received from the importer and remitted to the exporter\n through the banks involved in the collection in exc"},{"id":1062,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"from the importer and remitted to the exporter\n through the banks involved in the collection in exchange for those\n documents.\n\n Advantages of a Documentary Collection for Exporters\n 1. Under documentary collections, the documents will not be\n handed over to the importers unless payment or acceptance of\n a time draft has been obtained. As importers could not clear\n goods from the customs without the documents, at least in\n theory, documentary collections give considerable payment\n warranty to the exporters.\n 2. Documentary collections are less complicated than the\n alternatives such as letters of credit.\n\n 89\n\fMethods and Terms of Payments\n\n 3. Documentary collections are less expensive than the\n alternatives such as letters of credit.\n 4. Exporters retain the title of goods (only with sea shipments\n under negotiable B/L) until payment has been received.\n\n Risks or Disadvantages for the"},{"id":1063,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"a shipments\n under negotiable B/L) until payment has been received.\n\n Risks or Disadvantages for the Exporters\n\n 1. Non-Acceptance of Documents’ Risk: This is one of the major\n risk factors in a documentary collection for exporters. Under\n current documentary collection rules, which are called URC\n 522, importers are not obligated to collect documents from\n their banks.\n\n 2. Non-Payment Risk: Non-payment risk is the second most\n frequently seen risk factor in documentary collections. In\n most cases, we would expect to see a non-payment risk in a\n documentary collection which is available by Documents\n against Acceptance (D/A).\n\n Non-payment risk could happen if the importer accepts a time\n draft but refuses to pay at maturity.\n\n 3. Risk of Delivery of Goods to the Importer without Original\n Shipment Documents: Importers may clear goods from the\n customs without having original shipment d"},{"id":1064,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"Shipment Documents: Importers may clear goods from the\n customs without having original shipment documents on road,\n air and rail transportation.\n\n It is also possible for buyers to receive goods from transport\n companies under sea shipments if express bills of lading (non-\n negotiable bills of lading) have been utilised.\n\n Under all these conditions as explained above, the importers\n will be able to receive the goods without the need of original\n shipment documents, which may lead to a non-payment for the\n exporters under documentary collections.\n\n 90\n\fAdvantages of a Documentary Collection for Importers\n1. Documentary collections are less expensive than the\n alternatives such as letters of credit.\n2. Documentary collections are less complicated than the\n alternatives such as letters of credit.\n3. Under documentary collections, generally, importers make the\n payment after the shipment"},{"id":1065,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"credit.\n3. Under documentary collections, generally, importers make the\n payment after the shipment of goods.\n4. Importers may be able to review copies of the shipment\n documents before payment.\n5. Importers may be able to make the payment after they\n received the documents from their banks by accepting usance\n bills of exchange (time drafts).\n\nRisks or Disadvantages of a Documentary Collection for\nImporters\n1. Low Quality Goods Shipment Risk: This is one of the major risk\n factors in a documentary collection for the importers. Under\n current documentary collection rules, which are called URC\n 522, banks do not check or control the documents.\n2. Importers may face the risk of exchange rate fluctuation,\n especially where it is documents against acceptance (D/A).\n3. Importers’ cash flow may be affected where it is documents\n against payments (D/P).\n4. Not suitable for new timers in intern"},{"id":1066,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"be affected where it is documents\n against payments (D/P).\n4. Not suitable for new timers in international trade.\n\nKey Characteristics\nImporters normally pay for the goods after the shipment in whole. But\nit is also possible to make a partial advance payment under\ndocumentary collections. For example, it is possible to pay 25% of the\ntransaction amount in advance while the rest 75% is payable via cash\nagainst documents (CAD).\n\nGenerally, there are two payment options available under\ndocumentary collections: Documents against Payment (D/P) and\nDocuments against Acceptance (D/A).\n\n 91\n\fMethods and Terms of Payments\n\n The title of goods belongs to the exporter until the exporter is paid by\n the importer. But the exporter must be very careful about land, rail\n and air shipments. The transport documents issued under these\n shipments do not represent the title to the goods.\n\n Although banks p"},{"id":1067,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"documents issued under these\n shipments do not represent the title to the goods.\n\n Although banks play an essential role in documentary collections, they\n neither check/verify the documents, nor take any risks.\n\n Documentary collections are less complicated than letters of credit.\n Also, documentary collections are less expensive compared to letters\n of credit.\n\n Collection Instruction and its Scope\n Collection Instruction is a separate document, which the exporter\n should supply to his bank along with other commercial and financial\n documents under a documentary collection.\n\n Collection instructions are written in a cover letter format and normally\n should contain the following points:\n ➢ Governing Rules for the Collection: As an example “This\n collection is subject to The Uniform Rules for Collections, 1995\n Revision, ICC Publication No. 522.”\n\n ➢ Details of the bank from which the co"},{"id":1068,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"for Collections, 1995\n Revision, ICC Publication No. 522.”\n\n ➢ Details of the bank from which the collection was received,\n including full name, postal and SWIFT addresses, telex,\n facsimile numbers and reference.\n\n ➢ Details of the principal, including full name, postal address,\n and, if applicable, telex, telephone and facsimile numbers.\n\n ➢ Details of the drawee, including full name, postal address, or\n the domicile at which presentation is to be made and, if\n applicable, telex, telephone and facsimile numbers.\n\n 92\n\f➢ Details of the presenting bank, if any, including full name,\n postal address, and, if applicable, telex, telephone and\n facsimile numbers.\n\n➢ Total amount and currency of the documentary collection.\n\nDocuments to be Supplied\nURC 522, the rules of the documentary collections, define two types\nof documents: financial documents and commercial documents.\n• Financial Documen"},{"id":1069,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ns, define two types\nof documents: financial documents and commercial documents.\n• Financial Documents: Financial documents mean bills of\n exchange, promissory notes, cheques, or other similar\n instruments used for obtaining the payment of money.\n• Commercial Documents: Commercial documents mean\n invoices, transport documents, documents of title or other\n similar documents, or any other documents whatsoever, not\n being financial documents.\n ➢ Documents against Payment (D/P): Documents may be\n released only if the importer makes immediate payment\n according to the contractual agreement between the\n exporter and the importer. Also known as sight\n collection.\n ➢ Documents against Acceptance (D/A): Documents may\n be released only if the importer accepts the\n accompanying draft, thereby incurring an obligation to\n pay at a specified future date. In this arrangement, the\n exporter is exposed t"},{"id":1070,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ng an obligation to\n pay at a specified future date. In this arrangement, the\n exporter is exposed to the credit risk of the importer and\n the political risk of the country. Also known as a term\n collection.\n ➢ Charges: Charges to be collected, indicating whether\n they may be waived or not and who is going to pay for\n charges in detail.\n\n ➢ Interest: Collection of interest is a very rare case in\n documentary collections but, if applicable, collection\n instruction should indicate whether it may be waived or\n not, with the following information:\n\n 93\n\fMethods and Terms of Payments\n\n a. rate of interest;\n b. interest period;\n c. basis of calculation (for example 360 or 365 days\n in a year), as applicable.\n\n • Method of payment and form of payment advice.\n\n • Instructions in case of non-payment, non-acceptance and/or\n non-compliance.\n\n Parties in a Documentary Collection\n The main parties in"},{"id":1071,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"t, non-acceptance and/or\n non-compliance.\n\n Parties in a Documentary Collection\n The main parties in a documentary collection and their roles and\n responsibilities are summarised below:\n\n • The “principal”, who is the party entrusting the handling of a\n collection to a bank;\n • The “remitting bank”, which is the bank to which the\n principal has entrusted the handling of a collection;\n • The “collecting bank”, which is any bank, other than the\n remitting bank, involved in processing the collection;\n • The “presenting bank”, which is the collecting bank making\n presentation to the drawee.\n • The “drawee”, which is the one to whom presentation is to\n be made according to the collection instruction.\n\n Documentary Collection Process (Cash against Document\n Transaction Flow)\n 1. The exporter and importer sign a sales contract indicating the\n payment method and shipment terms in addition to oth"},{"id":1072,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"importer sign a sales contract indicating the\n payment method and shipment terms in addition to other\n details.\n 2. The exporter makes the shipment.\n 3. The exporter submits the documents and collection instructions\n to its bank with instructions for delivery.\n 4. The exporter’s bank sends the documents to the importer’s\n bank together with the exporter’s instructions.\n\n 94\n\f5. The importer’s bank reaches out to the importer, informing him\n about the documentary collection.\n6. The importer fulfils his collection obligations and gets the\n documents from his bank. The importer’s bank hands the\n documents to the importer on payment or acceptance of a bill\n of exchange.\n7. The exporter receives payment.\n\nWorkability of Collections and Governing Laws URC 522\n\nRules\nDocumentary collections are governed by the Uniform Rules for\nCollections, 1995 Revision, ICC Publication No. 522, simply known"},{"id":1073,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"governed by the Uniform Rules for\nCollections, 1995 Revision, ICC Publication No. 522, simply known as\nURC 522.\n\nDocumentary collection rules, URC 522, published by International\nChamber of Commerce, ICC. URC 522 will only be effective if it is\nincorporated into the text of the “collection instruction“.\n\nUniform Rules for Collections (URC)\n• It is an internationally accepted code of practice covering\n documentary collections.\n• It is a publication of the International Chamber of Commerce\n (ICC), reviewed from time to time.\n• The latest is the Uniform Rules for Collections Publication No.\n 522 which became effective in January 1996.\n\nAll bank authorities will state that the collection is subject to URC.\nThe following rules apply in order of importance to collections:\n\ni. National laws of the parties to collection;\nii. Legal and enforceable agreement between parties; and\niii. URC.\n\n 95\n\fMe"},{"id":1074,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"e parties to collection;\nii. Legal and enforceable agreement between parties; and\niii. URC.\n\n 95\n\fMethods and Terms of Payments\n\n Summary of the Provisions of URC\n\n Article 1: Application of URC 522\n This states that banks do not have to deal with collections but must\n advise the party, from whom they receive the collection, of their\n decision.\n\n Article 2: Definition of Collection\n It defines and differentiates between various terms:\n i. Financial documents are the instruments used for the purpose\n of obtaining payment, e.g. cheques.\n ii. Commercial documents related to the goods themselves, e.g.\n invoice, waybills.\n iii. Clean collections consist of financial documents that are not\n accompanied by commercial documents.\n iv. Documentary collections are commercial documents that may\n or may not be accompanied by financial documents.\n\n Article 3: Parties to a Collection\n This identifies t"},{"id":1075,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"y not be accompanied by financial documents.\n\n Article 3: Parties to a Collection\n This identifies the main parties to a collection:\n (i) The principal, i.e., the exporter.\n (ii) The remitting bank: This is the bank to which the principal\n entrusts the collection, normally the exporter’s own bank.\n (iii) The collecting bank: This is any bank, other than the remitting\n bank, which is involved with the collection. Usually, this will be\n a bank in the importer's country.\n (iv) The presenting bank: This is the collecting bank which notifies\n the drawee of the arrival of the collection and which requests\n for payment or acceptance.\n (v) The drawee: This will normally be the importer to whom\n presentation will be made in accordance with the collection\n instruction.\n\n Article 4: Collection Instruction\n This mainly covers the constituent parts of a collection instruction.\n Additionally, it state"},{"id":1076,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ction\n This mainly covers the constituent parts of a collection instruction.\n Additionally, it states that banks need not examine documents to\n\n 96\n\fascertain instructions and can ignore any instructions from other than\nthe party from whom the collection was received, unless the collection\ninstruction states otherwise.\n\nArticles 5,6,7,8: Presentation, Sight/Acceptance, Release of\nCommercial Document, Creation of Documents\nThese discuss procedures relating to the presentation, release and\ncreation of documents.\n\nArticle 9: Good Faith and Reasonable Care\nThis states that the bank will act in good faith and exercise reasonable\ncare.\n\nArticle 10: Documents - Goods/Services/Performance\nIt excludes banks from responsibility or liability when goods are\ndispatched directly to the address of a bank or consigned to a bank\nwhere prior permission has not been granted. Banks, in such\ncircumstances, n"},{"id":1077,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"or consigned to a bank\nwhere prior permission has not been granted. Banks, in such\ncircumstances, need not accept delivery of the goods.\n\nArticle 11: Disclaimer for Acts of an Instructed Party\n\nArticle 12: Obligations of a Collecting Bank\nIt obliges bank recipients of a collection instruction to advise the\nsender of this instruction of any documents that are not as listed or\nare not received without delays.\n\nArticle 13: Disclaimer on Effectiveness of Documents\nArticle 14: Disclaimer on Delays, Loss in Transit and\nTranslation\nArticle 15: Force Majeure\nArticle 16: Payment without Delay\nArticle 17: Payment in Local Currency\nArticle18: Payment in Foreign Currency\n\n 97\n\fMethods and Terms of Payments\n\n Article 19: Partial Payment\n An important point to note concerns partial payments. In respect of\n clean collections, such payments can be accepted, provided that such\n an action is considered l"},{"id":1078,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"of\n clean collections, such payments can be accepted, provided that such\n an action is considered lawful in the place where payment is made.\n However, with regard to documentary collections, partial payments\n are permissible only where the collection instruction expressly allows\n them.\n\n Articles 20 and 21: Interest Charges and Expenses\n These relate to action to be taken by banks where payment of interest,\n charges and expenses is refused by the drawee.\n\n Articles 22: Acceptance\n Article 23: Promissory Notes and Other Instruments\n\n Article 24: Protesting a Bill of Exchange\n This states that a bank need not protest in the event of non-payment\n or non-acceptance unless expressly authorised to do so in the\n collection instruction.\n\n Article 25: Case of Need\n This indicates that a case of need (i.e., an agent of the exporter who\n is resident in the importer's country) must have his powers"},{"id":1079,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ed (i.e., an agent of the exporter who\n is resident in the importer's country) must have his powers expressly\n stated in the collection instruction. In the absence of this, banks will\n not accept any instructions from the case of need.\n\n Article 26: Advices\n This imposes upon the collecting bank the duty of advising the fate of\n a collection without delay to the bank from which the collection\n instruction was received.\n\n2. 5 BANK PAYMENT OBLIGATIONS\n Bank payment obligation (BPO) is a new payment method in\n international trade. It is an irrevocable undertaking given by an\n Obligor Bank (typically the buyer’s bank) to a Recipient Bank (usually\n the seller’s bank) to pay a specified amount on an agreed date under\n\n 98\n\fthe condition of successful electronic matching of data according to\nan industry-wide set of rules adopted by ICC.\n\nBank payment obligation is a new payment method in intern"},{"id":1080,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ndustry-wide set of rules adopted by ICC.\n\nBank payment obligation is a new payment method in international\ntrade. The main payment methods in international trade so far were\ncash-in-advance payment, documentary collections, documentary\ncredits and open account.\n\nEach payment method in international trade has strengths and\nweaknesses. For example, open account and cash-in-advance\npayments are elementary payment options. They are not only simple,\nbut also inexpensive. However, they inherit high volumes of risks\neither for the importers or the exporters.\n\nDocumentary credits, on the other hand, are relatively secure\npayment methods, but they are complicated and expensive.\n\nDoes international trade finance need another payment method? Can\nbank payment obligation be the right answer?\n\nSome Important Definitions under Bank Payment Obligation\n(BPO):\n“Obligor bank” means buyer’s bank under Bank"},{"id":1081,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ortant Definitions under Bank Payment Obligation\n(BPO):\n“Obligor bank” means buyer’s bank under Bank Payment Obligations.\nThe obligor bank issues the legally binding, valid, irrevocable but\nconditional and enforceable payment undertaking to the recipient\nbank. Obligor bank is an equivalent term of issuing bank under letters\nof credit definitions.\n\n“Recipient Bank” means seller’s bank under Bank Payment\nObligations. “Trade Services Utility” (TSU) means a centralised\nmatching and work flow engine providing timely and accurate\ncomparison of data taken from underlying corporate purchase\nagreements and related documents, such as commercial invoices,\ntransport and insurance.\n\n 99\n\fMethods and Terms of Payments\n\n The URBPO (Uniform Rules for Bank Payment Obligations) ICC\n Publication No. 750, are the rules of Bank Payment Obligation adopted\n by the ICC banking commission.\n\n How Does Bank Paymen"},{"id":1082,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"the rules of Bank Payment Obligation adopted\n by the ICC banking commission.\n\n How Does Bank Payment Obligation Work?\n Bank payment obligation and letter of credit have some characteristics\n in common.\n\n Firstly, banks play a key role in both payment methods. Secondly,\n banks give the irrevocable payment undertaking.\n\n Bank Payment Obligation (BPO) has been established on two main\n expectations: the use of minimum fields, the buyer, the seller and\n respective banks agree on the payment terms and conditions, and on\n the minimum trade information required to assess the credit risk;\n\n The dispatch of documents, such as the bill of lading, certificate of\n origin and certificate of quality, from the seller directly to the buyer.\n\n Given the limited information required by the banks and the\n accelerated document exchange, companies should expect a lower\n rate of discrepancies and an accelerat"},{"id":1083,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"celerated document exchange, companies should expect a lower\n rate of discrepancies and an acceleration of the settlement process.\n\n Step-by Step Bank Payment Obligation Transaction\n Baseline Establishment\n Step 1: Buyer and seller agree on BPO (bank payment obligation) as\n a payment term on the sales contract. Buyer sends his purchase order\n to the seller.\n\n Step 2: Buyer provides the minimum data from the purchase order\n and conditions of the bank payment obligation to the obligor bank.\n\n Step 3: Seller confirms the data from the PO and send its acceptance\n of the BPO conditions to the recipient bank. If both the buyer’s and\n seller’s data are matched on the Transaction Matching Application,\n\n 100\n\fthen the baseline is established. Both buyer and seller will be receiving\nmatching reports from their banks.\n\nMatching\nStep 4: Seller ships the goods as agreed on the sales contract.\n\nStep 5"},{"id":1084,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"from their banks.\n\nMatching\nStep 4: Seller ships the goods as agreed on the sales contract.\n\nStep 5: Seller presents the shipment data and invoice data to its\nbank, which submits it to Transaction Matching Application for\nmatching.\n\nStep 6: Buyer receives a match report from its bank. Buyer is invited\nto accept mismatches, if any.\n\nStep 7: Seller’s bank informs seller about the successful data-set\nmatch.\n\nSettlement\nStep 8: Seller sends the trade documents directly to the buyer. Buyer\nwill clear goods from the customs with these documents.\n\nStep 9: On the due date, the obligor bank debits the proceeds from\nthe buyer’s account.\n\nWhat are the Advantages of the BPO (Bank payment\nObligation) for the Exporters?\n• Bank payment obligation is a secure payment method in\n international trade for the exporter. BPO can be more secure\n than the letter of credit, because the documents will not be\n ch"},{"id":1085,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"e exporter. BPO can be more secure\n than the letter of credit, because the documents will not be\n checked by human beings, thus eliminating alleged discrepancy\n risks.\n\n• The bank payment obligation is cheaper than letter of credit.\n Exporters could get their money very fast from the banks under\n the BPO transactions as documents are checked by an\n automatic system instantly.\n\n 101\n\fMethods and Terms of Payments\n\n • Exporters have more control over the goods until they have\n been paid by the banks under the BPO transactions as\n shipment documents will be held by the exporters during the\n BPO process.\n\n • Exporters do not send paper documents to the banks. Once\n exporters receive their money from the banks, they dispatch\n the documents to the importers separately.\n\n • Exporters could reach pre-shipment and post-shipment finance\n in BPO transactions.\n\n • Once the bank payment obligation is"},{"id":1086,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"pre-shipment and post-shipment finance\n in BPO transactions.\n\n • Once the bank payment obligation is opened, it is almost\n impossible for the importer to cancel the order without the\n consent of the exporter.\n\n • Non-payment risk shifts from importer to importer’s bank,\n which is called obligor bank in the BPO transaction.\n\n What are the Advantages of the BPO (Bank Payment\n Obligation) for the Importers?\n • As a payment method the bank payment obligation is more\n secure than the advance payment, because the BPO is a\n conditional payment method. Under the BPO transactions,\n banks send payment amounts to the exporters only after the\n shipment of the goods, not before.\n\n • Issuing a bank payment obligation may prove that the importer\n is a financially secure and strong company.\n\n • The bank payment obligation is an irrevocable payment\n method like the letter of credit.\n\n • As a result, impo"},{"id":1087,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ayment obligation is an irrevocable payment\n method like the letter of credit.\n\n • As a result, importers can convince exporters to make\n shipments with the BPO much more easily compared to open\n account or documentary collections.\n\n 102\n\f• The BPO facilitates financing of the shipment for the importers.\n It is possible for the importers to pay the transaction amount\n after receiving the shipment, if a deferred payment has been\n agreed upon such as “60 days after match”,“90 days after\n match”, etc.\n• The BPO may protect the buyers, at least in theory, against\n non-shipments, late shipments and inferior quality of goods\n shipments.\n\nBank Payment Obligation – Comparison to Letter of Credit\nand Open Account\n\nBank Payment Obligation Comparison to Letter of Credit\n• Both bank payment obligation and letter of credit have an\n irrevocable structure.\n• Bank payment obligation and letter of credit"},{"id":1088,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"and letter of credit have an\n irrevocable structure.\n• Bank payment obligation and letter of credit are governed by\n ICC rules. BPO rules are URBPO; letters of credit rules are\n UCPDC.\n• The conditional payment guarantee is given by a bank to\n another bank in BPO transactions. BPO is a bank-to-bank\n payment obligation. The conditional payment guarantee is\n given by a bank to a commercial company in L/C transactions.\n (Letters of credit can be issued bank-to-bank transactions as\n well.)\n• Letter of credit is paper-intensive. BPO is an electronic payment\n method.\n• Documents have been checked by bank staff manually under\n letter of credit transactions. Data match is completed by online\n means under BPO transactions.\n• Under BPO transactions, shipment documents would not be\n sent to the banks.\n• Letter of credit is slow and expensive. BPO is fast and not as\n expensive as L/Cs.\n\nBank Paymen"},{"id":1089,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"s.\n• Letter of credit is slow and expensive. BPO is fast and not as\n expensive as L/Cs.\n\nBank Payment Obligation Comparison to Open Account\n\n 103\n\fMethods and Terms of Payments\n\n • Both bank payment obligation and open account are fast and\n easy to handle.\n • Open account is the riskiest payment method for exporters.\n Non-payment risk is stemmed from the importer and must be\n covered by the exporter in full under open account payments.\n On the contrary, under the BPO, the obligor bank is the entity\n that is giving the payment guarantee to the exporter through\n the recipient bank. As a result, non-payment risk mitigates from\n the importer to the importer’s bank under BPO transactions.\n • There are no rules that exist for open account payments. On\n the other hand, bank payment obligations can be issued\n subject to URBPO 750.\n • Under open account transactions, exporters must finance\n impor"},{"id":1090,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"n be issued\n subject to URBPO 750.\n • Under open account transactions, exporters must finance\n importers, whereas they can benefit from pre-shipment finance\n and post-shipment finance under the BPO.\n\n What are the Key Points of Consideration When Choosing an\n International Payment Method?\n Financial Needs of the Parties: Open account payment term is a\n credit granted by the exporter in favour of the importer. In contrast,\n cash in advance is a credit granted by the importer in favour of the\n exporter.\n\n Letters of credit available by deferred payment and documentary\n collections payable with time drafts are also financial credits supplied\n to the buyers.\n\n For these reasons, financial needs of the parties is a key element\n determining the payment terms in international trade transactions.\n Negotiation Power of the Parties: Sometimes, exporters are\n under pressure to sell their products."},{"id":1091,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"Negotiation Power of the Parties: Sometimes, exporters are\n under pressure to sell their products. On the other hand, importers\n are desperate to find a particular good in a short period of time.\n\n If balance of the trade power diminishes in favour of one party, then\n the stronger party may want to benefit from the situation and dictate\n most favourable payment terms.\n\n 104\n\fCountry Risk of the Exporter and the Importer: As an example,\nif the importer resides in a developed country whereas the exporter\nsits in a risky country, then the importer may not want to take risks\nand choose the most secure payment method for himself.\n\nSector of the Transaction Parties: The sector occupied by the\nexporter and the importer is one of the most influencing elements\nthat affect the determination of the payment method. For example,\nthe food and textile sector practise open account payments or\ndocumenta"},{"id":1092,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"payment method. For example,\nthe food and textile sector practise open account payments or\ndocumentary collections; while, on the contrary, the oil sector uses\nletters of credit.\n\nWillingness of the Parties to Take Risks: Willingness to take risks\nis one of the key factors determining the payment method in\ninternational trade.\n\nOccasionally, exporters want to take extra risk, while importers tend\nto trust their suppliers. A risk-taking exporter may ship the goods\nagainst open account terms, whereas a risk-taking importer may\ndecide to pay in advance.\n\nIf both parties want to limit their risks, then they should either choose\ndocumentary collections or documentary credits.\n\nLegislation: If you want to export to some countries, you have to\nchoose either documentary collections or letters of credit as a\npayment method. This is their legislation and every exporter that\nmakes business with the"},{"id":1093,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"redit as a\npayment method. This is their legislation and every exporter that\nmakes business with them has to obey this rule.\n\nCountry Practices: Middle East countries tend to use letters of credit\nintensely, when importing goods. Chinese exporters also use letters\nof credit in great volumes. US importers use documentary collections\nfor small value purchases. German importers would like to pay 30 days\nafter bill of lading date under open account payment terms.\n\n 105\n\fMethods and Terms of Payments\n\n Means of Settlement\n This sometimes refers to the method of settlement. It is simply\n referred to as a means by which importers and exporters settle their\n financial obligations. Thus, whether invoice is made out in local or\n foreign currency, both the importer and exporter must agree on the\n mode/means of settlement. There are various types of interbank\n financial instruments through which fun"},{"id":1094,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"e/means of settlement. There are various types of interbank\n financial instruments through which funds could be transferred by one\n person to another.\n\n Among these are:\n • Bank cheque/personal cheque,\n • Bills of exchange,\n • Banker’s draft,\n • Traveller's cheque,\n • Mail transfer,\n • Swift (Society for Worldwide Interbank Financial\n Telecommunication); and\n • Electronic Money Transfer.\n\n Correspondence Banking\n Correspondence banking is a form of agency relationship. A\n correspondent bank is defined as a bank having a direct connection\n or friendly service relationship with another. Thus, correspondent\n banking simply means the utilisation of another bank's branch which,\n by its physical presence overseas, enables the bank to conduct\n business in a country or market in which it has no presence.\n\n 106\n\fTypes of Correspondent Banking\n• Domestic Correspondent Banking: This is a system whe"},{"id":1095,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"esence.\n\n 106\n\fTypes of Correspondent Banking\n• Domestic Correspondent Banking: This is a system whereby\n banks within a country hold deposits for one another and\n perform various services in return for those deposits.\n\n• Foreign Correspondent Banking: This is a banking system where\n banks, in addition to their own operations, also facilitate the\n transactions of other banks in different countries subject to\n constraints imposed on such other banks by factors such as\n distance, law, lack of expertise, lack of adequate resources, etc.\n\nFeatures of Correspondent Banking\nBasically, there are three main features of correspondent banking that\ndetermine the structure of the relationship as well as the objectives.\nThese are:\n\ni. Transactional: This is in form of a temporary or an ad-hoc\n relationship. It is usually meant for a particular purpose; and,\n once the purpose is achieved, the relation"},{"id":1096,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ship. It is usually meant for a particular purpose; and,\n once the purpose is achieved, the relationship ceases to\n continue, e.g. confirmation of a letter of credit.\nii. Operational: Otherwise known as revolving relationship, it\n represents the core of most correspondent banking\n arrangements. It is usually extended beyond a specific\n transaction into regular or even daily interchange business and\n of a continuous nature. The common example of such\n relationship is availability of acceptance facilities or\n confirmation of letter of credit on a regular basis.\niii. Strategic: This is a long-term relationship with a product of a\n permanent nature. It may involve a decision to form a strategic\n long-term partnership or provision of private electronic fund\n transfer system, or even a decision to form a joint venture or\n to merge.\n\n 107\n\fMethods and Terms of Payments\n\n Factors to be considere"},{"id":1097,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"to form a joint venture or\n to merge.\n\n 107\n\fMethods and Terms of Payments\n\n Factors to be considered before Entering into\n Correspondent Banking\n\n Internal Factors\n • Financial strength;\n • Management team;\n • Local/global rating;\n • Level of expertise;\n • Branch networking;\n • Financial risk involved;\n • Expected benefit;\n • Reliability;\n • Cost of service delivery; and\n • Image of the bank.\n\n External Factors\n • Distance;\n • Law;\n • Language;\n • Rate of economic crimes;\n • Culture of both countries;\n • Economic environment;\n • Restriction of banking services; and\n • Exchange control regulations.\n\n Services Rendered by Correspondent Banks\n • Assist in the confirmation of letters of credit;\n • Assist in bills for collection transactions;\n • Enhance efficient fund transfer on behalf of their principal;\n • Provide an environment for investment of surplus foreign\n exchange;\n • Assist in cu"},{"id":1098,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ir principal;\n • Provide an environment for investment of surplus foreign\n exchange;\n • Assist in currency management;\n • Offer assistance in treasury management;\n • Provide a link between a local bank and the international\n financial market;\n\n 108\n\f• Provide up-to-date information on political, economic and\n exchange control regulations in other countries; and\n• Finance the principal by approving credit line to facilitate\n international transactions.\n\nInter-Bank Settlements\nThe parties in international trade do settle their financial transactions\nthrough proper recording and keeping of accounting data. This\nrecording is done or facilitated through the use of the following\naccounts:\n• NOSTRO account;\n• VOSTRO account; and\n• MIRROR account.\n\nNostro Account: The term \"Nostro\" is a Latin word which means\n\"our”, i.e., our account with you. Thus, a Nostro account is a local\naccount that is be"},{"id":1099,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"which means\n\"our”, i.e., our account with you. Thus, a Nostro account is a local\naccount that is being maintained outside the country, i.e., when a\nNigerian bank has an account maintained in US Dollars with a bank in\nAmerica, such account will be referred to as a Nostro account.\n\nVostro Account: This is a complete opposite of Nostro account, i.e.,\n“Vostro” is a Latin word which simply means \"your\", i.e., your account\nwith us. Thus, a foreign account maintained in our local bank is known\nas Vostro account. If a bank from London maintains an account here\nin Nigeria, that account will be referred to as a Vostro account.\n\nMirror Account: This is an account used to reconcile both Nostro\nand Vostro accounts. Thus, a mirror account is the reverse picture of\nthe transaction passing through the Nostro account abroad, i.e., when\nNostro account is debited, Mirror account will be credited.\n\nSingle"},{"id":1100,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"stro account abroad, i.e., when\nNostro account is debited, Mirror account will be credited.\n\nSingle Euro Payments Area\nThe Single Euro Payments Area (SEPA) is a payment-integration\ninitiative of the European Union for simplification of bank transfers\ndenominated in euro. As of 2020, there were 36 members in SEPA,\nconsisting of the 27 member states of the European Union, the four\n\n 109\n\fMethods and Terms of Payments\n\n member states of the European Free Trade Association (Iceland,\n Liechtenstein, Norway and Switzerland), and the United Kingdom.\n Some countries participate in the technical schemes: Andorra,\n Monaco, San Marino, and Vatican City.\n\n Goals: The aim of SEPA is to improve the efficiency of cross-border\n payments and turn the previously fragmented national markets for\n euro payments into a single domestic one.\n\n SEPA enables customers to make cashless euro payments to any\n accoun"},{"id":1101,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"s into a single domestic one.\n\n SEPA enables customers to make cashless euro payments to any\n account located anywhere in the area, using a single bank account\n and a single set of payment instruments. People who have a bank\n account in a eurozone country can use it to receive salaries and make\n payments all over the Eurozone; for example when they take a job in\n a new country.\n\n The project includes the development of common financial\n instruments, standards, procedures, and infrastructure to enable\n economies of scale. This should, in turn, reduce the overall cost to the\n European economy of moving capital around the region. SEPA does\n not cover payments in currencies other than the euro. This means that\n domestic payments in SEPA countries not using the euro will continue\n to use local schemes, but cross-border payments will, to a high\n degree, use SEPA and euro against eurozone count"},{"id":1102,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"schemes, but cross-border payments will, to a high\n degree, use SEPA and euro against eurozone countries. The Nordic\n countries which (except Finland) do not plan to adopt the euro, have\n in 2017-2019 started initiatives to get simpler, faster and cheaper\n cross-border payments among themselves.\n\n SUMMARY AND CONCLUSION\n In this chapter, we have examined various methods of payment in\n international trade, starting from open account where there must be\n absolute trust between the exporter and the importer, since the goods\n are sold on credit. Advance payment, on the other hand, is applicable\n to a seller’s market, where the seller dictates the terms of the contract.\n In collections, the exporter entrusts the collection of payments to his\n\n 110\n\fbank. Bank Payment Obligation is the new addition to international\npayment terms due to advent technology.\n\nIn conclusion, international merchants"},{"id":1103,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ion to international\npayment terms due to advent technology.\n\nIn conclusion, international merchants must thoroughly understand\nthe four main methods of payment with the new one recently\nintroduced and the responsibility that goes with them before entering\ninto any serious international contract.\n\n 111\n\fMethods and Terms of Payments\n\n REVISION QUESTIONS AND ANSWERS\n\nEssay Questions\n\nQuestion 1\nYour customer, Kelass and Company, imports wool and mohair from the Far\nEast and South America. Purchases have usually been on account basis, but\none of your customer's merchant suppliers now needs assistance to finance\nhis purchases.\n\nYou have been asked to support your customer by arranging one instrument,\nwhich will satisfy the merchant's requirements and allow series of shipments\nand drawings during the forthcoming importing season,\n\nRequired:\nWrite brief notes on:\n (a) The basic instrument (an"},{"id":1104,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ing the forthcoming importing season,\n\nRequired:\nWrite brief notes on:\n (a) The basic instrument (and how it operates) which would assist the\n supplier and satisfy your customer's needs.\n(b) Any disadvantages in using the instrument in (a) above which the\ncustomer must consider.\n(c) Your explanation of the international rules and practices which are\nrelevant to the basic instrument you have defined in (a) above. Discuss the\nprotection afforded to the buyer and the seller, in respect of drawings relating\nto partial shipments.\n\nAnswer\n(a) The basic instrument that would assist the supplier and satisfy the\n customer's needs is an Irrevocable Red Clause Letter of Credit. This\n is a type of credit incorporating a clause, traditionally in red,\n authorising the advising/confirming bank to advance to the exporter\n a specified percentage of the credit value even before shipment and\n presentation"},{"id":1105,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"to the exporter\n a specified percentage of the credit value even before shipment and\n presentation of stipulated documents. A red clause letter of credit can\n be clean/unconditional or documentary/secured/conditional. It is\n unconditional whereby the exporter is merely expected to give a\n written statement that any fund obtained will be utilised for the\n purpose meant. It is conditional whereby the exporter is expected to\n\n 112\n\f give a written pledge that the stipulated documents of title will be\n presented, and in addition shows evidence of title to the related goods\n before he obtains funds.\n\n Relying on the issuing bank's undertaking, the advising/confirming\n bank will give facilities, whether by way of an overdraft, advance or\n otherwise to the exporter. The issuing bank is bound to reimburse the\n advising/confirming bank, even if the exporter defaults. Depending on\n the agreement"},{"id":1106,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"reimburse the\n advising/confirming bank, even if the exporter defaults. Depending on\n the agreement of the parties, the advance may be made clean or\n discussed above. Furthermore, and the special nature of the credit,\n normal and necessary documents such as title documents, transport\n documents, financial documents and third- party documents would be\n called for.\n\n(b) Disadvantages in Using an Irrevocable Red Clause Letter of\nCredit\nThe advances/facilities extended to the exporter are to pay tax, deductible\nfrom the proceeds of sale which is to accrue to his presentation of the\nstipulated documents. Failure of the exporter to make shipment and thus\npresent documents for payment will mean his inability to repay the loan. The\nadvising/confirming bank must be reimbursed by the issuing bank and the\nlatter will definitely pass over this liability to the customer in form of debit\ninto his acco"},{"id":1107,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"d the\nlatter will definitely pass over this liability to the customer in form of debit\ninto his account. Furthermore, if the advance/facility has been made\nunsecured, the risks become outrageous and may be worse than the\nsituation in an open account trading.\n\n(c)The international rules relevant to the basic instrument identified under\n(a) above is the Uniform Custom and Practice for Documenter Credit ICC\nPublication No. 600. The UCP contains series of rates guiding the issuance\nand operation of documentary letters of credit,\nthereby making attempts to standardise terms and conditions under which\ncredits are issued.\n\n Article 31 of the UCP covers partial drawings as well as partial\n shipment. The Article provides that partial drawing and/or partial\n shipments are allowed unless the credit stipulates otherwise. The\n\n 113\n\fMethods and Terms of Payments\n\n Article goes further to say that whe"},{"id":1108,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"tipulates otherwise. The\n\n 113\n\fMethods and Terms of Payments\n\n Article goes further to say that where shipments are made at different\n times, at different ports on the same vessel and on the same voyage,\n such shipment will be regarded as a single shipment, even though a\n number of bills of lading are presented showing shipment on board\n on different dates. This provision enables exporters to enjoy cheaper\n freight charges associated with such shipments. Another protection is\n that partial drawings will only be granted in a case where partial\n shipments are allowed and made.\n\nQuestion 2\nYour Apapa Branch of New Trust Bank Nigeria Plc received notification that\nsome goods had arrived at the Tin Can Island Port, Apapa, Lagos unsigned\nto the branch of the bank for eventual delivery to a valued customer of the\nbank, Sajim Group of Companies Nigeria Limited.\n\nHowever, the bank has received n"},{"id":1109,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ed customer of the\nbank, Sajim Group of Companies Nigeria Limited.\n\nHowever, the bank has received no instruction or information about the\nconsignment from a remitting bank abroad. A representative of Sajim Group\nof Companies informs you that the goods were ordered from a French\ncompany, Sibon & Cie of Paris, and he provides a pro forma invoice as\nevidence of the consignment.\n\nThe representative of Sajim Group of Companies adds further that the goods\nare now required for operational use and any delay in obtaining them will be\nvery costly, not only in terms of warehousing and insurance but also in loss\nbusiness.\n(a) You are required to advise the bank on what to do in this\n circumstance in order not to lose the client.\n(b) What are the features of a collection order?\n\nAnswer\n(a)\n i. Bank may handle the inward collection and must act in good faith\n and reasonable care -Article 9 URC 522.\ni"},{"id":1110,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"y handle the inward collection and must act in good faith\n and reasonable care -Article 9 URC 522.\nii. Goods are not supposed to be consigned directly to the address or\n order of the bank without its prior agreement and, where it does, it\n\n 114\n\f will be at the risk and responsibility of the party dispatching the\n goods. Article 10(a & b) URC 522.\n iii. Where the delivery of the goods so dispatched is taken as the\n collection bank, it assumes no liability or responsibility as to the fate\n of the goods and any omission on its part or that of any party; but it\n must immediately advise the party from whom the collection order\n was received of its actions. Such party must bear the collection\n expenses and charges incurred by the collection bank; Article 10(d &\n e) URC 522.\n iv. Documents and goods as far as the collection is concerned are\n supposed to be released against the terms of the acc"},{"id":1111,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"oods as far as the collection is concerned are\n supposed to be released against the terms of the accompanying\n collection order, but this is not applicable and therefore goods can\n only be released against payment using the pro forma invoice value,\n while customers are to determine between 50 and 100\n fluctuations and overcharges. URC 522 Article 7(a-c).\n v. Payment received from the customer can be placed in account while\n the customer is asked to provide a counter-indemnity of up to 200%\n of the pro forma invoice value to enable us issue a bill of lading\n indemnity for the delivery of the goods while advice of payment is\n sent to the remitting bank immediately. Article 26 (c) URC 522.\n vi. Bill of Lading indemnity is issued to the customer to enable him take\n delivery of the goods.\nvii. Documents, when finally received, are compared to those listed on\n the collection order to ensure co"},{"id":1112,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"Documents, when finally received, are compared to those listed on\n the collection order to ensure compliance with exchange control\n regulations.\nviii. Payments made can be reversed and converted at the ruling rate of\n exchange on the date of release of goods and remitted to the\n remitting bank in accordance with URC 522 (Articles 19 & 20).\n ix. Charges and expenses are to be deducted and collected as in URC\n 522 (Article 21).\n\n (b) Features of a Collection Order\n A collection order must contain the following information in\n compliance with the relevant provision of the Uniform Rules for\n Collection-Article 4, URC 522:\n i. Details of the bank from which the collection was received, including\n\n 115\n\fMethods and Terms of Payments\n\n full name, postal and SWIFT address, telex, telephone, facsimile\n numbers and reference,\nii. Details of the principal, including full name, postal address, and,"},{"id":1113,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ile\n numbers and reference,\nii. Details of the principal, including full name, postal address, and, if\n applicable, telex, telephone and facsimile numbers.\niii. Details of the draw, including full name, postal address, or the\n domicile at which representation is to be made and, if applicable,\n telex, telephone and facsimile numbers.\niv. Details of the presenting bank, if any, including full name, postal\n address, and, if applicable, telex and facsimile numbers.\nv. Amount(s) and currency(ies) to be collected.\nvi. List of documents enclosed and the numerical counts of each\n document.\n (a) Terms and conditions upon which payment and/or acceptance\n is to be obtained.\n (b) Terms of delivery of documents against:\n (i) Payment and/or acceptance;\n (ii) Other terms and conditions.\nvii It is the responsibility of the party preparing the collection instruction\n to ensure that the terms of the deliv"},{"id":1114,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ponsibility of the party preparing the collection instruction\n to ensure that the terms of the delivery of documents are clearly and\n unambiguously stated; otherwise banks will not be responsible for any\n consequences arising therefrom.\nviii Charges to be collected, indicating whether they may be waived or\n not.\nix Interest to be collected, if applicable, whether it may be waived or\n not, including:\n a. Rate of interests;\n b. Interest periods;\n c. Basis of calculation (for example, 360 days or 365 days in a year)\n as applicable.\nx. Method of payment and form of payment advice.\nxi Instruction in case of non-payment, non-acceptance/or non-\n compliance with other instructions.\nxii. Collecting instruction should bear the complete address of the\n drawee or to the domicile at which the presentation is to be made. If\n address is incomplete or incorrect, then ascertain the proper\n address.\n\n 116"},{"id":1115,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ion is to be made. If\n address is incomplete or incorrect, then ascertain the proper\n address.\n\n 116\n\fxiii The collecting bank will not be liable or responsible for any ensuing\n delay as a result of an incomplete/incorrect address being provided.\nxiv If the principal nominates a representative to act as need be. In the\n event of non-payment and/or non- acceptance, the collection\n instruction should clearly and fully indicate the power of such case of\n need. In the absence of such indication, the bank will not accept any\n instruction from the case of need.\n\nQuestion 3\nHosanna Oils Plc is your major customer and major player in the export\nmarket, mainly in red palm and vegetable oils. Your bank has advised and\nconfirmed an irrevocable documentary letter of credit on behalf of the issuing\nbank. The following information relates to the documentary letter of credit:\nIssuing Bank - Commerce an"},{"id":1116,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"k. The following information relates to the documentary letter of credit:\nIssuing Bank - Commerce and Credit Bank, Ontario, Canada\nAccreditor - Millennium Foods Limited\nBeneficiary - Hosanna Oils Plc\nType & Value - Documentary letters of credit available by\n negotiation valued at US$ 1.5m quarterly\nConsignment - 250 metric tons of red palm oil to be shipped in\n 20x20 tankers before August 20, Part-shipment\n not allowed. L/C expiry date September 16.\n\nHosanna Oil Plc wrote a letter to you on September 6, intimating you that\nthey have not acted on their copy of the credit advice, which was sent to you\nbecause the accreditor merely informed them by facsimile message and that\nthey are awaiting the original advice copy through courier; and when you\ncross-checked the file, you discovered the credit advice was sent: the same\nadvice to you.\n\nRequired:\n(d) Briefly define revolving documentary let"},{"id":1117,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"dit advice was sent: the same\nadvice to you.\n\nRequired:\n(d) Briefly define revolving documentary letters of credit as to time.\n(e) Explain to Hosanna Oils Plc with reference to the relevant provisions\n of UCP 600 the meaning of documentary letters of credit available by\n negotiation.\n\n 117\n\fMethods and Terms of Payments\n\n(f) State the relevant provision of the UCP 600 on Teletransmitted and\n Pre-Advised Credits so as to educate Hosanna Oils Plc on the\n complaints.\n\nAnswer\n(a) Revolving documentary letters of credit as to time is when the original\n amount of a credit is reinstalled after the expiration of any given\n period of time which could be monthly, quarterly or annually. The\n arrangement is used where a contract could extend over a fairly long\n period of time and is of a repetitive nature. It removes the\n cumbersome effects of establishing new documentary credit each\n time a transac"},{"id":1118,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ture. It removes the\n cumbersome effects of establishing new documentary credit each\n time a transaction occurs.\n(d) According to Article 2(a) of UCP 600, all credits must clearly indicate\n whether they are available by sight payment, by deferred payment,\n by acceptance or by negotiation. Negotiation, according to Article 2 of\n UCP 600, means the giving of value of Draft(s) and/or document(s)\n but the bank authorised to negotiate. Mere examination of documents\n without giving of value does not constitute a negotiation.\n(e) Tele-transmitted and Pre-Advice Credits Article 11 (a-c)\n (ii) When an issuing bank instructs an advising bank by an\n authenticated Tele- transmission to advise a credit or an\n amendment -to a credit, the Tele-transmission will be deemed\n to be operative credit instrument or the operative amendment,\n and no mail confirmation should be sent. Should a mail\n confirmation"},{"id":1119,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"t or the operative amendment,\n and no mail confirmation should be sent. Should a mail\n confirmation nevertheless be sent, it will have no effect and\n the advising bank will have no obligation to check such mail\n confirmation against the operative credit instrument or the\n operative amendment received by Tele-transmission. Article 11\n (a).\n (ii) If the Tele-transmission states \"full details to follow\" (or words\n of similar effect) or states that the mail confirmation is to be\n the operative credit instrument or the operative amendment,\n then the Tele-transmission will not be deemed to be the\n operative credit instrument or the operative amendment. The\n issuing bank must forward the operative credit instrument or\n\n 118\n\f the operative amendment to such advising bank without delay.\n Article 1 l(a) ii.\n\n A preliminary advice of the issuance or amendment of an\n irrevocable credit shall only b"},{"id":1120,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"l(a) ii.\n\n A preliminary advice of the issuance or amendment of an\n irrevocable credit shall only be given by an issuing bank if such\n bank is prepared to issue the operative credit instrument or the\n operative amendment thereof. Unless otherwise stated in such\n preliminary advice, the issuing bank having given such pre-\n advice shall be irrevocably committed to issue or amend the\n credit, in terms not consistent with the pre-advice without delay\n Article 11\n (THE SAME AS QUESTION 5 OF CHAPTER 1, PLS!!!!!!!!!)\nQuestion 4\n\nPayment\nSule Refrigerator and Air-conditioning Limited is a manufacturer of Klis Kool\nbrand of industrial and domestic air conditioners. The company has recently\ncontracted a large order of export of Industrial Cooling Systems to Jelly Lilly\nLimited and Tourism Company of Ghana.\n\nThe Finance Director called to see you this morning to discuss some salient\nissues on the"},{"id":1121,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"f Ghana.\n\nThe Finance Director called to see you this morning to discuss some salient\nissues on the suitable methods of payment for the transaction denominated\nin US Dollars. He informed you that at the board of directors' meeting earlier\nin the day, there was a discussion of documentary letter of credit but they\nwere not clear on how it operates. You have taken pains to educate the\nFinance Director on the advantages and disadvantages of documentary\nletters of credit as a form of payment, particularly over other forms of\npayment. You further made an attempt to educate the Finance Director on\nhow an exporter can achieve the highest degree of security under\ndocumentary credit.\n\nRequired:\n(a) Give a detailed explanation of how a documentary letter of credit\n suitable for the transactions involving Sule Refrigeration and Air-\n conditioning Limited works.\n(b) Sate six (6) advantages of the de"},{"id":1122,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"lving Sule Refrigeration and Air-\n conditioning Limited works.\n(b) Sate six (6) advantages of the description in [a) above.\n\n 119\n\fMethods and Terms of Payments\n\n(c) Give three (3) points on the disadvantages of the method.\n(d) State six (6) points on how the discrepancies can be reduced.\n\nAnswer\n(a) The documentary letter of credit suitable for Sule Refrigeration is the\n confirmed irrevocable inward documentary letter of credit. Although\n any form of documentary credit would do, the confirmed inward\n documentary letter would provide the highest degree of security.\n\n This is a method in international trade in which the exporter would be\n paid Dollar drafts drawn by him on the confirming/advising bank, the\n issuing bank or any other party (as stipulated in the credit] or such\n stipulated party(ies) who is/are acting on the instructions of the\n importer through the issuing bank, provided t"},{"id":1123,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"rty(ies) who is/are acting on the instructions of the\n importer through the issuing bank, provided the terms and conditions\n of the credit are met by the exporter.\n\n(b) Advantages to the Exporter Only\n i. It is the safest method of receiving payment - the exporter gets\n paid immediately or thereafter once the credit terms are\n complied with.\n ii. It increases the credibility of parties, particularly where the\n parties to the credit are well known and of sound financial\n backing.\n iii. It affords the exporter the opportunity of bank credit facilities,\n particularly under documentary acceptance credit.\n iv. Documentary credit can serve as collateral security for bank\n borrowing.\n v. Documentary credit cannot be cancelled or amended without\n the consent of all the parties.\n\n(c) Disadvantages to an Exporter\n i. It is very costly, particularly where the exporter bears the cost.\n ii. It could"},{"id":1124,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"to an Exporter\n i. It is very costly, particularly where the exporter bears the cost.\n ii. It could expose the exporter to exchange rate risk.\n iii. The exporter may not be paid at all where he fails to adhere to\n the terms of the document, even where such error is a simple\n one which could have earned him quantum merit payment\n\n 120\n\f iv. A simple amendment requires the consent of all parties.\n v. He must fulfil the obligations due to other parties, even where\n there is a ground for amendment or cancellation.\n\n(d) How to Reduce the Discrepancies\n i. The exporter can make cost-sharing or cost-transfer as part\n of the terms of the credit in which the importer shares or\n bears the transaction cost.\n ii. The exporter should hedge against the risk of exchange rate\n movement either with his bank or at the stock market.\n i. The exporter can call for a steady documentary letter of credit\n to fu"},{"id":1125,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"k or at the stock market.\n i. The exporter can call for a steady documentary letter of credit\n to further guarantee payment and ensure that only a reputable\n bank in Nigeria advised the credit.\n ii. A highly reputable company should be appointed as the freight\n forwarder in Nigeria to ensure good quality package and\n preparation of documents in compliance with the importer's\n requirements.\n iv. Any other arrangement with third parties should be by way of\n transferable credit whereby the same credit is transferred.\n v. The exporter should apply for export credit from the Nigerian\n Export-Import (NEXIM) Bank to cushion the effect of the cost\n of credit facilities.\n vi. The exporter can also apply for export incentives with NEXIM\n Bank to reduce transanction costs and enjoy export incentives.\n\nQuestion 5\n(a) An irrevocable credit constitutes a \"definite undertaking of the issuing\n bank, pro"},{"id":1126,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"Question 5\n(a) An irrevocable credit constitutes a \"definite undertaking of the issuing\n bank, provided that the stipulated documents are presented to the\n nominated bank or to the issuing bank\" and that the terms and\n conditions of the credit are complied with. What are the terms and\n conditions validating the liability of the issuing and confirming banks,\n under the Uniform Customs and Practice, as regards:\n (i) Payment? (Give 2 points)\n (ii) Acceptance? (Give 2 points)\n (iii) Negotiation? (Give 2 points)\n (iv) Confirmation? (Give 2 points]\n\n 121\n\fMethods and Terms of Payments\n\n (v) Reimbursement? (Give 2 points]\n\n(b) (i) What is a revolving documentary letter of credit as to time?\n (ii) State the conditions under which a revocable credit will\n become irrevocable.\n\nAnswer\n(a) Article 8 states the liability of the issuing and confirming banks as\n follows:\n\n(i) Payment\n - If the credit"},{"id":1127,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"states the liability of the issuing and confirming banks as\n follows:\n\n(i) Payment\n - If the credit calls for sight payment to pay at sight.\n - If the credit calls for differed payment to pay on maturity.\n(ii) Acceptance\n If the credit provides for acceptance: by the confirming bank, to\n accept at maturity draft(s) drawn by the beneficiary on the confirming\n bank and pay them at maturity. Or\n By another drawee bank; to accept and pay at maturity draft[s] drawn\n by the beneficiary on the confirming bank, in the event the drawee\n bank stipulated in the credit does not accept draft(s) accepted but\n not paid by such drawee bank at maturity.\n(iii) Negotiation\n If the credit provides for negotiation, to pay without recourse to\n drawer's and/or bonafide holders, drafts drawn by the beneficiary\n and/or documents presented under the credit.\n(iv) Confirmation\n A confirmation of an irrevocable cre"},{"id":1128,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"and/or documents presented under the credit.\n(iv) Confirmation\n A confirmation of an irrevocable credit by another bank (the\n confirming bank] upon the authorisation or request of the issuing bank\n constitutes a definite undertaking of the confirming bank, in addition\n to that of the issuing bank, provided that the stipulated documents\n are presented to the confirming bank or to any other nominated bank\n and that the terms and conditions on the credit are complied with.\n - If the credit provides for sight payment to pay at sight.\n - If the credit provides for deferred payment to pay on the\n maturity date(s) determination in accordance with\n the stipulation of the credit.\n\n 122\n\f - If the credit provides for acceptance by the confirming bank to\n accept drafts drawn by the beneficiary on the confirming bank.\n - If the draft is drawn on another drawee bank to accept and pay\n at maturity, dr"},{"id":1129,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"confirming bank.\n - If the draft is drawn on another drawee bank to accept and pay\n at maturity, drafts drawn by the beneficiary on the confirming\n bank on the terms the drawee bank stipulated in the credit\n does not accept, draft(s] accepted but not paid by such.\n - If the credit provides for negotiation, to negotiate without\n recourse to drawer(s) and/or legitimate holders, drafts drawn\n by the beneficiary and/or documents] presented under the\n credit.\n\n(v) Reimbursements\n If another bank is authorised or requested by the issuing bank to add\n its confirmation to a credit but not prepared to do so, it must inform\n the issuing bank without delay unless the issuing bank specifies\n otherwise in its authorisation or request; to add confirmation, the\n advising bank may advise the creditor to the beneficiary without\n confirmation.\n\n If the confirming bank, it may or may not extend its confir"},{"id":1130,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"the beneficiary without\n confirmation.\n\n If the confirming bank, it may or may not extend its confirmation to\n any amendment and must inform the issuing bank without delay.\n(b) (i) A revolving documentary letter of credit as to time is a type of\n credit authorising the beneficiary to ship goods of a specified\n amount, quantity and standard as may have been agreed or\n the credit as evidenced by the relative exchange control\n documents, to the accreditor on a regular basis, usually\n monthly, quarterly or any other stipulated time.\n (ii) A revocable credit is that whose terms and conditions can be\n amended or altered by any of the parties, particularly the\n accreditor, without the prior consent of others. A revocable\n credit becomes irrevocable where any of the parties,\n particularly the beneficiary or the confirming/advising banker,\n has already incurred a cost on it, in which case the acc"},{"id":1131,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ficiary or the confirming/advising banker,\n has already incurred a cost on it, in which case the accreditor\n would be made to pay for such up to the time of cancellation\n or amendment.\n (REPEATED!!!!!!!!! SEE QUESTION 6 OF CHAPTER 1.)\n\n 123\n\fMethods and Terms of Payments\n\n MULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTIONS (MCQs) AND ANSWERS\n\n1. If the above transaction(??????) had been the remittance on account\n of an amount in US Dollars, which would have been correct?\n (a) Debit customer, credit Vostro (Global bank) Debit Mirror, credit\n beneficiary (USA bank).\n (b) Debit customer, credit Mirror Nostro (Global bank). Debit\n Nostro, credit beneficiary (USA bank).\n (b) Debit customer, credit Nostro (Global bank) .Debit Mirror,\n credit beneficiary (USA bank).\n (d) Debit customer, credit Vostro (Global bank). DebitNostro,\n Credit beneficiary (USA bank).\n\n2. Which of the following is the most secured payment term"},{"id":1132,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"Nostro,\n Credit beneficiary (USA bank).\n\n2. Which of the following is the most secured payment term from the\n exporter’s point of view?\n (a) Net cash on receipt of goods\n (b) Cash with order\n (c) Documentary collection D/P\n (d) Confirmed irrevocable documentary credit\n\n 124\n\f3. An exporter wishes to retain control of his goods until payment. Which\n of the following terms of payment will achieve this objective?\n (a) Documentary collection D/A\n - Transport documents\n - Air waybills with importer as consignee\n (b) Net cash on receipt of goods\n (c) Documentary collection D/P\n - Transport document\n - Air waybills with the presenting bank as consignee\n (d) Open account\n\n4. Which of the following terms of payment will entirely eliminate country\n risk?\n (a) Revocable documentary credit\n (b) Documentary collection D/P (with NCM insurance)\n (c) Confirmed irrevocable documentary credit\n (d) Documen"},{"id":1133,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ntary collection D/P (with NCM insurance)\n (c) Confirmed irrevocable documentary credit\n (d) Documentary collection D/A with acceptance per Aval\n\n5. Collection of financial documents not accompanied by commercial\n documents is:\n (a) Documentary Collection.\n (b) Documentary Acceptance credit.\n (c) Clean collection.\n (d) Double collection.\n\n6. ADC calls for presentation of an Insurance Certificate. Which of the\n following Insurance documents would be acceptable for CIF shipment\n where the invoice value is N75,000,000?\n (i) Insurance Certificate for N82,500,000\n (ii) Insurance Policy for N85,000,000\n (iii) Insurance declaration under open cover for N75,000,000\n (iv) Insurance certificate for N75,000,000\n (a) i and ii only\n (b) i and iv only\n (c) ii and iii only\n (d) iii and iv only\n\n 125\n\fMethods and Terms of Payments\n\n7. The order of safety from the point of view of an exporter is:\n (a) Op"},{"id":1134,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"hods and Terms of Payments\n\n7. The order of safety from the point of view of an exporter is:\n (a) Open Account, Payment in Advance, Bills for Collection, Open\n Account.\n (b) Advance Payment, Documentary Letter of Credit, Bills for\n Collection, Open Account.\n (c) Documentary Letters of Credit, Open Account, Advance\n Payment.\n (d) Open Account, Payment in Advance, Documentary Letters of\n Credit, Bills for Collection.\n\n8. One of the three main features of correspondent banking that extends\n beyond a specific transaction into regular or even daily interchange\n business and of a continuous nature is known as ____\n A. Transactional.\n B. Operational.\n C. Strategic.\n D. Translational.\n\n9. In this type of payment transaction, all shipping documents, including\n title documents, are handled directly by the trading parties. The role\n of banks is limited to clearing funds as required. This statement"},{"id":1135,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"by the trading parties. The role\n of banks is limited to clearing funds as required. This statement is true\n for:\n (a) Clean payments.\n (b) Bill for collection.\n (c) Documentary credit.\n (d) None of these.\n\n10. The following, EXCEPT one, are means of early recognition of\n discrepancies in correspondence relationship arrangements:\n (a) Tracers on outstanding items\n (b) Correct coding and classification\n (c) Correction of previous errors\n (d) Ensuring accuracy of records\n\n 126\n\fAnswers\n\n1. b\n2. b\n3. c\n4. c\n5. c\n6. b\n7. b\n8. b\n9. a\n10. c\n\n 127\n\fMethods and Terms of Payments\n\n REFERENCES\n\nAdetifa, S.B. (2002).International Business Finance in Nigeria, Mcbay\n Publishers, Lagos.\n\nCowdel, P/Hyde, D/Watson A. (2002). Finance of International Trade.\n Financial World Publishing, UK.\n\nExaminers’ Reports of Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria.\n\nInternational Chamber of Commerce (1996). Uniform"},{"id":1136,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"rts of Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria.\n\nInternational Chamber of Commerce (1996). Uniform Rules for Collection.\n ICC Publication No. 522.\n\nInternational Chamber of Commerce (2007). Uniform Customs and Practice\n for Documentary Credits, ICC Publication No. 600.\n\nOdekunle, T. (2016). Questions and Answers on International Finance with\n Notes. Jaytees Publishers, Lagos.\n\n 128\n\f Basis and Theories of\n International Trade\n\nLearning Outcomes:\nAt the end of this Chapter, the reader should be able to explain the various\nTheories of International Trade:\n\n• Absolute Advantage;\n• Theory of Comparative Advantage;\n• Mercantilist Theory;\n• Heckscher-Ohlin Theory;\n• The New Trade Theory;\n• Raymond Vernon’s Product Life Cycle Theory; etc.\n\n3.1 INTRODUCTION\n International trade is the exchange of capital, goods, and services\n across international borders or territories. Trading partners reap"},{"id":1137,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"f capital, goods, and services\n across international borders or territories. Trading partners reap\n mutual gains when each nation specialises in goods for which it holds\n an advantage and then engages in trade for other products.\n International trade has existed in the absence of any international\n financial arrangements through direct exchange or barter.\n International trade activities are the major causes of international\n finance. Without international trade, there won’t be international\n finance. International trade is the trade among countries; each\n country has its own economic policies, natural resources and trials. All\n these interact together to elevate the well-being of the world. The\n basic reason for trade between countries is the existence of\n differences in prices or qualities, differences in demand pattern,\n differences in efficiency of production, quality of production (o"},{"id":1138,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"s, differences in demand pattern,\n differences in efficiency of production, quality of production (output),\n natural gifts (endowment) and the obvious varieties in the demand\n patterns in different nations.\n\fBasis and Theories of International Trade\n\n Trade theories help managers and government policymakers focus on\n three critical questions:\n • What products should be imported and exported?\n • How much should be traded?\n • With whom should they trade?\n\n3.2 WHY DO NATIONS TRADE?\n 1. A nation trades because it expects to gain something from its\n trading partner.\n 2. Whenever a buyer and a seller come together, each expects to\n gain something from the other. The same expectation applies\n to nations that trade with each other.\n 3. It is virtually impossible for a country to be completely self-\n sufficient without incurring undue costs.\n 4. International trade allows a country to specialise"},{"id":1139,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"-\n sufficient without incurring undue costs.\n 4. International trade allows a country to specialise in the\n manufacture and export of products and services that it can\n produce efficiently, as well as import products and services that\n can be produced more efficiently in other countries.\n 5. Limits on imports may be beneficial to producers, but not to\n consumers.\n\n3.3 THEORIES OF INTERNATIONAL TRADE\n Different theories of international trade have evolved over time.\n They include:\n\n3.3.1 Mercantilist Theory\n The first theory of international trade emerged in England in the mid-\n 16th century. Its principal assertion was that gold and silver were the\n mainstay of national wealth and essential to vigorous commerce. The\n primary objective of Mercantilism was to increase the power of the\n nation or state wealth which is measured by its holdings of treasure\n (usually gold).\n At that time, gold"},{"id":1140,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"n or state wealth which is measured by its holdings of treasure\n (usually gold).\n At that time, gold and silver were the currency of trade between\n countries. A country earned gold and silver by exporting goods. By\n\n 130\n\f the same token, importing goods from other countries would result in\n an outflow of gold and silver to those countries.\n\n The mercantilist argued that it was in a country’s best interest to\n maintain a trade surplus, i.e., to export more than its import. They\n saw no virtue in a large volume of trade. Rather, they recommended\n policies to maximise exports and minimise imports. To achieve this,\n imports were restricted by tariffs and quotas, while exports were\n subsidised.\n\n The flaw with mercantilism was that it viewed trade as a zero-sum\n game in which one country’s gain came at the expense of some other\n countries, since a surplus in international trade for one count"},{"id":1141,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"n came at the expense of some other\n countries, since a surplus in international trade for one country must\n be a deficit for other(s).\n Protectionism is a major characteristic of mercantilism.\n\n3.3.2 Theory of Absolute Advantage\n Adam Smith (1776), in his book “Wealth of Nations,” countered the\n mercantilists’ assumption that trade is a zero-sum game. He argued\n that countries differ in their ability to produce goods efficiently. He\n also pointed out that the mercantilists drive for more exports and\n fewer imports turns social priorities upside down with the following\n points:\n • National well-being is based on the ability to consume products\n now and in the future. Imports are part of the expansion of\n national consumption that nations seek.\n • Exports are not desirable on their own; rather, they are useful\n because they pay for imports.\n • Trade is a positive-sum activity, as trade fr"},{"id":1142,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"her, they are useful\n because they pay for imports.\n • Trade is a positive-sum activity, as trade freely transacted\n between countries generally leads to gain for all countries.\n\n Adam Smith’s theory of absolute advantage promotes free trade. He\n argued that a country has an absolute advantage in the production of\n a product when it is more efficient than any other country in producing\n it and enhance global efficiency through participation in free trade.\n\n 131\n\fBasis and Theories of International Trade\n\n Consider the effects of trade between two countries, Nigeria and\n Thailand. The production of any good (output) requires resources\n (inputs) such as land, labour and capital. Assume that Nigeria and\n Thailand have the same amount of resources which can be used to\n produce either rice or cocoa. Assume further that 200 units of\n resources are available in each country. Imagine that in Nig"},{"id":1143,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ocoa. Assume further that 200 units of\n resources are available in each country. Imagine that in Nigeria it\n takes 10 resources to produce one ton of cocoa and 20 resources to\n produce one ton of rice. Thus, Nigeria has capacity to produce either\n 20 tons of cocoa and no rice or 10 tons of rice and no cocoa.\n\n Similarly, imagine that in Thailand it takes 40 resources to produce\n one ton of cocoa and 10 resources to produce one ton of rice. Thus,\n Thailand has capacity to produce either 20 tons of rice or no cocoa,\n or 5 tons of cocoa with no rice. Nigeria has an absolute advantage in\n the production of cocoa while Thailand has absolute advantage in the\n production of rice.\n\n Table 3.1\n (i) Resources Required to Produce 1 Ton of Cocoa and Rice\n Cocoa Rice\n Nigeria 10 20\n Thailand 40 10\n\n (ii) Production and Consumption without Trade and Specialisation\n (Assuming resources are allocated eq"},{"id":1144,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"i) Production and Consumption without Trade and Specialisation\n (Assuming resources are allocated equally between products,\n i.e., 100 for cocoa and 100 for rice).\n\n Cocoa Rice\n Nigeria 10.0 5.0\n Thailand 2.5 10.0\n Total Production 12.5 15.0\n\n (iii) Production with Specialisation (All resources for a product, i.e.,\n 200 for either cocoa or rice).\n\n Cocoa Rice\n\n 132\n\f Nigeria 20.0 0.0\n Thailand 0.0 20.0\n Total Production 20.0 20.0\n\n Thus, specialisation increases trade output and consumption of both\n cocoa and rice in both nations. This affirms that trade is a positive-\n sum game which produces net gains for parties involved. Therefore,\n a country has an absolute advantage in the production of a product\n when it is more efficient than any other country in producing it. He\n advocated that countries should specialise in the production of goods\n for which they have absolute advantage and the"},{"id":1145,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"untries should specialise in the production of goods\n for which they have absolute advantage and then trade these for\n goods produced by other countries.\n\n Smith’s basic argument, therefore, is that a country should never\n produce goods at home if it can be bought at lower cost from other\n countries. He demonstrated that by specialising in the production of\n goods in which each has absolute advantage and both countries\n benefited by engaging in trade.\n\n3.3.3 Assumptions Underlying the Theory of Absolute Advantage\n (a) Only two types of goods can be produced.\n (b) Two countries are involved in international trade.\n (c) There is no particular standard through which one production\n can be exchanged for another.\n (d) There are fixed quantities of land and labour in each country.\n (e) There is perfect mobility of labour within each country but\n perfect immobility across the country.\n\n3.3.4 Th"},{"id":1146,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"perfect mobility of labour within each country but\n perfect immobility across the country.\n\n3.3.4 Theory of Comparative Advantage\n David Ricardo, in his book of 1817, “Principles of Political Economy,”\n advanced Adam Smith’s theory a step further by explaining what\n might happen when one country has absolute advantage in the\n production of all goods. According to Ricardo’s theory of comparative\n advantage, it is sensible for a country to specialise in the production\n of those goods that it produces most efficiently and to buy the goods\n that it produces less efficiently from other countries. The keyword in\n\n 133\n\fBasis and Theories of International Trade\n\n Ricardo’s theory is comparative, which implies ‘relative’ and not\n necessarily absolute. The principle of comparative advantage is based\n on the importance of opportunity cost - the value/quantity of other\n products forgone to produce"},{"id":1147,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"d\n on the importance of opportunity cost - the value/quantity of other\n products forgone to produce more of a particular product. A country\n should export the goods and services it can produce at a low\n opportunity cost and import the goods and services that it would\n otherwise produce at a high opportunity cost.\n\n Illustration\n Assume that Nigeria is more efficient in the production of both cocoa\n and rice, that it has an absolute advantage in the production of both\n products. In Nigeria, it takes 10 resources to produce one ton of cocoa\n and 13.5 resources to produce one ton of rice. Thus, given her 200\n units of resources, Nigeria can either produce 20 tons of cocoa and\n no rice or 15 tons of rice and no cocoa, or any combination within the\n resources given.\n\n In Thailand, it takes 40 resources to produce one ton of cocoa and 20\n resources to produce one ton of rice. Thus, Thailand ca"},{"id":1148,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"esources to produce one ton of cocoa and 20\n resources to produce one ton of rice. Thus, Thailand can either\n produce 5 tons of cocoa and no rice or 10 tons of rice and no cocoa,\n or any combination within the resources given.\n\n Again, assume that, without trade, each country uses half of her\n resources on each product respectively. Thus, without trade, Nigeria\n will produce 10 tons of cocoa and 7.5 tons of rice, while Thailand will\n produce 2.5 tons of cocoa and 5 tons of rice.\n\nFig. 3.1. Ricardo’s Theory of Comparative Advantage\n\n 134\n\f 20\n\n 15\n Cocoa\n 10\n\n 5\n\n 2.5\n\n 0 0 3.75 5 7.5 10 15 20\n\n Rice\n\n Without trade, each country must consume what it produces. By\n engaging in trade, the two countries can increase their combined\n production of rice and cocoa, and consumers in both nations can\n consume more of both goods. The theory of comparative advantage\n suggests that trade is a positiv"},{"id":1149,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"n\n consume more of both goods. The theory of comparative advantage\n suggests that trade is a positive–sum game in which all countries that\n participate realise economic gain. This theory provides a strong\n rationale for encouraging free trade.\n\n3.3.5 Assumptions and Limitations of the Free Trade Theories\n The theories of absolute and comparative advantage both make\n assumptions that may not be entirely valid.\n\n 1. Full employment of resources;\n 2. Exclusive pursuit of economic efficiency objectives;\n 3. Equitable division of gains from specialisation;\n 4. Only two countries and two commodities;\n 5. Exclusion of transport costs;\n 6. A static rather than a dynamic view;\n 7. Exclusion of services;\n 8. Unrestricted factor mobility;\n\n 135\n\fBasis and Theories of International Trade\n\n3.4 MODERN THEORIES OF INTERNATIONAL TRADE\n\n3.4.1 Heckscher-Ohlin Theory\n Also called Factor Proportion Theory,"},{"id":1150,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"HEORIES OF INTERNATIONAL TRADE\n\n3.4.1 Heckscher-Ohlin Theory\n Also called Factor Proportion Theory, Swedish economists Eli\n Heckscher (1919) and Bertil Ohlin (1933) put forward a different\n explanation of comparative advantage.\n\n This theory was born out of the criticism of David Ricardo’s theory. It\n holds that all the assumptions of the law of comparative cost\n advantage are misleading; they argued that comparative advantage\n arises from difference in national factor endowments such as land,\n labour and capital. Nations have various factor endowments.\n\n The theory advocates exploitation in factor endowments inclusive of\n technology, tastes and preferences. It states that a country should\n concentrate on producing a commodity for which it has relative\n abundance and cheap resources for production. Different factor\n endowments explain differences in factor costs. The more the factor\n end"},{"id":1151,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"oduction. Different factor\n endowments explain differences in factor costs. The more the factor\n endowment, the lower its costs. Heckscher–Ohlin theory predicts that\n countries will export those products that make intensive use of factors\n that are locally abundant and import products that make intensive use\n of factors that are locally scarce. This theory attempts to explain the\n pattern of international trade that we observe in the world economy\n today.\n\n Like Ricardo’s theory, Heckscher–Ohlin theory argues that free trade\n is beneficial. On the other hand, unlike Ricardo’s theory, Heckscher–\n Ohlin theory argues that the pattern of international trade is\n determined by differences in factor endowments rather than\n differences in productivity.\n\n Assumptions of the Theory\n (a) A country should specialise in the production of goods based\n on the factors of production in which she is natu"},{"id":1152,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"hould specialise in the production of goods based\n on the factors of production in which she is naturally endowed.\n (b) Some goods are labour-intensive while some have their\n intensity based on land, capital and the entrepreneur.\n\n 136\n\f (c) Not only are there many factors of production, but also\n different goods require different ratios of the diverse factors.\n (d) Different countries have diverse factors of production/gifts\n (endowments) and these result in different relative factor\n prices.\n\n Does The Heckscher-Ohlin Theory Hold?\n • Wassily Leontief (1953) theorised that since the US was\n relatively abundant in capital compared to other nations, the\n US would be an exporter of capital-intensive goods and an\n importer of labour-intensive goods.\n • However, he found that US exports were less capital-intensive\n than US imports.\n • Since this result was at variance with the predictions of"},{"id":1153,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ess capital-intensive\n than US imports.\n • Since this result was at variance with the predictions of the\n trade theory, it became known as the Leontief Paradox.\n • Because of this, new theories were put forward:\n ➢ Human Skills Theory;\n ➢ Product Life-Cycle Theory; and\n ➢ Similarity of Preferences Theory.\n\n3.4.2 Human Skills Theory\n Donald Keesing (1966) emphasises differences in endowments and\n intensities of skilled and unskilled workers.\n His explanation of the Leontief paradox is that since the US has\n highly trained educated workers relative to other countries, US\n exports tend to be skilled-labour intensive.\n\n3.4.3 Product Life-Cycle Theory\n Raymond Vernon initially proposed the product life-cycle theory in the\n mid-1960s. It was developed in response to the failure of the\n Heckscher–Ohlin model to explain the observed pattern of\n international trade. The theory suggests that trade"},{"id":1154,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"–Ohlin model to explain the observed pattern of\n international trade. The theory suggests that trade patterns are\n influenced where a new product is introduced. It explains that just\n because a new product is developed by a company in a country and\n first sold in that country’s market does not follow that the product\n must be produced in that country. It could be produced abroad at\n\n 137\n\fBasis and Theories of International Trade\n\n some low-cost location and then exported back into the country where\n it was initially produced; as products mature, both the location of sales\n and the optimal production location will change, affecting the flow and\n direction of trade.\n\n Globalisation and integration of the world economy have made this\n theory less valid today; the theory is ethnocentric. Production today\n is dispersed globally, while products are introduced in multiple\n markets simultaneous"},{"id":1155,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"uction today\n is dispersed globally, while products are introduced in multiple\n markets simultaneously.\n\n Stages of International Product Life Cycle\n There are three stages in an international product life cycle:\n 1. New Product\n • The IPLC begins when a company in a developed\n country wants to exploit a technological breakthrough\n by launching a new, innovative product on its home\n market.\n • Such a market is more likely to start in a developed\n nation because more high-income consumers are able\n to buy and willing to experiment with new, expensive\n products (low price elasticity).\n • Furthermore, easier access to capital markets exists to\n fund new product development. Production is also more\n likely to start locally in order to minimise risk and\n uncertainty: “\n • Export to other industrial countries may occur at the end\n of this stage that allows the innovator to increase\n revenue an"},{"id":1156,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"rial countries may occur at the end\n of this stage that allows the innovator to increase\n revenue and also the downward descent of the product’s\n experience curve. Other advanced nations have\n consumers with similar desires and incomes making\n exporting the easiest first step in an internationalisation\n effort.\n • Competition will come from a few local or domestic\n players that produce their own unique product\n variations.\n\n 138\n\f2. Maturing Product\n Exports to markets in advanced countries further increase\n through time, making it economically possible and sometimes\n politically necessary to start local production.\n\n The product’s design and production process becomes\n increasingly stable.\n\n Foreign direct investments (FDI) in production plants drive\n down unit cost because labour cost and transportation cost\n decrease.\n\n Offshore production facilities are meant to serve local markets"},{"id":1157,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"d transportation cost\n decrease.\n\n Offshore production facilities are meant to serve local markets\n that substitute exports from the organisation’s home market.\n Production still requires high-skilled, high-paid employees.\n Competition from local firms jump-start in these non-domestic\n advanced markets. Export orders will begin to come from\n countries with lower incomes.\n\n3. Standardised Product\n During this phase, the principal markets become saturated. The\n innovator's original comparative advantage based on functional\n benefits has been eroded.\n\n The firm begins to focus on the reduction of process cost rather\n than the addition of new product features. As a result, the\n product and its production process become increasingly\n standardised and enable further economies of scale while\n increasing the mobility of manufacturing operations. Labour\n can start to be replaced by capital.\n\n To"},{"id":1158,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"reasing the mobility of manufacturing operations. Labour\n can start to be replaced by capital.\n\n To counter price competition and trade barriers or simply to\n meet local demand, production facilities will relocate to\n countries with lower incomes. As previously the case in\n advanced nations, local competitors will get access to first-\n hand information and can start to copy and sell the product.\n\n 139\n\fBasis and Theories of International Trade\n\n • The demand of the original product in the domestic\n country dwindles from the arrival of new technologies,\n and other established markets will have become\n increasingly price-sensitive. Whatever market is left\n becomes shared between competitors, who are\n predominately foreign.\n • A multinational company (MNC) will internally maximise\n “offshore” production to low-wage countries since it can\n move capital and technology around, but not labour."},{"id":1159,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"production to low-wage countries since it can\n move capital and technology around, but not labour. As\n a result, the domestic market will have to import\n relatively capital-intensive products from low- income\n countries. The machines that operate these plants often\n remain in the country where the technology was first\n invented.\n\nFig. 3.2: Product Life Cycle\n\n Export New Mature Standardised\n net product product product\n\n Time\n\n Developed Developing Inventor’s\n country country country\n\n Country where new product is launched\n Other advanced, high-income country\n Developing, low-income country\n\nThree Stages from the Initiating Country’s Viewpoint\nInternational Product Life Cycle\nProduct Stage Trade Target Competitors Production Cost\n Market Locally\n\n 140\n\f1. New Limited; USA/Inventor’s Few local firms Initially high\n Production for country\n home market\n2. Mature Increasing USA, advanced Co"},{"id":1160,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ocal firms Initially high\n Production for country\n home market\n2. Mature Increasing USA, advanced Competitors Declining due to\n exports and later from advanced economies of\n developing markets scale\n markets\n3. Standardisation Declining USA/Inventor’s Competitors Increase owing to\n export at first; country from mostly lower economies\n later in phase developing of scale and\n become markets comparative\n imports disadvantages\nRaymond Vernon, 1966. “International Investment and International Trade\nin the Product Cycle.” The Quarterly Journal of Economics, 80(2), pp. 190-\n207.\n\n 3.4.4 Similarity of Preferences Theory\n When a firm develops a new product in response to observed\n conditions in its home market, it is likely to turn to those foreign\n markets that are most similar to its domestic market, when\n commencing its initial international expansion activities.\n\n So much trade is taking plac"},{"id":1161,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ket, when\n commencing its initial international expansion activities.\n\n So much trade is taking place among industrialised countries because\n of:\n\n • The growing importance of acquired advantages (skills and\n technology).\n • Markets are large enough to support new product\n introductions.\n • Cultural similarity.\n • Historical and political relationships, as well as economic\n agreements.\n\n 3.4.5 New Trade Theory\n Marc Melitz and Pol Antras started a new trend in the study of\n international trade which emerged in the 1970s. New trade theory\n\n 141\n\fBasis and Theories of International Trade\n\n suggests that the ability of firms to gain economies of scale (unit cost\n reductions associated with a large scale of output) can have important\n implications for international trade. Countries may specialise in the\n production and export of particular products because, in certain\n industries, the world"},{"id":1162,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ise in the\n production and export of particular products because, in certain\n industries, the world market can only support a limited number of\n firms. New trade theory emerged in the 1980s. Paul Krugman won the\n Nobel Prize for his work in 2008.\n\n3.4.6 What Are the Implications of the New Trade Theory for\n Nations?\n • Nations may benefit from trade even when they do not differ in\n resource endowments or technology.\n • A country may dominate in the export of a commodity simply\n because it was lucky enough to have one or more firms among\n the first to produce that commodity.\n • Governments should consider strategic trade policies that\n nurture and protect firms and industries where first-mover\n advantages and economies of scale are important.\n\n The New trade theory has been criticised as not providing an efficient\n allocation of resources. The criticism opines that, if a nation has to\n pr"},{"id":1163,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"t providing an efficient\n allocation of resources. The criticism opines that, if a nation has to\n protect infant industries, which does not support comparative\n advantage, then the nation will do better to shift resources into an\n industry where it has competitive advantage and can dominate\n without protection. This would represent the most efficient utilisation\n of global resources.\n\n3.5 PORTER’S DIAMOND OF COMPETITIVE ADVANTAGE\n Michael Porter (1990) tried to explain why a nation achieves\n international success in a particular industry. Porter identified four\n attributes that promote or impede the creation of competitive\n advantage:\n\n 142\n\f 1. Factor endowments;\n 2. Demand conditions;\n 3. Relating and supporting industries; and\n 4. Firm strategy, structure, and rivalry.\n\n3.5.1 What Is Porter’s Diamond of Competitive Advantage?\n\nFig. 3.3: Determinants of National Competitive Advantage:"},{"id":1164,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"rter’s Diamond of Competitive Advantage?\n\nFig. 3.3: Determinants of National Competitive Advantage: Porter’s\nDiamond\n\n Firm Strategy,\n Structure, and\n Rivalry\n\n Factor Demand\n Endowments Conditions\n\n Related and\n Supporting\n Industries\n\n Does Porter’s Theory Hold?\n • Government policy can affect demand through product\n standards and influence rivalry through regulation and antitrust\n laws.\n • It can impact the availability of highly educated workers and\n advanced transportation infrastructure.\n • The four attributes, government policy, and chance to work as\n a reinforcing system, are complementing each other and, in\n combination, creating the conditions appropriate for\n competitive advantage.\n • So far, Porter’s theory has not been sufficiently tested to know\n how well it holds up.\n\n 143\n\fBasis and Theories of International Trade\n\n Other Trade Theories\n • Trade Theory of Country Size;\n •"},{"id":1165,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"Basis and Theories of International Trade\n\n Other Trade Theories\n • Trade Theory of Country Size;\n • Trade Theory of Technology Gap (Josiah Tucker in the mid-\n 1700s);\n • Trade Theory of Independence, Interdependence and\n Dependence;\n • Global Strategic Rivalry Theory;\n • Opportunity Cost Theory; and\n • Investment Theories.\n\nSUMMARY AND CONCLUSION\nThis chapter has reviewed a number of theories that explain why it is\nimportant for a country to be involved in international trade and the pattern\nof international trade that is observed in the world economy. The theories of\nAdam Smith, David Ricardo and Heckscher–Ohlin make strong arguments for\nunrestricted free trade. In addition, the mercantilist theory and the new trade\ntheory can be interpreted to support government intervention to promote\nexports and to reduce imports through tariffs and quotas.\n\nSince the latter part of the 20th century"},{"id":1166,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"exports and to reduce imports through tariffs and quotas.\n\nSince the latter part of the 20th century, change from the ‘inward-looking\nimport-substitution-oriented development framework’ to the ‘outward-\nlooking export-led, growth-oriented development and privatisation have led\nto significant dependence on trade and investment inflows as trusted ways\nto economic development.\n\n 144\n\f REVISION QUESTIONS AND ANSWERS\n\nQuestion 1\n(a) What do you understand by the term ‘comparative cost advantage’ in\n international business?\n(b) State the basic assumptions underlying the comparative cost\n advantage theory.\n(c) Critically review, in seriatim, the basic assumptions underlying the\n comparative factor cost advantage theories, using the Heckscher-\n Ohlin or any other widely accepted relevant theory.\n\nAnswer\n(a) The law of comparative cost advantage, propounded first as the law\n of absolute advantage"},{"id":1167,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"Answer\n(a) The law of comparative cost advantage, propounded first as the law\n of absolute advantage by Adam Smith and later developed by Ricardo,\n states that a country should specialise on the production of goods upon\n which it enjoys comparative cost advantage.\n\n(b) Basic Assumptions\n (i) There are no trade barriers.\n (ii) There are only two countries and two goods.\n (iii) There are no transportation costs between countries.\n (iv) Other things are equal.\n (v) The whole world uses a single currency.\n (vi) There are no other costs than cost of production.\n (vii) All other factors of production remain the same worldwide.\n\n(c) The Heckscher-Ohlin theory emanated as a result of the criticisms of\n David Ricardo’s theory.\n (i) It assumes that not only was there more than one factor of\n production, but also that different goods require different\n proportions of the various factors.\n (ii) Some"},{"id":1168,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ion, but also that different goods require different\n proportions of the various factors.\n (ii) Some goods are labour-intensive, some land-, some capital-\n and some entrepreneur-intensive.\n (iii) Different countries have different amounts of factors of\n production and these result in different relative factor prices.\n\n 145\n\fBasis and Theories of International Trade\n\n (iv) A country should specialise in the production of goods based\n on the factors of production on which it is naturally endowed.\n (v) Japan, which is naturally endowed in skills, science and\n technology, should specialise in the production of technology-\n based goods; the USA, naturally endowed in organised financial\n markets, should specialise in the production of capital-intensive\n goods, while Nigeria and China, with high levels of endowment\n in population, should produce labour-intensive goods.\n (vi) The weak points of"},{"id":1169,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"levels of endowment\n in population, should produce labour-intensive goods.\n (vi) The weak points of the Heckscher-Ohlin theory are enunciated\n by the Leontif Paradox (1954).\n\nQuestion 2\nThe Classical Theory of Comparative Advantage has long been the pillar of\nmodern international economic theory. But comparative advantage was\nbased on the assumption that the factors of production such as labour and\ncapital were immobile, or trapped in their home country. Following this\nprinciple, how would you answer the following questions?\n\n (a) Is this assumption realistic in the world we live in today, in which\n firms move workers and obtain capital across frontiers?\n(b) Even if this assumption is no longer relevant, are the principles of\n comparative advantage and the benefits of international trade still\n true? Please state them.\n\nAnswer\n (a) The assumptions no longer hold because globalisation has"},{"id":1170,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"till\n true? Please state them.\n\nAnswer\n (a) The assumptions no longer hold because globalisation has:\n (i) Exposed many hidden markets and other factors of production\n while multinationals now look for market potentials, taking into\n consideration risk factors. Multinational corporations site industries\n in markets where the returns on investment are optimally utilised.\n (ii) Globalisation concepts enable small companies to have access\n to funds in international markets at cheaper cost, thus availing\n them of financial optimisation.\n (iii) Globalisation allows the financial centres like London, New\n York, Tokyo, and Frankfurt to provide funds needed for\n industrial expansion in countries where labour is cheaper.\n\n 146\n\f (iv) Goods are produced in remote countries for consumption in the\n advanced countries.\n\n (c) Benefits of international trade still hold and they are as follows:\n (i) It"},{"id":1171,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"vanced countries.\n\n (c) Benefits of international trade still hold and they are as follows:\n (i) It boosts and stimulates local and international consumption.\n (ii) It increases level of social interaction between nations.\n (iii) It enhances technological advancement.\n (iv) It creates employment.\n (v) It increases social and economic powers.\n (vi) It reduces marginal production cost.\n (vii) It is a vehicle for even distribution and utilisation of resources.\n\nQuestion 3\nMercantilism is a bankruptcy theory that “has no place in the modern world.”\nBased on the above assertion,\n(a) List four new international trade theories that debunked\n the mercantilist theories.\n(b) State the basic assumptions underlying at least three of these new\n theories.\n\nAnswer\n(a)\n(i) Heckscher-Ohlin theory.\n(ii) Product life cycle theory.\n(iii) New trade theory.\n (v) Porter's theory of national competitive advanta"},{"id":1172,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"duct life cycle theory.\n(iii) New trade theory.\n (v) Porter's theory of national competitive advantage.\n\n (bi) Heckscher-Ohlin's theory argues that the pattern of\n international trade is determined by differences in factor endowments.\n It predicts that countries will export those goods that make\n intensive use of locally abundant factors and will import goods\n that make intensive use of factors that are locally scarce.\n (ii) Raymond Vernon's product life cycle theory suggests that trade\n patterns are influenced with the invention of a new product.\n (iii) The new trade theory states that trade allows a nation to\n specialise in the production of certain goods that have attained\n\n 147\n\fBasis and Theories of International Trade\n\n economies of scale by covering the costs of producing those\n goods while buying goods that it does not produce from other\n nations that are similarly specialised. I"},{"id":1173,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ds while buying goods that it does not produce from other\n nations that are similarly specialised. It also states that those\n in industries where substantial economies of scale exist will\n only have the support of world market for a few firms.\n (iv) Porter's theory of national competitive advantage suggests that\n the pattern of trade is influenced by four attributes of a nation\n - Factor endowments - Domestic demand conditions -Relating\n and supporting industries - Firm strategy structure and rivalry.\n\nQuestion 4\n(a) Drawing on the theory of comparative advantage to support your\n arguments, outline the case for free trade.\n(b) Mercantilism is a bankrupt theory that has no relevance in the modern\n world. Critically review this statement in line with modern trade\n theories.\n\nAnswer\n(a) The theory of comparative advantage suggests that it makes sense\n for a country to specialise in producin"},{"id":1174,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"heory of comparative advantage suggests that it makes sense\n for a country to specialise in producing those goods that it can produce\n most efficiently, while buying goods that it can produce relatively less\n efficiently from other countries; even if it implies buying from other\n countries that which it could produce more efficiently herself.\n\n The theory of comparative advantage suggests that unrestricted free\n trade brings about increased world production, that is, trade is a\n positive-sum game.\n\n According to the theory, opening a country to free trade stimulates\n economic growth which creates dynamic gains from trade. The theory\n is a step ahead of Adam Smith's theory of absolute advantage which\n advocates that a country should specialise in the production of goods\n in areas where it has absolute advantage and import goods where\n other countries have absolute advantage, mindless of t"},{"id":1175,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"as absolute advantage and import goods where\n other countries have absolute advantage, mindless of the fact that\n some countries have absolute advantage in all goods.\n\n 148\n\f(b) Mercantilists argued that it was in a country's best interest to run a\n balance of trade surplus.\n They viewed trade as a zero-sum game, in which one country's gains\n cause loss for other countries.\n\n Modern trade theories that debunked the mercantilist theory are:\n i. The theory of absolute advantage;\n ii. The theory of comparative advantage;\n iii. Heckscher-Ohlin theory, which argues that the pattern of\n international trade is determined by differences in factor\n endowments;\n iv. The product life cycle theory by Raymond Vernon, which\n suggests that trade patterns are influenced by where a new\n product is introduced;\n v. The new trade theory, which states that trade allows a nation\n to specialise in the producti"},{"id":1176,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ed;\n v. The new trade theory, which states that trade allows a nation\n to specialise in the production of certain goods that foster\n economies of scale by lowering the costs of producing those\n goods and buying goods that it does not produce from other\n nations that are similarly specialised.\n vi. Porter's theory of national competitive advantage, which\n suggests that the trade pattern is influenced by four attributes\n of a nation. These are: factor endowments, domestic demand\n conditions, supporting industries; and firm strategy structure\n and rivalry.\n\n 149\n\fBasis and Theories of International Trade\n\nQuestion 5\nInternational Product Life Cycle (IPLC) theory is an important theory that\nstresses that a company will begin to export its products and later take\nForeign Direct Investment (FDI) as the product moves through its life cycle.\n\nRequired:\n(a) What is International Product Life Cycl"},{"id":1177,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"as the product moves through its life cycle.\n\nRequired:\n(a) What is International Product Life Cycle (IPLC)?\n(b) Give a brief description of the relevant stages of the IPLC.\n(c) What are the objectives of the IPLC mode?\n\nAnswer\n(a) International Product Life Cycle (IPLC) describes an\n internationalisation process in which a local manufacturer in an\n advanced country [Vernon referred to the United States of America\n (USA) as the principal source of inventions] starts by selling a new\n technologically advanced product to high net worth companies in its\n home market.\n\n Production capabilities are built locally by close contact with clients\n and hence minimise risk and uncertainty. As demand from customers\n in other markets rises, production increasingly shifts, enabling the firm\n to increase economies of scale and to expose trade barriers. As the\n product matures and becomes more of a comm"},{"id":1178,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"economies of scale and to expose trade barriers. As the\n product matures and becomes more of a commodity, the number of\n competitors increases.\n\n(b) The IPLC consists of 3 stages, viz:\n • New Product Stage: The IPLC begins when the company\n in a developed country wants to explore technological\n breakthrough by bringing a new product supply to the home\n market.\n • Standardised Product: During this stage, the principal\n market becomes saturated. The innovator's original\n comparative advantage based on financial benefits becomes\n eroded.\n • Maturing Product: Export to markets in advanced countries\n further increases through time, making it economically\n\n 150\n\f possible and sometimes profitable. The product design and\n production process become increasingly stable. Foreign Direct\n Investment (FDI) in production partly drives down unit\n cost because labour and transportation cost has increas"},{"id":1179,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"(FDI) in production partly drives down unit\n cost because labour and transportation cost has increased.\n\n(c) Objectives of IPLC\n ✓ The initial objective of the IPLC model was to advance the trade\n theory beyond David Ricardo's static framework of comparative\n advantage.\n ✓ It is ideal for a country with an absolute advantage in all product\n categories to trade and allow its workforce to specialise in those\n categories with the highest value added.\n ✓ It explains how trade pattern changes over time.\n\nQuestion 6\nWhat are the major causes of trade and investment flow?\n\nAnswer\n(i) Difference in Factor Endowments: The skewed distribution of factors\nof resources, i.e. production capabilities, possibilities and scales, differ across\nnations, eventually forcing nations to trade among themselves.\n\n(ii) Cost Advantage: Trade and investment flows are goaded by cost\nadvantage. Cost leadership is wha"},{"id":1180,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"(ii) Cost Advantage: Trade and investment flows are goaded by cost\nadvantage. Cost leadership is what international firms aim at in a world\nof thinning down margins.\n\n(iii) Patterns of Specialisation: Trade and investment flows are triggered\nby patterns of specialisation. Countries specialise. For example, Germany,\nJapan, UK and USA are good in manufacturing machine tools and equipment;\nSingapore and India are good in IT and IT-enabled services; USA and France\nare good in aircraft manufacturing; Switzerland and India are good in\npharmaceutical goods, etc. This specialisation spells need for trade flows.\n\n(iv) Profit from Exchange: Trade and investment flows are motivated\nby profit from exchange. Milton Friedman would say the sole purpose of\nbusinesses is making profit by serving the society. Profit sources are\n\n 151\n\fBasis and Theories of International Trade\n\nmany: one is international e"},{"id":1181,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"y. Profit sources are\n\n 151\n\fBasis and Theories of International Trade\n\nmany: one is international exchange. Export sales guarantee more profit\nper unit sale than comparable domestic sale.\n\n(v) Diversification of Sources and Markets for Physical and\nFinancial Products and Risk: Trade and investment flows are\npropelled by the need for diversification of sourcing and markets both\nfor physical and financial products. Multiple sources and markets both\nfor inputs and outputs and both for physical and financial products are\nessential to ward off uncertainties of supply chain and consumer\npatronage.\n\n(vi) Exploitation of Natural Resources: Investment and trade flows\nare driven by profit-seeking transnational corporations that are\ninterested in the exploitation of natural resources. This pattern of trade\nand investment leads to a high growth, though that is subject to the\nvicissitudes of terms-o"},{"id":1182,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"f trade\nand investment leads to a high growth, though that is subject to the\nvicissitudes of terms-of-trade and risk-return aspects of foreign\ninvestments.\n\n(vii) Policy “U-turn” towards Mercerisation by Many Economies:\nSince the latter part of the 20th century, change from the ‘inward-looking,\nimport-substitution-oriented development framework’ to the ‘outward-\nlooking, export-led, growth-oriented development and privatisation has led\nto significant dependence on trade and investment inflows as trusted ways\nto economic development.\n\n(viii) Common Market/Currency/Economy: At the sub-regional level,\nthe policy of Common Market/Currency/Economy has facilitated intra-\nregional trade and investment.\n\n(ix) Bilateral Trade/Investment and Economic Relationship:\nThis helps to open a new paradigm that relies on mutual understanding\nand growth result.\n\n(x) Enabling Multilateralism along with Regio"},{"id":1183,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"hat relies on mutual understanding\nand growth result.\n\n(x) Enabling Multilateralism along with Regional Pluralism: Finally,\na major cause of trade and investment flow is multilateralism, in the form of\nGATT & WTO and World Bank & Multilateral Investment Guarantee Agency\n\n 152\n\f(MIGA), besides others, co-existing with regional pluralism in the form of\nregional trade blocks and agreements between trade blocks.\n\n MULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTIONS (MCQs) AND ANSWERS\n\n(1) In the Heckscher–Ohlin model, the pattern of international trade is\n determined by:\n (a) Difference in factor endowments.\n (b) Volume of exports, including factor costs.\n (c) Volume of imports, excluding factor costs.\n (d) Labour and capital adequacy.\n\n(2) As per Michael Porter’s Diamond Model for the National Competitive\n Advantage, which of the following is NOT the attribute of the\n Diamond?\n (a) Diamond conditions.\n (b) Facto con"},{"id":1184,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"which of the following is NOT the attribute of the\n Diamond?\n (a) Diamond conditions.\n (b) Facto conditions.\n (c) National GDP.\n (d) Related and supporting industries.\n\n(3) Which of the following is an implication of the new trade theory?\n (a) A country may both export and import goods in the same\n industry.\n (b) The country as a whole must gain from trade.\n (c) A country can only hurt itself by using government protection\n to promote export.\n (d) The consumer loses from the increased variety of goods that\n trade makes available.\n\n(4) The theory of competitive advantage was propounded by:\n (a) David Ricardo.\n (b) Adam Smith.\n (c) Raymond Vernon.\n (d) Michael Porter.\n\n 153\n\fBasis and Theories of International Trade\n\n 154\n\f(5) Under the Heckscher-Ohlin Model, international trade can lead to\n increase in ____\n (a) Consumer welfare only, if output of both products is increased.\n (b) Output o"},{"id":1185,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"increase in ____\n (a) Consumer welfare only, if output of both products is increased.\n (b) Output of both products and consumer welfare in both\n countries.\n (c) Total production of both products, but not consumer welfare in\n both countries.\n (d) Consumer welfare in both countries, but not total production of\n both products.\n\n(6) According to Michael Porter, all except one of the following are\n determinants of a country’s competiveness:\n (a) Factor condition.\n (b) Demand condition.\n (c) Government condition.\n (d) Related and supporting industries.\n\n(7) In Adam Smith’s view, international trade:\n (a) Benefited both trading countries.\n (b) Was based on absolute cost differences.\n (c) Reflected the resource base of the countries in question.\n (d) All of the above.\n\n(8) According to Adam Smith’s postulation, trade will not take place\n where:\n (a) One country has absolute advantage in both co"},{"id":1186,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"’s postulation, trade will not take place\n where:\n (a) One country has absolute advantage in both commodities.\n (b) Both countries have absolute advantage in both commodities.\n (c) Neither country has absolute advantage in both commodities.\n (d) None of the above.\n\n(9) Vernon’s international product life cycle theory\n (a) Explains the movement from absolute advantage to\n comparative advantage.\n (b) Explains why a product that begins as a nation’s export often\n ends up becoming an import.\n (c) Extends the concept of comparative advantage by taking into\n consideration the endowment and cost of factors of production.\n\n 155\n\fBasis and Theories of International Trade\n\n (d) Shows why the United States, surprisingly, exports relatively\n more labour-intensive goods and imports capital-intensive\n goods.\n\n(10) Which of the following theories holds that countries will produce and\n export products t"},{"id":1187,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"oods.\n\n(10) Which of the following theories holds that countries will produce and\n export products that use large amounts of production factors than\n they have in abundance?\n (a) The factor endowment theory.\n (b) The theory of absolute advantage.\n (c) Mercantilism.\n (d) None of the above.\n\nAnswers\n1. a\n2. b\n3. a\n4. d\n5. b\n6. c\n7. c\n8. a\n9. c\n10. a\n\n 156\n\f REFERENCES\n\nCharles, W.L. (2005).International Business: Competing in the Global\n Marketplace. The McGraw Hill Companies Inc., USA.\n\nClark, E. (2002).International Finance. Thomson, High Holborn House,\n London.\n\nOdekunle, T. (2015). Concepts and Theories of International Finance,\n Jaytees Publishers.\n\nPaul, R.R. (2004). Money, Banking and International Trade. Kalyani\n Publishers, New Delhi.\n\nPugel, U. (2015). International Economics. McGraw Education, (16th\n Edition).\n\nMulugeta, G.B. (2017). International Trade Theories and Its Trends."},{"id":1188,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"aw Education, (16th\n Edition).\n\nMulugeta, G.B. (2017). International Trade Theories and Its Trends.\n Research Gates.\n\n 157\n\f Trade Risks and Risk\n Assessment in\n International Trade\n\nLearning Outcomes:\nAt the end of this Chapter, the reader should be able to understand and\nexplain the following risks in international trade:\n• Commercial risk;\n• Product, production and transportation risk;\n• Financial risk;\n• Currency risk;\n• Counterparty risk;\n• Adverse business risk; and\n• Political risk.\n\n4.1 INTRODUCTION\n Buyers and sellers that are engaged in international trade may\n experience one or more forms of risk which can have severe financial\n consequences on their transactions. The risks can also be managed\n to a reasonable extent. The major types of risks in international trade\n are as follows:\n\n4.2 COMMERCIAL RISKS\n Commercial risks are caused due to the following factors:\n (i) Lack of kn"},{"id":1189,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ws:\n\n4.2 COMMERCIAL RISKS\n Commercial risks are caused due to the following factors:\n (i) Lack of knowledge about the foreign markets;\n (ii) Inadaptability of the export product to change in the conditions\n of the foreign market requirements;\n (iii) Longer transit time and ignorance;\n (iv) Varying situations to be handled, not anticipated before export.\n\n Commercial risks exist in the domestic market too. But, their impact\n in the international market is greater, in comparison to the domestic\n\fmarket. The changes in the international market are hazardous and\ndifficult to anticipate. Suitability and acceptability of the product\ninternational market is rather difficult to gauge. Variations in demand\nand supply conditions are more unpredictable.\n\nMost of the commercial risks are to be borne by the exporters.\nExporters can now shift these risks to the professional risk bearers,\npaying insura"},{"id":1190,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"y the exporters.\nExporters can now shift these risks to the professional risk bearers,\npaying insurance premium. The exporter is not aware of the\nconditions in the foreign market the same way he is aware of the\ndomestic market. Long distances to travel, along with cost and time\nimplications, distinguish international trade from domestic trade. If\ngoods are not sold or price realisation is lower than anticipated, due\nto changes in demand or supply, the exporter has to bring back the\ngoods, incurring additional freight cost, or opt to sell the goods at a\nloss.\n\nIn the international market, as in the domestic market, the presence\nof competitors influences the demand and supply conditions, and the\nentry of new competitors depresses the market more. Further, local\nproduction may bring down the prices. Introduction of substitutes to\ncapture the market may take away the exporter's share of the"},{"id":1191,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"prices. Introduction of substitutes to\ncapture the market may take away the exporter's share of the market.\n\nThe price realisation of the product in the export market is influenced\nby:\n(a) Changes in Exchange Rates: Changes in the home currency\n or foreign currency affect the price realisation. If the home\n currency is devalued, the competitive capacity' of the exporter\n is enhanced. If the foreign currency is depreciated, there is\n considerable reduction in the exporter's competitive strength.\n(b) Changes in Import Duties or Tariff Barriers: Changes in\n import duties and creation of tariff barriers disturb even an\n established market. In this field, through the efforts of the WTO\n (GATT), import duties have been fairly reduced and the market\n has become stable. On account of these impediments,\n exporters open manufacturing facilities in the importing\n countries to overcome these problem"},{"id":1192,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ents,\n exporters open manufacturing facilities in the importing\n countries to overcome these problems.\n\n 159\n\fTrade Risks and Risks Assessment in International Trade\n\n (c) Changes in Transport Costs: Transport costs constitute,\n generally, a major part of the invoice value; and so, any change\n in transport costs affects the competitive edge of the exporter.\n Change in transport costs does not affect FOB price.\n (d) Changes in Foreign Market Characteristics: A classical\n example is change in styles soon after shipment of goods; in\n particular, when the shipment is made without letter of credit,\n ready-made garments suffer greatly from this problem.\n\n Minimisation of Commercial Risks\n Commercial risks can be minimised by using forecasting techniques\n and keeping a careful watch on the changing business conditions in\n the concerned country, in particular, and also keeping track of the\n chan"},{"id":1193,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ng business conditions in\n the concerned country, in particular, and also keeping track of the\n changes in the world economy.\n\n4.3 TRANSPORTATION RISK/TRANSIT RISK\n Transportation of cargo has undergone radical improvements over a\n long period. Most of the goods are transported by sea. Transit risks\n are a common hazard for those engaged in export/import business.\n The list of dreary and hazardous risks in transit is long, viz. storms,\n collisions, theft, leakage, explosion, spoilage, fire, and high sea piracy.\n\n The issue of supply chain risk is gaining increased attention as\n manufacturing and distribution companies assess potential threats to\n their business. Specifically, shippers can be forced to pay the bill when\n one of their carriers gets into an accident or loses a shipment and has\n too little insurance to cover all the damages. The issues involved in\n transportation risk are:"},{"id":1194,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"s\n too little insurance to cover all the damages. The issues involved in\n transportation risk are:\n - Costly litigation;\n - Identity theft;\n - Fraud/cargo theft;\n - Irreparable damage to your brand;\n - Disruption in service: from your suppliers, or shipments to your\n customers; in a lean environment, the impact of disruption is\n magnified.\n\n 160\n\f The key to mitigating transportation risk to your company is simple;\n you must know who you are doing business with and build forensic\n evidence which attests to the fact that you have done your due\n diligence in selecting your carriers. This can be done by making and\n documenting smart, safe decisions. Every exporter and importer\n should have working knowledge of marine insurance so that he knows\n whether he is getting the required risk protection at the minimum\n cost. It is always possible to transfer the financial losses resulting from\n peri"},{"id":1195,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"on at the minimum\n cost. It is always possible to transfer the financial losses resulting from\n perils at sea and perils in transit to professional risk bearers known as\n underwriters. Principles of marine insurance are equally applicable to\n insurance of air cargo.\n\n4.4 LIQUIDITY RISK\n This is a financial risk that, for a certain period of time, a given\n financial asset, security or commodity cannot be traded quickly\n enough in the market without impacting the market price.\n\n Types of Liquidity Risk\n i. Market Liquidity: This is an asset that cannot be sold due to\n lack of liquidity in the market – essentially a subset of market\n risk.\n\n ii. Funding Liquidity: This is the risk that liabilities:\n - Cannot be met when they fall due;\n - Can only be met at an uneconomic price; and\n - Can be named – specific or systemic.\n\n How Can Liquidity Risk Be Managed?\n There are a number of ways that b"},{"id":1196,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"amed – specific or systemic.\n\n How Can Liquidity Risk Be Managed?\n There are a number of ways that banks, companies, and investors can\n manage their exposure to liquidity risk:\n\n Forecasting Cash Flow: Cash flow forecasting estimates the\n amount of cash businesses or individuals will have on hand in the\n future, which can help them better manage their finances.\n\n 161\n\fTrade Risks and Risks Assessment in International Trade\n\n Comparing Assets and Liabilities: By comparing assets and\n liabilities, businesses can determine how much cash they could\n generate on short notice and how much they might owe.\n\n Creating a Buffer between Earnings and Expenses: A large gap\n between the amount of cash coming in and going out can make it\n easier to cover unexpected expenses.\n\n Prioritising Short-Term Liquid Asset Investments over Long-\n term Illiquid Assets: Short-term assets are generally much easier"},{"id":1197,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"uid Asset Investments over Long-\n term Illiquid Assets: Short-term assets are generally much easier\n to sell than long-term assets. By holding liquid assets before\n purchasing illiquid ones, businesses and investors can more easily\n generate cash without incurring major losses.\n\n Analysing Financial Ratios: Analysing financial and liquidity ratios\n can help identify and limit exposure to liquidity risk. Reducing leverage\n and taking on too much debt can be a major source of liquidity risk.\n\n By reducing leverage, companies and individuals can shrink the gap\n between the amount they have and the amount they owe. This\n effectively reduces their liquidity risk.\n\n Conducting Stress Tests: Simulations or other analyses can identify\n risk areas in the event of an economic shock. Using this information,\n the company or individual can develop processes and safeguards to\n mitigate risk.\n\n Diversi"},{"id":1198,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"mation,\n the company or individual can develop processes and safeguards to\n mitigate risk.\n\n Diversifying Investments: Diversifying a portfolio can help limit\n your exposure to risk and loss in the event that one of the investments\n loses value or cannot be sold quickly enough.\n\n4.5 CREDIT RISKS\n Once goods are sold on credit, risks arising in realising the sale\n proceeds are referred to as credit risks. Risk may arise due to inability\n of the buyers to pay on the due date. Alternatively, even if the buyer\n makes the payment, situations may change in the buyer's country that\n\n 162\n\f could cause his funds not to reach the exporter. An outbreak of war,\n coup or an insurrection may block or delay the payment for goods\n exported. Whatever the reason may be, if funds are not received, it\n becomes a credit risk to the exporter.\n\n Credit risk has assumed an alarming proportion on account of lar"},{"id":1199,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"es a credit risk to the exporter.\n\n Credit risk has assumed an alarming proportion on account of large\n volumes in international business and sweeping changes in political\n and economic conditions, globally. In such a high-risk situation, credit\n risk insurance is of immense help to the exporters as well as banks\n that finance the exporters. Credit risk can be reduced by asking for\n payment in advance where possible ,requesting for letter of credit\n and other means of mitigation which will be discussed under export\n operations.\n\n4.6 COUNTERPARTY RISK/DEFAULT RISK\n This is the risk that a person or institution with whom you have\n entered a financial contract - who is a counterparty to the contract -\n will default on the obligation and fail to fulfil that side of the\n contractual agreement.\n\n Counterparty risk is a risk to both parties and should be considered\n when evaluating a contract."},{"id":1200,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"Counterparty risk is a risk to both parties and should be considered\n when evaluating a contract. Counterparty is the other party that\n participates in a financial transaction, and every transaction must have\n a counterparty in order for the transaction to go through. More\n specifically, every buyer of an asset must be paired up with a seller\n who is willing to sell, and vice versa. Parties may use hedging or take\n out credit insurance in order to mitigate the effect of counterparty\n risk.\n\n 163\n\fTrade Risks and Risks Assessment in International Trade\n\n4.7 ADVERSE BUSINESS RISK\n4.7.1 Legal Risks\n These are risks arising out of foreign laws; as every country has its\n own commercial law. So, different laws prevail both in the exporter’s\n and the importer’s countries. Legal proceedings are complex as well\n as expensive. In every relationship, however cordial and long-standing\n it may be,"},{"id":1201,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"complex as well\n as expensive. In every relationship, however cordial and long-standing\n it may be, differences are likely to arise. Legal risks can be avoided to\n a great extent by incorporating the provision for appointment of an\n arbitrator, in case of dispute about contractual terms.\n\n4.7.2 Exchange Rate Risk/Foreign Exchange Risk\n Foreign exchange risk refers to the potential for loss from exposure to\n foreign exchange rate fluctuations. It is also known as currency risk,\n FX risk or exchange rate risk. When a domestic currency appreciates\n against a foreign currency, profit or returns earned in the foreign\n country will decrease after being exchanged back to the domestic\n currency.\n\n Foreign exchange rate risk is the risk that an asset or investment\n denominated in a foreign currency may lose its value as a result of\n unfavourable exchange rate fluctuations between the investment’s"},{"id":1202,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"may lose its value as a result of\n unfavourable exchange rate fluctuations between the investment’s\n foreign currency and the investor holder’s domestic currency.\n\n In international trade, if the exporter has invoiced in the buyer's\n currency, he will be subjected to risk of foreign exchange fluctuations.\n If the foreign currency depreciates in terms of his local currency, the\n exporter will receive a lesser amount in terms of local currency or vice\n versa. In the same way, if the local currency depreciates, the exporter\n stands to gain.\n\n It is crucial for businesses to have an appropriate exchange policy in\n place. This will help in:\n • Stabilising profit margins over sales made;\n • Mitigating the negative impact of fluctuating rates on sales and\n procurements;\n • Enhancing cash flow control; and\n\n 164\n\f • Simplifying domestic and foreign pricing.\n\n Businesses need to identify foreign"},{"id":1203,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ntrol; and\n\n 164\n\f • Simplifying domestic and foreign pricing.\n\n Businesses need to identify foreign exchange risks to frame an\n effective policy. It is also essential to recognise the tools available for\n hedging these risks and carry out a comparative analysis on a regular\n basis for selecting the best tool available. Exchange rate risk can have\n severe financial consequences like transaction, translation and\n economic risks.\n\n4.7.3 Interest Rate Risk\n Interest rate risk is the risk borne by an interest-bearing asset such\n as a floating rate loan. Interest rate risks affect the value of bonds\n more directly than stocks, and it is a major risk to all bondholders. As\n interest rates rise, bond prices fall, and vice versa. If financing is\n needed, fixed interest rate loan or interest rate swap agreements\n should be entered into to mitigate interest rate risk.\n\n4.8 POLITICAL RISKS\n These r"},{"id":1204,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"wap agreements\n should be entered into to mitigate interest rate risk.\n\n4.8 POLITICAL RISKS\n These risks arise due to changes in political situations in the concerned\n importing and exporting countries. The following are factors affecting\n the political situation:\n (i) Changes in the party in power in the concerned countries,\n followed by change in head of government;\n (ii) Coups, war, revolution or civil disturbances in the buyer’s or\n seller’s countries;\n (iii) Wars between the countries or among many countries;\n (iv) Capture of cargo by enemies during war;\n (v) Imposition of restrictions in the buyer's country by the\n government for remittance of sale proceeds which may block\n or delay the payment to the exporter;\n (vi) New import restrictions in the buyer's country or cancellation\n of valid import licence, after the date of shipment or contract,\n as applicable; and\n (vii) Cancellatio"},{"id":1205,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"valid import licence, after the date of shipment or contract,\n as applicable; and\n (vii) Cancellation of valid export licence or imposition of new\n licensing restrictions after the date of contract, applicable\n under Contracts’ Policy.\n\n 165\n\fTrade Risks and Risks Assessment in International Trade\n\n Political risks can be avoided, to a certain extent, by judicious selection\n of the countries to which goods are exported. Insurance companies\n may agree to provide cover for some of these risks, by collecting\n additional premium. Export Credit Guarantee Corporations also cover\n these types of risks.\n\nSUMMARY AND CONCLUSION\nThis chapter analysed the risk of international trade and various ways of\nreducing the negative effects. International trade exposes exporters and\nimporters to substantial risk, especially when the trading partner is far away\nor in a country where contracts are hard to enf"},{"id":1206,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"isk, especially when the trading partner is far away\nor in a country where contracts are hard to enforce. Firms can mitigate these\nrisks through specialised trade finance products offered by financial\nintermediaries.\n\nRegulatory changes, too, have affected international business or trade.\nSeveral financial institutions reduce their trade finance activities or exit the\nbusiness. Other banks have started scrutinising trade guarantees, given the\ncontinuing strong demand for such services.\n\n 166\n\f REVISION QUESTIONS AND ANSWERS\n\nQuestion 1\nIt is generally said that risks abound in all human endeavours; sudden\nchanges in market value are therefore an important consideration in\ninternational investment. Changes in foreign currency and exchange rates\nwill affect all international investments, and there are other special risks to\nbe considered before deciding whether to invest.\n\n(a) What are the"},{"id":1207,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"there are other special risks to\nbe considered before deciding whether to invest.\n\n(a) What are the special risks in international investments?\n(b) What are the different ways to invest internationally?\n(c) Itemise the sources of information available to an international\n investor.\nAnswer\n(a) Special Risks in International Investments\n Although risks are taken when investments are held in any stock,\n international investments have some special risks such as:\n • Changes in currency exchange rate - these can bring about\n increase or decrease in investment returns;\n • Dramatic change in market value;\n • Political, economic and social events;\n • Lack of liquidity;\n • Less information;\n • Reliance on foreign legal remedies; and\n • Different market operations.\n(b) Different Ways to Invest Internationally\n These are divided into:\n • Mutual funds;\n • Global funds;\n • International funds;\n • Reg"},{"id":1208,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"nationally\n These are divided into:\n • Mutual funds;\n • Global funds;\n • International funds;\n • Regional or country funds; and\n • International index funds.\n • American Depository Receipts:\n The stock of most foreign companies that trade in the US\n market are traded in US American Depository Receipts\n (ADRs) issued by US depository banks.\n\n 167\n\fTrade Risks and Risks Assessment in International Trade\n\n Each ADR represents one or more shares of a foreign stock\n or a fraction of a share;\n • US-traded foreign stocks.\n\n(c) Sources of Information Available to an International Investor\n • Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) reports;\n • Mutual fund firms;\n • The company;\n • Broker-Dealers;\n • Publications; and\n • Electronic information.\n\nQuestion 2\nIn spite of the tremendous benefits accruing to countries from international\ntrade, many countries still impose artificial restrictions on the"},{"id":1209,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ng to countries from international\ntrade, many countries still impose artificial restrictions on the free flow of\ntrade.\nRequired:\n(a) Why do you think some countries impose restrictions on free flow of\n international trade?\n(b) What are the methods employed by countries to achieve the objective\n of effective restrictions in international trade?\n(c) How do you mitigate the risk associated with international trade?\nAnswer\n(a) Why Countries Impose Restrictions\n i. To avoid dumping of goods by other nations;\n ii. To correct balance of payments problem;\n iii. To encourage and protect infant industries at home;\n iv. To reduce outflow of foreign exchange for importation of non-\n essential goods;\n v. As a form of retaliation if trading partners become unfair in\n their dealings;\n vi. To encourage self-reliance;\n vii. To check citizens' penchant for consumption of imported\n goods;\n viii. To conse"},{"id":1210,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"self-reliance;\n vii. To check citizens' penchant for consumption of imported\n goods;\n viii. To conserve foreign exchange for more productive use;\n and\n\n 168\n\f ix. To boost employment at home.\n\n(b) Methods Used to Restrict Trade\n i. Use of exchange control;\n ii. Subsidy to local manufacturers for export;\n iii. Increase in tariff on imported goods;\n iv. Use of import licence to restrict quantity of import;\n v. Devaluation of home currency to encourage export and\n discourage import;\n vi. Discriminatory tariff structure, which may be skewed against\n importation from a certain zone for some imports.\n\n(c) Mitigating the Risk of International Trade\n i. Obtain status report.\n ii. Deal only with rated entities.\n iii. Exporter could seek export insurance with NEXIM Bank.\n iv. Exporter should insist on confirmed irrevocable letters of credit.\n v. Importer should insist on open account trading or bi"},{"id":1211,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"on confirmed irrevocable letters of credit.\n v. Importer should insist on open account trading or bills for\n collection.\n vi. Obtain information on trade practices and rules in overseas\n countries.\n vii. Seek the services of experts.\n viii. Importers to insist on documents issued by an independent and\n reputable third party.\n ix. Ensure the use of reputable bank as intermediary.\n x. Exposures in foreign exchanges could be hedged using\n appropriate hedging techniques such as:\n • Forward exchange contract;\n • Domiciliary account; or\n • Currency netting.\n xi. Deal only with reputable countries to reduce country risk.\n\n 169\n\fTrade Risks and Risks Assessment in International Trade\n\n MULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTIONS (MCQs) AND ANSWERS\n1. The risk of exchange in interest earned at the time a financial contract\n rate is reset is known as:\n (a) Reporting Risk.\n (b) Interest Rate Risk.\n (c) Reposition Ri"},{"id":1212,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ontract\n rate is reset is known as:\n (a) Reporting Risk.\n (b) Interest Rate Risk.\n (c) Reposition Risk.\n (d) Re-pricing Risk.\n\n2. Currency risk is best managed by establishing:\n (a) Arbitrage process.\n (b) Open-position limit.\n (c) Closed-position limit.\n (d) Exchange rate forecasting.\n\n3. The exchange risk exposure that a multinational corporation (MNC)\n can encounter in the process of consolidating its balance sheet at the\n end of its accounting year is known as:\n (a) Economic exposure.\n (b) Transaction exposure.\n (c) Strategic exposure.\n (d) Translation exposure.\n\n4. Counterparty risk is also known as ……\n (a) Default risk.\n (b) Liquidity risk.\n (c) Credit risk.\n (d) Interest rate risk.\n\n5. The possibility that one of the parties under a financial contract would\n default in meeting its obligations is known as:\n (a) Credit risk.\n (b) Exchange rate risk.\n (c) Counterparty risk.\n (d) Inte"},{"id":1213,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"bligations is known as:\n (a) Credit risk.\n (b) Exchange rate risk.\n (c) Counterparty risk.\n (d) Interest rate risk.\n\n 170\n\f8. A risk that exists when a financial transaction is denominated in a\n currency other than that of the base currency of the company is\n known as the risk of loss of and from a:\n a. Accounting risk.\n b. Economic risk.\n c. Exchange rate risk.\n d. Liquidity risk.\n\n7. The risk that arises for bond owners from fluctuating interest rate is:\n a. Exchange rate risk.\n b. Interest rate risk.\n c. Default risk.\n d. Country risk.\n\n8. The risk of loss of principal or loss of financial reward from a\n borrower’s failure to repay a loan is known as …………\n\n9. All but one of the following are steps for success in mitigating\n transport risk:\n a. Contract.\n b. Research.\n c. Source.\n d. Deploy.\n10. Political risk could become more of a factor as the time horizon of an\n investment gets lon"},{"id":1214,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"loy.\n10. Political risk could become more of a factor as the time horizon of an\n investment gets longer. True/False?\nAnswer\n1. d\n2. c\n3. d\n4. a\n5. c\n6. c\n7. b\n8. Credit risk\n9. d\n10. True\n\n 171\n\fTrade Risks and Risks Assessment in International Trade\n\n REFERENCES\n\nBluhm, C., Overbeck, L., & Wagner, C. (2002). An Introduction to Credit Risk\n Modelling. Chapman & Hall /CRC. ISBN 978-1-58488-326-5.\n\nCulpi, C. (2001). The Risk Management Process. Wiley Finance. ISBN 978-0-\n 471-40554-2.\n\nEun, S.C. & Resnick, B.G. (2009). International Financial Management (4th\n Edition). McGraw-Hill.\n\nHolton, A.(2013). “Value-at-Risk: Theory and Practice, (2nd Edition).\n Retrieved July 2, 2013.\n\nHachmeister, A. (2007). Informed Traders as Liquidity Providers. DUV. ISBN\n 978-3-8350-0755-0.\n\nHomaifai, G. A. (2004). Managing Global Financial and Foreign Exchange\n Risk. Hoboken, NJ: John Wiley & Sons. ISBN 978-0"},{"id":1215,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"4). Managing Global Financial and Foreign Exchange\n Risk. Hoboken, NJ: John Wiley & Sons. ISBN 978-0-471-28115-3.\n\nKnight, L. & Satchell, S. (2003). Forecasting Volatility in the Financial\n Markets. Butterworth – Heinemann. ISBN 978-0-7506-5515-6\n\nLevi, M. D. (2005). International Finance, (4th Edition). New York, Routledge.\n ISBN 978-0-415-3090-4. Liquidity Risk Management (pdf). Nigeria\n Deposit Insurance Corporation. 2008. Retrieved August 8, 2012.\n\n 172\n\f Trade Terms and\n Documentation\n\nLearning Outcomes:\nAt the end of this Chapter, the reader should be able to understand the\nvarious trade terms and documentation in international trade;\n\n• International Commercial Terms of (ICC) – Incoterms; and\n• Trade Documentation in International Trade.\n\n5.1 INTRODUCTION\n The problem in international trade is that different countries have\n varying interpretations of the same contract wording; and"},{"id":1216,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"al trade is that different countries have\n varying interpretations of the same contract wording; and this\n problem can be solved only by creating a set of internationally agreed\n terms. The purpose of incoterms is to provide a set of standardised\n terms which mean exactly the same to all parties in trade countries.\n Trade documents are required for record purposes and to support\n business transactions based on international standards. The trade\n documents which are used in international trade may be grouped into\n commercial documents, official documents, financial documents and\n insurance documents.\n\n5.2 INCOTERMS OR SHIPMENT TERMS\n International Commercial Terms (Incoterms) are published by the\n International Chambers of Commerce (ICC) outlining the obligations\n of the exporter and responsibilities of the importer. By process of\n elimination, any obligation that does not appear in a par"},{"id":1217,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"sibilities of the importer. By process of\n elimination, any obligation that does not appear in a particular\n incoterm must be the responsibility of the importer.\n\n The use of incoterms is not mandatory; but where it is agreed to be\n used by parties, they must be incorporated by specific reference in\n the sales contract in the accompanying commercial document terms.\n\fTrade Terms and Documentation\n\n What Incoterms Are\n Incoterms are a set of three-letter standard trade terms most\n commonly used in international contracts for the sale of goods. First\n published in 1936, Incoterms provide internationally accepted\n definitions and rules of interpretation for most common commercial\n terms.\n\n What Incoterms Do\n Incoterms inform the sales contract by defining the respective\n obligations, costs and risks involved in the delivery of goods from the\n seller to the buyer.\n\n What Incoterms Do Not Do"},{"id":1218,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"risks involved in the delivery of goods from the\n seller to the buyer.\n\n What Incoterms Do Not Do\n Incoterms, by themselves, do not:\n - Constitute a contract;\n - Supersede the law governing the contract;\n - Define where title transfers; nor\n - Address the price payable, currency or credit terms.\n\n These items are defined by the express terms in the sales contract\n and by the governing law.\n\n The Incoterms 2020\n Before considering shipping merchandise internationally, an important\n question must be answered: At which point are the risks and charges\n transferred to the buyer?\n\n In 1936, for the first time, the International Chamber of Commerce\n (ICC), published under the name of Incoterms 1936 (INternational\n COmmercial TERMS), a series of international rules that answers this\n question.\n\n In order to adapt these rules into the most recent international trade\n practices, many amendments h"},{"id":1219,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"In order to adapt these rules into the most recent international trade\n practices, many amendments have been added to the rules of 1936\n\n 174\n\faccomplishing what today is called \"Incoterms 2020\" which has\nreplaced Incoterms 2010.\n\nThe main changes made by Incoterms 2020 to Incoterms 2010\nconcern the following points:\n• Evolution of the Incoterm FCA (bills of lading with an on-board\n notation);\n• Appearance of a new Incoterm: DPU (Delivered at Place Unloaded)\n to replace the Incoterm DAT;\n• Differentiated levels of insurance coverage between CIF and CIP;\n• Organisation of transport with the seller's or buyer's own means\n of transport in FCA, DAP, DPU and DDP.\n\nManaging Risk Control\nBy making references in their contracts, using one of the Incoterms\nof the ICC, the buyer and the seller reduce the uncertain risks inherent\nin all international transactions: commercial practices and different"},{"id":1220,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"e the uncertain risks inherent\nin all international transactions: commercial practices and different\ninterpretations from one country to the other. They specify their own\nrespective responsibilities and obligations during the process of\ndelivering the merchandise and the mandatory documentation that\nthe vendor must supply.\n\nAlso, even if they are optional, the Incoterms are recognised as\nstandardised clauses which will prevent any litigation by clearly\ndistributing between the buyer and the vendor: obligations, risks,\ncosts. In addition, they dissociate the question of transferring risks\nfrom those of transferring ownership; this last issue remains under\nthe control of the law that rules the contract.\n\nConcretely, Incoterms will clarify the following points:\n1. Place the critical point of transferring the risks from the vendor\n to the buyer during the process of shipping the goods (loss,"},{"id":1221,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"transferring the risks from the vendor\n to the buyer during the process of shipping the goods (loss,\n damage or theft of the merchandise) allowing the one who is\n liable for these risks to make his own dispositions, notably in\n terms of insurance;\n\n 175\n\fTrade Terms and Documentation\n\n 2. Indicate which one, either the seller or the buyer, must\n underwrite the shipping contract;\n 3. Distribute among the two parties the logistic and administrative\n charges during the different stages of the process;\n 4. Specify who takes care of the packaging, labelling, handling\n operations, loading and unloading of goods or stuffing and\n stripping of containers as well as inspection procedures;\n 5. Set up the individual obligations of each party in the process of\n accomplishing export and/or import formalities, legal\n regulations and duty taxes as well as providing all the\n documents required.\n There ar"},{"id":1222,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ities, legal\n regulations and duty taxes as well as providing all the\n documents required.\n There are 11 Incoterms being kept by the ICC.\n\n How to use the Incoterms 2020\n\n 1. Clarify this concept in the contract of sale\n In order to use Incoterms, this must be clearly stated in the\n contract of sale by indicating: the Incoterms rule chosen, the\n port, designated place or location, followed by \"Incoterms\n 2020\". Example: CIF Hong Kong Incoterms 2020.\n\n 2. Choose the appropriate Incoterms rule\n The choice of the Incoterm is an integral part of a commercial\n transaction. It has to be done in line with the organisational\n capacities of the enterprise, the type of transportation used,\n the level of service that the enterprise wishes to provide to the\n client or the resources of its supplier; or it could be in\n agreement with the common practices of the market, or the\n practices used by the co"},{"id":1223,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"it could be in\n agreement with the common practices of the market, or the\n practices used by the competitors, etc.\n\n The Incoterm selected must also be well-adapted to the type\n of goods that will be shipped and the type of transportation\n that will be used.\n\n 176\n\f3. Specify the place and port with precision\n For an optimal application of Incoterms, the contract parties\n are required to assign a place or a port with maximum\n exactitude: ex FCA 25 rue Saint Charles, Bordeaux, France,\n Incoterms 2020.\n\n It must be stressed in this part that, for certain Incoterms such\n as CPT, CIP, CFR, CIF, the place designated is not the same as\n the place of delivery: it designates the destination paid for.\n\n In order to specify the final destination of the goods, it is\n important to mention the specific address in order to avoid any\n ambiguity.\n\n4. Other precautions to be taken\n Some precautions must"},{"id":1224,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"c address in order to avoid any\n ambiguity.\n\n4. Other precautions to be taken\n Some precautions must be taken when using Incoterms, such\n as:\n\n • A good knowledge of the meaning of each Incoterm and\n its acronym;\n • The usage of the variants of Incoterms with exactitude\n in order to prevent confusion that could result from a\n misinterpretation (ex: FOB USA).\n\n The Incoterms are standards accepted worldwide. In that\n capacity, like all standards (industry, quality, pollution), their\n names do not cause any divergence. Only the standardised\n abbreviations are used. Any other code will be prohibited, as\n any standard; they are an explicit reference. The three letters\n of the Incoterm must be followed by the specific names of the\n designated places and the mention of \"Incoterm ICC\".\n\n 177\n\fTrade Terms and Documentation\n\n Table 5.1: Incoterms 2020 Applicable to All Modes of\n Transport\n Code F"},{"id":1225,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"de Terms and Documentation\n\n Table 5.1: Incoterms 2020 Applicable to All Modes of\n Transport\n Code Full Name\n EXW EX Works\n FCA Free Carrier\n *Possibility to add the on-board notation\n CPT Carriage Paid To\n CIP Carriage and Insurance Paid to\n *Integration of Institute Cargo Clause A\n of the Institute Cargo Clauses, including\n \"All Risks\" insurance coverage.\n DAP Delivered at Place\n DPU* Delivered at Place Unloaded\n DDP Delivered Duty Paid\n * New for Incoterms 2020\n\n Table 5.2: Incoterms 2020 Applicable to Maritime and Inland\n Waterway Transport\n Code Full Name\n FAS Free Along Side ship\n FOB Free On Board\n CFR Cost and Freight\n CIF Cost, Insurance, Freight\n *Incorporation of Institute Cargo Clause C,\n including \"Minimum\" insurance coverage.\n *New for Incoterms 2020\n\n Sale on Departure, Sale on Arrival: A Fundamental Difference\n\n Sale on Departure: A sale on departure means that the mercha"},{"id":1226,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"on Arrival: A Fundamental Difference\n\n Sale on Departure: A sale on departure means that the merchandise\n will be shipped at the risk and hazard of the buyer, i.e.:\n • from the moment that the goods are placed at disposal at the\n vendor's premises (EXW) ;\n • from the moment that the goods are handed to the carrier in\n order to be shipped (FCA, FAS, FOB, CFR, CIF, CPT et CIP).\n\n 178\n\f The Incoterms for a sale on departure assign to the buyer (in a more\n or less large amount) the costs and the risks linked to the shipping of\n the merchandise.\n\n Sale on Arrival: A sale on arrival means that the merchandise will\n be shipped at the risk and hazard of the seller until it reaches the\n designated destination point or port. Three Incoterms are provided:\n • until the end of its maritime transportation and its\n disembarkation (DAP);\n • until its destination point (DPU, DDP).\n\n Who Bears the Variou"},{"id":1227,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ion and its\n disembarkation (DAP);\n • until its destination point (DPU, DDP).\n\n Who Bears the Various Costs and Risks?\n\nTable 5.3: Distribution of costs between the seller and the buyer\n according to the Incoterm negotiated in the contract.\n\n Incoterms for any mode or modes of Incoterms for sea and\n transport inland waterway transport\n EXW FOB CPT CIP DAP DPU DDP FAS FOB CFR CIF\n Packaging S S S S S S S S S S S**\n Loading B S S S* S S S S S S S**\n from\n warehouse\n Pre-carriage B S*** S S* S S S S S S S**\n\n What Do the Different Incoterms Mean?\n\n EXW (Ex Works)\n Seller: The only responsibility of the seller is to prepare the\n merchandise for the buyer, at his own premises, suitably packed for\n export shipping purposes (in general, the price includes loading the\n merchandise in the pallet).\n\n 179\n\fTrade Terms and Documentation\n\n Buyer: The buyer is responsible for all the charges and risks"},{"id":1228,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"179\n\fTrade Terms and Documentation\n\n Buyer: The buyer is responsible for all the charges and risks involved\n in the shipment of the merchandise from the moment it leaves the\n seller's warehouse until it reaches its destination.\n\n The term EXW represents a minimum obligation for the seller.\n However, if the parties agree that the vendor insures the loading of\n the merchandise at the point of departure \"EXW Loaded\", and make\n the vendor responsible for these risks and charges, they have to\n express this issue in an explicit clause included in the sales contract\n (ex: EXW Lagos loaded, ICC 2020).\n\n The seller is expected to provide for the buyer, at his request and at\n his charge and risks, all the assistance required to obtain an export\n licence and insurance, as well as provide the buyer with all the useful\n information in his possession which will allow him to insure the export\n of his"},{"id":1229,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"with all the useful\n information in his possession which will allow him to insure the export\n of his merchandise in full security.\n\n Variant: « EXW Loaded ». The revised version of Incoterms 2020\n introduced this concept of \"EXW Loaded\" which recognises a\n frequently used practice: the seller takes care and responsibility of\n loading the merchandise into the buyer's vehicle.\n\n Table 5.4: “EXW Loaded Variant Model\n Specifications EXW Charges Risks\n Packaging S S\n Pre-carriage B B\n Export customs B B\n Loading into main carriage (handling) B B\n Main transportation B B\n Transportation insurance B B\n Unloading from main carriage (handling) B B\n Import customs B B\n Post-carriage B B\n\n FCA (Free Carrier)\n\n 180\n\fSeller: If the delivery takes place at the seller’s premises, it is the\nseller who handles the loading of the suitably packaged goods into the\nvehicle provided by the buyer (specify “FCA"},{"id":1230,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"dles the loading of the suitably packaged goods into the\nvehicle provided by the buyer (specify “FCA seller’s premises”). Export\ncustoms’ clearance is the responsibility of the seller.\n\nBuyer: The buyer has chosen the type of transportation and the\ncarrier with whom he has signed a transportation contract and pays\nfor the main transportation (if applicable). The transfer of charges and\nrisks takes place at the moment the carrier picks up the merchandise.\nThe parties must agree upon naming a place where to hand over the\nmerchandise (the carrier’s terminal or the vendor’s premises).\n\nThe seller must, should the case arise, provide for the buyer, at the\nright time, all the assistance needed to obtain all the documents and\ninformation regarding the security requirements for the export and/or\nimport of the merchandise, and/or for its transportation to its final\ndestination. The cost of the do"},{"id":1231,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"mport of the merchandise, and/or for its transportation to its final\ndestination. The cost of the documents furnished and/or the\nassistance given are costs and risks paid by the buyer.\n\nThe Incoterms 2020 rules explicitly allow for the necessary transport\nto be performed under a contract of carriage or to be arranged by\nown means (without the involvement of a carrier acting as a third\nparty).\n\nVariant: \"FCA seller's premises\". This Incoterm was officially added\nto the revised version of Incoterms 2000: it is the responsibility of the\nseller to load the merchandise.\n\nGeographical precision: More than in any of the other Incoterms,\nin FCA, the \"named place\" agreed upon must be precise and indicated\nwith care. FCA (Le Havre) is not enough if the buyer is located in Le\nHavre. Is it FCA (warehouse Le Havre) or FCA (in-transit bulking\nwarehouse X Le Havre) or even FCA (dock No. X at the port o"},{"id":1232,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"use Le Havre) or FCA (in-transit bulking\nwarehouse X Le Havre) or even FCA (dock No. X at the port of Le\nHavre)?\n\n 181\n\fTrade Terms and Documentation\n\n If the delivery is going to be done at a place other than the vendor's\n premises, for example: handing it over at a transportation terminal -\n truck, rail, air, maritime - the vendor will be in charge of transporting\n the merchandise up to this named terminal but he will not be\n responsible for unloading the vehicle. The unloading will be handled\n by the one in charge of receiving the merchandise at the\n transportation terminal. Prefer FCA instead of FOB if the\n transportation is done in containers or by roll-on roll-off ship.\n\n Bill of lading with an on-board notation in FCA sales: When the\n goods are sold FCA, sellers or buyers (or their bank, if a letter of credit\n is involved) may wish to obtain a bill of lading with an on-board\n nota"},{"id":1233,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"bank, if a letter of credit\n is involved) may wish to obtain a bill of lading with an on-board\n notation.\n\n However, delivery under the Incoterm FCA is effective prior to the\n loading of the goods on board a vessel. It is by no means certain that\n the seller can obtain an on-board bill of lading from the carrier.\n According to the transport contract, this carrier is only required and\n entitled to produce a bill of lading when the goods are properly loaded.\n\n To remedy this situation, the Incoterms 2020 have provided an\n additional option. The buyer and seller can agree that the buyer\n should instruct its carrier to provide the seller with the bill of lading\n with the on-board notation after the goods have been loaded, and the\n seller is then obliged to deposit this bill of lading with the buyer,\n typically via banks. The seller will not be liable to the buyer in respect\n of the terms of"},{"id":1234,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"buyer,\n typically via banks. The seller will not be liable to the buyer in respect\n of the terms of the contract of carriage.\n\n Table 5.5: Incoterm FCA Model\n Specifications FCA Charges Risks\n Packaging S S\n Pre-carriage S S\n Export customs S S\n Loading into main carriage (handling) B B\n Main transportation B B\n Transportation insurance B B\n\n 182\n\f Unloading from main carriage (handling) B B\n Import customs B B\n Post-carriage B B\n\nFAS (Free Alongside Ship)\nSeller: The obligations of the seller are henceforth fulfilled when the\nmerchandise is placed, after customs clearance, alongside the ship at\nthe dock or at the lading of the designated port of shipment.\n\nBuyer: From this moment on, the buyer is responsible for all charges\nand risks of loss or damage, from the moment that the merchandise\nis delivered alongside the ship, especially in the case of a ship's\nschedule delay or the cancellat"},{"id":1235,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"is delivered alongside the ship, especially in the case of a ship's\nschedule delay or the cancellation of a port of call. The buyer\ndesignates the carrier, arranges the transportation contract and pays\nfor the freight.\n\nObligations of place and moment: The seller does not deliver FAS\nif the vessel is not at the dock. It is a responsibility of time and\nmoment.\n\nLicence acquisition: The acquisition of an export licence or any\nother official authorisation is at the charge and risk of the seller. In\nthe same way, the buyer is responsible for the import licence. The\nbuyer must provide the vendor with all the information regarding the\nname of the vessel, the loading place and the time chosen to deliver\nthe merchandise within the period accorded.\n\nDocuments fees: The seller must, should the case arise, provide for\nthe buyer, at the right time, all the assistance needed to obtain all the\ndocume"},{"id":1236,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"arise, provide for\nthe buyer, at the right time, all the assistance needed to obtain all the\ndocuments and information regarding the security requirements for\nthe export and/or import of the merchandise and/or for its\ntransportation to its final destination. The cost of the documents\nfurnished and/or the assistance given are costs and risks paid by the\nbuyer.\n\n 183\n\fTrade Terms and Documentation\n\n FOB (Free on Board)\n Seller: He has to deliver the merchandise at the designated loading\n port, on board of the vessel chosen by the buyer, and fulfil all the\n formalities of export customs clearance, if there are any.\n\n Under a contract type FOB, the seller fulfils his delivery obligation\n when the merchandise is on board of the vessel at the designated\n loading port; or, in the case of successive sales, the vendor obtains\n the merchandise and delivers it, as well, in order to have it all\n tr"},{"id":1237,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ive sales, the vendor obtains\n the merchandise and delivers it, as well, in order to have it all\n transported up to the designated destination place indicated in the\n sales contract.\n\n Buyer: He selects the vessel, pays the maritime freight, the\n insurance, and he takes care of the formalities at the arrival port. He\n is also responsible for all the charges and risks of loss and damage\n that could arise to the merchandise from the moment it was delivered.\n\n Variant: For information, the \"ARRANGING FOB\" is the term used by\n the freight brokers to indicate that the operations that take place prior\n to placing the merchandise aboard have been done and accomplished,\n as well as the export customs clearance operations, if needed. All\n these operations represent an extra cost, to be paid by the seller,\n which is sometimes called \"fee of placing into FOB\".\n\n The \"FOB STOWED\" and/or \"FOB STOWED"},{"id":1238,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"seller,\n which is sometimes called \"fee of placing into FOB\".\n\n The \"FOB STOWED\" and/or \"FOB STOWED and TRIMMED\" are\n variations. The seller is responsible for the total charges incurred by\n the merchandise at the loading port. However, it has to be stipulated\n in the contract at which point the transfer of risks takes place.\n\n The seller must, should the case arise, provide for the buyer, at the\n right time, all the assistance needed to obtain all the documents and\n information regarding the security requirements for the export and/or\n import of the merchandise and/or for its transportation to its final\n destination. The cost of the documents furnished and/or the\n assistance given are costs and risks paid by the buyer.\n\n 184\n\fCFR (Cost and Freight)\nSeller: He chooses the transportation, contracts and pays for the\nfreight up to the named port of destination; the unloading of the\nmerchand"},{"id":1239,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ontracts and pays for the\nfreight up to the named port of destination; the unloading of the\nmerchandise is not included. The loading of the merchandise after\ncustoms clearance into the vessel is his responsibility as well as the\nshipping formalities.\n\nHowever, the transfer of risk is the same as in FOB.\n\nBuyer: He is responsible for the risk of transportation from the\nmoment the merchandise is delivered alongside the ship at the loading\nport; he receives the carrier and picks up the merchandise delivered\nat the designated destination port.\n\nDocument fees: The seller must, at his own expense, furnish the\nbuyer with a customary transportation document to be used until the\nmerchandise reaches the designated port of destination, covering the\ncontractual merchandise which serves him as a guarantee (ex: claims\nof merchandise to the carrier, sale of merchandise while in transit,\netc.). He also"},{"id":1240,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"tee (ex: claims\nof merchandise to the carrier, sale of merchandise while in transit,\netc.). He also has to provide all the information required in order to\ntake proper measures in receiving the merchandise.\n\nThe information and documents related to the security that the buyer\nneeds in order to export and/or import and/or for the transportation\nof the merchandise until its final destination must be furnished by the\nseller, following the buyer's request, and at his own expense and risks.\n\nCIF (Cost Insurance and Freight)\nSeller: It is a term identical to CFR, but with the supplementary\nobligation for the seller to provide maritime insurance against the risk\nof loss or damage caused to the merchandise. The vendor pays the\ninsurance premium. Under the CIF rule of Incoterms 2020, the seller\nis required to obtain limited insurance coverage in accordance with\nClause C of the Institute Cargo Cla"},{"id":1241,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"required to obtain limited insurance coverage in accordance with\nClause C of the Institute Cargo Clauses or any other similar set of\nclauses. However, the parties are free to agree on a higher level of\ncover.\n\n 185\n\fTrade Terms and Documentation\n\n Buyer: He is responsible for the cost and risk of transportation from\n the moment the merchandise is delivered alongside the ship at the\n loading port. He receives and takes the merchandise from the carrier\n at the named destination port.\n\n The buyers appreciate this Incoterm because they are released from\n logistic formalities.\n\n Document fees: The information and documents related to the\n security that the buyer needs in order to export and/or import, and/or\n for the transportation of the merchandise up to its final destination,\n must be furnished by the seller following the buyer's request and at\n his own expense and risks.\n\n CPT (Carriage P"},{"id":1242,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"hed by the seller following the buyer's request and at\n his own expense and risks.\n\n CPT (Carriage Paid To)\n Seller: The seller controls the logistic chain. After having taken care\n of export customs clearance, he chooses the cargo carrier and pays\n the charges up to the designated place.\n\n Buyer: The risk of damage or loss is borne by the buyer from the\n moment the merchandise is loaded into the first carrier. After that,\n the buyer takes care of the import customs clearance and the\n unloading expenses.\n\n Unloading fees: It is important to clarify the concept of who is\n responsible for the unloading charges into the frame of the\n transportation contract. Normally, the buyer must be responsible for\n these charges unless they are included in the transportation fee. In\n this case, they are charged to the vendor. The vendor must clarify this\n question with the buyer in order to prevent find"},{"id":1243,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"harged to the vendor. The vendor must clarify this\n question with the buyer in order to prevent finding himself in a\n situation where the receiver refuses to pay and the cargo carrier turns\n back to the provider (the seller) to demand his part of the payment\n\n 186\n\ffor the unloading charges as well as the eventual fees for the vehicle's\nimmobilisation while waiting for the problem to be solved.\n\nGeographical precisions: Under the rule CPT, there are transfers\nof risks and charges in different places. It is recommended that the\nparties involved specify clearly in their contract the delivery place\nwhere the risk is transferred to the buyer and the named destination\nup to which the seller is required to arrange a transportation contract.\n\nDocument fees: The information and documents related to security,\nthat the buyer needs for the export/import of merchandise and/or for\nthe transportation"},{"id":1244,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"o security,\nthat the buyer needs for the export/import of merchandise and/or for\nthe transportation up to its final destination, must be provided by the\nseller at the request of the buyer and at its own charge and risks.\n\nCIP (Carriage and Insurance Paid To)\nSeller: CIP is identical to CPT, but the seller must supply, in addition,\na transportation insurance. The seller settles the transportation\ncontract, pays the freight and the insurance premium. \"Under the CIP\nrule of Incoterms 2020, the seller is required to obtain limited\ninsurance coverage in accordance with Clause A of the Institute Cargo\nClauses or any other similar set of clauses.\n\nHowever, the parties are free to agree on a lower level of cover.\n\nBuyer: The risk of damage or loss is borne by the buyer from the\nmoment that the merchandise is loaded into the first carrier. After\nthat, the buyer takes care of the import customs cl"},{"id":1245,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"chandise is loaded into the first carrier. After\nthat, the buyer takes care of the import customs clearance and the\nunloading expenses.\n\nInsurance coverage: According to the term ‘CIP’, the seller is not\nobliged to apply for insurance but for a minimum coverage. If the\nbuyer wishes to protect himself by a superior coverage, under these\ncircumstances, he would need to obtain the agreement of the seller\nor apply on his own for a complementary insurance.\n\n 187\n\fTrade Terms and Documentation\n\n Document fees: The information and documents related to security,\n that the buyer needs for the export/import of merchandise and/or for\n the transportation up to its final destination must be provided by the\n seller at the request of the buyer and at his own charge and risks.\n\n DAP (Delivered at Place)\n Seller: The seller has to deliver the merchandise and place it at the\n buyer's disposal into the inl"},{"id":1246,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"Seller: The seller has to deliver the merchandise and place it at the\n buyer's disposal into the inland freight transportation carrier ready to\n be unloaded at the designated destination. He has to take care of the\n export customs clearance; however, he is under no obligation of\n performing the import customs clearance. The seller must bear a\n contract for the transportation of the merchandise up to the named\n destination and unload it from the transportation carrier at its arrival.\n The seller has no obligation towards the buyer of obtaining an\n insurance contract. Nevertheless, he must provide the buyer, at his\n own expense, the documents that will allow him to pick up the\n merchandise delivered. The Incoterms 2020 rules explicitly allow for\n the necessary transport to be performed under a contract of carriage\n or to be arranged by own means (without the involvement of a carrier\n actin"},{"id":1247,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"a contract of carriage\n or to be arranged by own means (without the involvement of a carrier\n acting as a third party).\n\n Buyer: He has to pay the price of the merchandise, as stipulated in\n the sales contract, and he has to pick up the merchandise once it has\n been delivered.\n\n Security: The buyer must request from the seller to furnish him with\n all the information required in relation to the security which he will\n need for the export, import and transportation of the merchandise\n until its final destination. This new rule replaces the DDU. It is advised\n to use it only in the countries where the means of transportation to a\n destination are under good control.\n\n 188\n\fDPU (Delivered at Place Unloaded)\nThe Incoterm DPU replaces DAT (Delivered At Terminal) to underline\nthe fact that the destination is not necessarily a \"terminal\". However,\nif the destination is not a terminal, the sell"},{"id":1248,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"destination is not necessarily a \"terminal\". However,\nif the destination is not a terminal, the seller must ensure that he is\nable to unload the goods at the place where he intends to deliver\nthem.\n\nSeller: He must deliver the goods by making them available to the\nbuyer at the agreed destination, on the agreed date or within the\nagreed period. The seller must conclude a contract for the transport\nof goods to that place at his own expense and unload the goods from\nthe arriving means of transport. The seller is not obliged vis-à-vis the\nbuyer to conclude an insurance contract.\n\nHowever, he must provide the buyer, at his own expense, with the\ndocument enabling him to take delivery of the goods. The Incoterm\nDPU obliges the seller to clear the goods for export.\n\nHowever, he has no obligation to carry out customs clearance on\nimport. The Incoterms 2020 rules explicitly allow for the necessary"},{"id":1249,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"o carry out customs clearance on\nimport. The Incoterms 2020 rules explicitly allow for the necessary\ntransport to be performed under a contract of carriage or to be\narranged by own means (without the involvement of a carrier acting\nas a third party).\n\nBuyer: He must take delivery of the goods as soon as they have been\ndelivered and pay the price as stipulated in the sales contract. In\naddition, the buyer must advise the seller of the need to provide him\nwith any safety information he may require for the export, import and\ntransport of the goods to their final destination. This Incoterms rule\nwas created specifically for containerised transport. It is also suitable\nfor conventional maritime transport when the seller wants to retain\nthe risks of unloading the ship at the port of destination. In this case,\nit is advisable to specify the place of availability (quay, under hoist,\netc.).\n\n 189"},{"id":1250,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"In this case,\nit is advisable to specify the place of availability (quay, under hoist,\netc.).\n\n 189\n\fTrade Terms and Documentation\n\n DDP (Delivered Duty Paid)\n Seller: The seller has, in this case, the maximum obligation; he is\n responsible for all transfer charges and risks until the merchandise is\n delivered to the buyer. The import customs clearance is also under his\n charge.\n\n The Incoterms 2020 rules explicitly allow for the necessary transport\n to be performed under a contract of carriage or to be arranged by\n own means (without the involvement of a carrier acting as a third\n party).\n\n Buyer: The buyer picks up the delivery at the designated destination\n and pays the unloading fees. He must request from the seller to\n furnish him with all the information required in relation to the security\n which he will need for the export, import and transportation of the\n merchandise until its"},{"id":1251,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"security\n which he will need for the export, import and transportation of the\n merchandise until its final destination.\n\n DDP versus EXW: The term DDP is exactly the opposite of EXW.\n\n Charges relating to the importation of merchandise: If the\n parties wish to exclude from the seller's obligations the payment of\n particular fees payable, by reason of imports of the merchandise, it\n must specify. For example: \"Delivered Duty Paid, VAT unpaid (DDP,\n VAT unpaid)\".\n\n Which law governs the contracts?\n The three possibilities: The two parties are placed in front of three\n possibilities:\n • To keep the law of the exporting country: That would be\n very often the wish of the seller who would prefer to apply his\n own law, considering that it is the one that he knows well.\n However, it is not always the best solution. As a matter of fact,\n some laws, such as the French and Belgian laws, protect muc"},{"id":1252,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"the best solution. As a matter of fact,\n some laws, such as the French and Belgian laws, protect much\n more the buyer;\n\n 190\n\f • To keep the law of the importing country: This law can\n be more interesting for the exporter since it is less constraining;\n however, it is important to know it and master it well because\n it could be very dangerous to be subject to regulations that are\n totally or partially unknown.\n • To keep the law of a third country: This choice allows\n neutralising the legal nationalism. It is often used in\n commercial interests for compromising or for convenient\n reasons (in the case where the court of competent jurisdiction\n belongs to this third country).\n\n5.3 INTERNATIONAL TRADE DOCUMENTS\n These are documents in which the layout, presentation as well as\n applicable trade codes are recorded. They support business\n transactions based on international standards and best"},{"id":1253,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"e codes are recorded. They support business\n transactions based on international standards and best business\n practices. Thus, they are documents required in international trade\n that prove that certain events have taken place.\n\n The documents commonly used in international trade may be grouped\n by function into four categories, which are:\n i. Commercial documents;\n ii. Financial documents;\n iii. Insurance documents; and\n iv. Official documents.\n\n5.3.1 Commercial Documents\n These are sometimes called transport documents. They are those\n documents that are used to facilitate the shipment and delivery of\n goods from the exporter to the importer. The underlisted documents\n are classified as commercial/transport documents.\n (a) Invoices;\n (b) Transport documents;\n (c) Certificate of origin;\n (d) Blacklist certificate;\n (e) Third-party inspection certificate;\n (f) Packing list; and\n\n 191\n\fTra"},{"id":1254,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"d) Blacklist certificate;\n (e) Third-party inspection certificate;\n (f) Packing list; and\n\n 191\n\fTrade Terms and Documentation\n\n (g) Certificate of Analysis.\n5.3.1.1 Types of Invoices\n (a) Commercial Invoice: This is a claim for payment for goods\n already shipped. It gives details of the goods, details of the\n payment and delivery terms, and a detailed breakdown of the\n monetary amount due. Usual details on a commercial invoice\n are:\n i. Name and address of the exporter;\n ii. Name and address of the importer;\n iii. Date of invoice;\n iv. Place of issuance;\n v. Details of the invoice;\n vi. Description and price of the goods;\n vii. Total amount payable;\n viii. Terms of trade;\n ix. Terms of payment;\n x. Mode of transport;\n xi. Accompanying documents; and\n xii. Other special term.\n\n (b) Pro Forma Invoice: This is very similar to a commercial\n invoice, except that it will not include any shipp"},{"id":1255,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ma Invoice: This is very similar to a commercial\n invoice, except that it will not include any shipping marks, and\n it will be clearly stamped 'PROFORMA'. It is an invitation to buy,\n which is sent to a potential buyer.\n\n (c) Consular Invoice: This is another form of commercial invoice\n but with the attestation of the authorised commercial embassy\n officials of the importer's country. The consulate is located in\n the exporter's country. The purpose of consular invoice is to\n certify that the exporter is not dumping goods at artificially low\n prices. It is also to provide information that forms the basis of\n the import duty to be paid on the goods.\n\n (d) Legalised Invoice: When a consular invoice is not available,\n the consulate will authenticate the exporter's own invoice.\n\n 192\n\f (e) Certified Invoice: This is a certification as to standard, quality\n of the goods supplied and the reason"},{"id":1256,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ified Invoice: This is a certification as to standard, quality\n of the goods supplied and the reasonableness of the prices. It\n is normally issued by an independent third party in the\n exporter's country.\n\n5.3.1.2 Transport Documents\n These are documents necessary for the shipment of goods from the\n exporter to the importer. The type of transport documents will depend\n on the mode of transportation agreed by the parties.\n\n Transport documents can be any of the following, as stipulated by\n Uniform Customs and Practice (UCP) publications 600.\n (a) Bill of Lading,\n (b) Seaway Bill,\n (c) Airway Bill, or\n (d) Multi-modal transport documents.\n\n (a) Bill of Lading (B/L)\n This is issued as a receipt by the shipping company where\n goods are transported by sea. It is also a document of title\n to the goods. Bill of lading is the most widely used document\n in international trade. It is a transport d"},{"id":1257,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"goods. Bill of lading is the most widely used document\n in international trade. It is a transport document for goods\n shipped by sea. Usual details on a Bill of Lading (B/L) are:\n i. The name of the shipping company.\n ii. The name of the shipper or consignor who is usually the\n exporter or his agent.\n iii. The name and address of consignee (importer). If the\n word 'order' appears, then the shipper must endorse the\n B/L. The name of the carrying vessel and the ports of\n loading and discharge.\n iv. Brief description of the goods, including identification\n marks and numbers.\n v. Number of original bill of lading issued.\n\n 193\n\fTrade Terms and Documentation\n\n vi. The date the shipping company received the goods for\n shipment and/or loaded the goods on board the ship.\n Whether freight costs had been paid or are payable at\n destination. Ship's master or his agent’s signature.\n\n (i) Features o"},{"id":1258,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"d been paid or are payable at\n destination. Ship's master or his agent’s signature.\n\n (i) Features of a Bill of Lading\n • It is an evidence of contract of carriage.\n • It serves as an acknowledgement of receipt of the\n goods.\n • It is a title document.\n • It is a transport document.\n\n (ii) Contents of a Bill of Lading\n • Date of the bill;\n • Name of the shipping company;\n • Name of the exporter;\n • Details of the importer;\n • Details of the goods;\n • Carriage contract terms;\n • Details of the bill;\n • Port of loading and discharge;\n • Name of the carrying vessel;\n • Place of issue; and\n • Whether freight charges have been paid or are payable\n at the destination.\n\n (iii) Types of Bills of Lading\n There are several types of bills of lading, and these include the\n following:\n • Shipped Bill of Lading: Under the Carriage of Goods\n by Sea Act 1924, the shipper can demand that the ship-\n owner"},{"id":1259,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"f Lading: Under the Carriage of Goods\n by Sea Act 1924, the shipper can demand that the ship-\n owner supplies bills of lading proving that the goods\n have been actually shipped. For this reason, most bills\n of lading forms are already printed as shipped bills and\n commence with the wording: \"Shipped in apparent good\n order and condition\". It confirms that the goods are\n\n 194\n\f actually on board the vessel. This is the most\n satisfactory type of receipt, and the shipper prefers such\n a bill as there is no doubt about the goods being on\n board and consequent dispute on this point will not arise\n with the bankers or consignee, thereby facilitating\n earliest financial settlement of the export sale.\n\n• Received Bill of Lading: This arises where the word\n \"shipped\" does not appear on the bill of lading. It merely\n confirms that the goods have been handed over to the\n ship-owner and are in his"},{"id":1260,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"lading. It merely\n confirms that the goods have been handed over to the\n ship-owner and are in his custody. The cargo may be in\n his dock warehouse/transit shed or even inland. The bill\n has therefore not the same meaning as a \"shipped\" bill\n and the buyer under a CIF contract need not accept such\n a bill for ultimate financial settlement through the bank\n unless provision has been made in the contract.\n Forwarding agents will invariably avoid handling\n \"received bills\" for their clients unless special\n circumstances obtain.\n\n• Through Bills of Lading: In many cases, it is\n necessary to employ two or more carriers to get the\n goods to their final destination. The on-carriage may be\n either by a second vessel or by a different form of\n transport (e.g. to destinations in the interior of Canada).\n In such cases, it would be very complicated and more\n expensive if the shipper had to arrange"},{"id":1261,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ada).\n In such cases, it would be very complicated and more\n expensive if the shipper had to arrange on-carriage\n himself by employing an agent at the point of\n transshipment.\n\n• Groupage Bill of Lading: Forwarding agents are\n permitted to \"group\" together particular compatible\n consignments from individual consignors to various\n consignees, situated usually in the same destination\n country/area, and despatch them as one consignment.\n The ship-owner will issue a groupage bill of lading,\n\n 195\n\fTrade Terms and Documentation\n\n whilst the forwarding agent, who cannot hand to his\n principals the shipowners’ bill of lading, will issue to the\n individual shippers a Certificate of Shipment, sometimes\n called \"house bills of lading\". At the destination, another\n agent working in close liaison with the agent forwarding\n the cargo will break-bulk the consignment and distribute\n the goods to the va"},{"id":1262,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"the agent forwarding\n the cargo will break-bulk the consignment and distribute\n the goods to the various consignees. This practice is on\n the increase, usually involving the use of containers and\n particularly evident in the continental trade and deep\n sea container services. It will doubtless increase with\n containerisation development and is ideal to the shipper\n who has small quantities of goods available for export.\n Advantages of groupage include less packing: lower\n insurance premiums; usually quicker transits; less risk of\n damage and pilferage; and lower rates when compared\n with such cargo being despatched as an individual\n parcel/consignment.\n\n • Transshipment Bill of Lading: This type is issued\n usually by shipping companies when there is no direct\n service between two ports, but when the shipowner is\n prepared to tranship the cargo at an intermediate port\n at his expense."},{"id":1263,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ut when the shipowner is\n prepared to tranship the cargo at an intermediate port\n at his expense.\n\n • Clean Bill of Lading: Each bill of lading states \"in\n apparent good order and condition,\" which of course\n refers to the cargo. If this statement is not modified by\n the shipowner, the bill of lading is regarded as \"clean\"\n or \"unclaused\". By issuing clean bills of lading the ship-\n owner admits his full liability of the cargo described in\n the bill under the law and his contract. This type is much\n favoured by banks for financial settlement purposes.\n\n 196\n\f • Claused Bills of Lading: If the shipowner does not\n agree with any of the statements made in the bill of\n lading, he will add a clause to this effect, thereby\n causing the bill of lading to be termed as \"unclean\",\n \"foul\", or \"claused\". There are many recurring types of\n such clauses, including: inadequate packaging;\n \"unprotected"},{"id":1264,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ed\". There are many recurring types of\n such clauses, including: inadequate packaging;\n \"unprotected machinery”; \"second-hand cases”; “wet or\n stained cartons\"; \"damaged crates\"; \"two cartons\n missing\"; etc. The clause \"shipped on deck at owner's\n risk\" may thus be considered to be a clause under this\n heading. This type of bill of lading is usually\n unacceptable to a bank.\n\n Functions of a Bill of Lading\n • It acts as a receipt for the goods from the shipping\n company to the exporter.\n • It is an evidence of the contract of carriage between the\n exporter and the carrier.\n • It is a quasi-negotiable document. Any transferee for\n value who takes possession of an endorsed bill of lading\n obtains a good title to it, provided the transferor has a\n good title in the first place.\n • It acts as a document of title for goods being shipped\n overseas. The goods will only be released against\n produ"},{"id":1265,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"document of title for goods being shipped\n overseas. The goods will only be released against\n production of one of the original bills of lading. Original\n B/L is usually issued in sets of two or three.\n\n(b) Seaway Bills\n i. Also known as ship waybill, it is a transport document\n that can be issued by the shipping company as an\n alternative to bill of lading.\n ii. Issued in the same way as a bill of lading, except that\n they are never issued to order.\n iii. Functions of a sea waybill are similar to those of a bill\n of lading, except that a sea waybill is not negotiable and\n it is not a document of title.\n\n 197\n\fTrade Terms and Documentation\n\n iv. It can be used where the exporter agrees to sell on open\n account terms.\n\n (c) Airway Bills\n • Issued by airlines when goods are sent by air.\n • Have similar features as seaway bills.\n • Serve as receipts of goods received into custody, not as"},{"id":1266,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"Have similar features as seaway bills.\n • Serve as receipts of goods received into custody, not as\n title documents.\n\n (d) Multimodal Transport Documents\n i. Sometimes used where goods will pass through the sea,\n air and land before actual delivery.\n ii. One of the innovations introduced by the Uniform\n Customs and Practice Publication 500.\n iii. Contain all the features of all transport documents\n discussed previously.\n\n5.3.1.3 Certificate of Origin\n (a) Some countries like Nigeria insist on certificate of origin before\n goods are allowed into their country.\n (b) The main purpose is to ensure an attestation of the origin,\n standard and quality of goods by an independent body like a\n chamber of commerce.\n (c) The shipping marks on the certificate of origin should\n correspond with those on other documents such as the invoice\n or bill of lading.\n\n5..1.4 Black List Certificate\n (a) It will"},{"id":1267,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"other documents such as the invoice\n or bill of lading.\n\n5..1.4 Black List Certificate\n (a) It will certify that the goods have no connection with certain\n countries.\n (b) Some countries in the Middle East will insist on such document\n before allowing goods to be imported.\n\n 198\n\f5.3.1.5 Third-Party Inspection Certificate\n This is a certificate issued by an independent third-party resident in\n the exporter's country, stating that the goods conform to a certain\n standard. It is known as clean report of findings.\n\n5.3.1.6 Packing/Weight List\n This is issued by an appointed third-party agent in the exporter's\n country for the purpose of exchange control regulations and must\n meet the specifications of the authorised issuing body.\n\n5.3.1.7 Certificate of Analysis\n A certificate of analysis is required to import food, drugs, cosmetics\n and pesticides from the manufacturer and the country wher"},{"id":1268,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"required to import food, drugs, cosmetics\n and pesticides from the manufacturer and the country where the\n goods were manufactured. It is a certificate that must be issued by\n the accredited representative of the importer in the exporter’s country\n to confirm the main, the technical or chemical soundness of the\n medicine or food-related products being imported into Nigeria. Any\n importation contract must specify this certificate as one of the\n documents to evidence the shipment in accordance with the prevailing\n exchange control requirements in Nigeria. Federal Government\n agencies like the National Agency for Food and Drug Administration\n and Control (NAFDAC) and Standard Organisation of Nigeria (SON)\n have representatives abroad who issue the certificate for a small fee\n after conducting all necessary tests.\n\n5.3.2 Financial Documents\n These serve as evidence of financial commitments o"},{"id":1269,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"all necessary tests.\n\n5.3.2 Financial Documents\n These serve as evidence of financial commitments or undertaking by\n the importer to pay the exporter. Examples are:\n i. Bill of Exchange;\n ii. Foreign Drafts;\n iii. Personal Cheques; and\n iv. Promissory Notes.\n\n5.3.2.1 Bill of Exchange\n A bill of exchange can be defined as an unconditional order in writing,\n addressed by one person (the drawer) to another person (the\n\n 199\n\fTrade Terms and Documentation\n\n drawee), signed by the person giving it (the drawer), requiring the\n person to whom it is addressed to pay on demand, or at a fixed or\n determinable future date, a sum certain in money to (or to the order\n of) a specified person or to the bearer (payee). Bills of exchange are\n often referred to as drafts. A bill of exchange is a document commonly\n used in international trade to pay for goods and services. It is signed\n by the person prom"},{"id":1270,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ommonly\n used in international trade to pay for goods and services. It is signed\n by the person promising to pay and given to the person entitled to\n receive the money. The bill may specify that payment is due on\n demand or at a specific future date. It is not a contract in itself but it\n is often used to fulfil a contract. When it is issued by a bank, it is\n called a bank draft/bill while it is a trade draft/bill if issued by an\n individual.\n\n (a) Types of Bill of Exchange\n i. Sight Bill of Exchange: This is a bill that requires immediate\n payment at sight or on presentation.\n ii. Tenor, Term or Issuance Bill of Exchange: This is a bill\n payable at a determinable future time.\n\n5.3.2.2 Foreign Draft\n This is a bank draft drawn on a financial institution in the country’s\n currency. A foreign draft is generally used to send money to a foreign\n country; thus, it is a banking instrument that"},{"id":1271,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"n draft is generally used to send money to a foreign\n country; thus, it is a banking instrument that facilitates the transfer\n of money originating as currency in one country to the currency of\n another country. A foreign draft minimises currency exchange risk\n and bank routing delays which makes the transfer of money cheaper,\n easier and quicker.\n\n5.3.2.3 Personal Cheque\n This is a writing order directing a bank to pay money. It is a form of\n payment that is drawn from an account but not guaranteed until\n presented and cleared by the bank. Thus, a personal cheque has a\n level of risk for the person accepting it.\n\n 200\n\f5.3.2.4 Promissory Note\n This is a written, signed, unconditional promise to pay a certain\n amount of money on demand at a specified time. It is a legal\n instrument in which one party promises in writing to pay a\n determinate sum of money to the other, either at a fixed o"},{"id":1272,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"h one party promises in writing to pay a\n determinate sum of money to the other, either at a fixed or\n determinable future time or on demand of the payee, under specific\n terms.\n\n5.3.3 Insurance Documents\n These are documents issued by an insurance company or broker and\n used to verify the existence of insurance under specific conditions\n granted to listed individuals. The documents confirm the effective\n date of the policy, type of insurance coverage purchased, type of\n currency and amount of applicable liability.\n\n (a) Insurance Policy;\n (b) Insurance Certificate; and\n (c) Cover Note.\n\n5.3.3.1 Insurance Policy: This is a statutory comprehensive document\n evidencing a contract arrangement on the goods signed by the\n insurance company in order to indemnify parties against possible\n losses that may arise.\n\n It is a document detailing the terms and conditions of a contract of\n insurance. T"},{"id":1273,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"that may arise.\n\n It is a document detailing the terms and conditions of a contract of\n insurance. Thus, it is a formal contract document issued by an\n insurance company to an insured. The document:\n\n i. Puts an indemnity cover into effect;\n ii. Serves as legal evidence of the insurance agreement;\n iii. Sets out the exact terms on which the indemnity cover has\n been provided; and\n iv. States associated information which includes but not limited to\n the following:\n • Specific risk and perils covered,\n • Duration of coverage,\n\n 201\n\fTrade Terms and Documentation\n\n • Amount of premium,\n • Mode of premium payment, and\n • Deductibles, if any.\n\n5.3.3.2 Insurance Certificate: This is a document issued by the insurance\n company to the insured as an evidence of the insurance. The\n certificate is not a substitute for the actual policy, and is normally a\n non-negotiable document which cannot be ass"},{"id":1274,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"t a substitute for the actual policy, and is normally a\n non-negotiable document which cannot be assigned to a third party.\n It is also unacceptable under the terms of letter of credit and in\n making claims. It just shows an abstract of the most important\n provision of the insurance contract. However, under certain\n situations, the insured may assign his/her right under the certificate\n of insurance to a third party, usually the consignee, by endorsing the\n reverse of the certificate. It contains the same details as the policy,\n except that it is an abbreviated version of the provision of the policy.\n\n5.3.3.3 Insurance Cover Note: This is issued by a broker as a temporary\n or interim cover while the insurance contract is being finalised. It is a\n document issued in lieu of insurance certificate, usually where the\n latter is not ready. Under an open policy, the seller takes out a master"},{"id":1275,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"icate, usually where the\n latter is not ready. Under an open policy, the seller takes out a master\n policy (a floating policy) which applies automatically to every\n shipment once proper notification of carrying ship's identity is made\n to the insurer. A copy of the policy is completed by the seller for each\n shipment and serves as an insurance certificate. An open policy is\n generally preferred where there are recurrent shipments because a\n new policy need not be executed for each shipment. Thus, it is an\n insurance that provides blanket cover against loss or damage to all\n goods transported by a specific carrier, or by a specific shipper during\n a defined period. It is sometimes referred to as an open policy.\n\n The usual details on insurance documents are:\n • Name of the insurer;\n • Name of the insured;\n • The sum insured;\n • Description of the risk covered;\n • Place where claims are pa"},{"id":1276,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"of the insured;\n • The sum insured;\n • Description of the risk covered;\n • Place where claims are payable;\n\n 202\n\f • Nature of the insurance document; and\n • Duration of cover.\n\n In accordance with the Exchange Control Regulations in Nigeria,\n insurance relating to imports and exports must be effected here in\n Nigeria.\n\n5.3.4 Official Documents\n These are documents that state some contractual relationship or\n grant some rights and provide information of an official nature as well\n as rules, regulations and guidelines imposed by an authority. They\n consist of:\n • Tax clearance certificate;\n • Foreign exchange market allocation slip;\n • Duly completed Form M;\n • Clean report of inspection;\n • New Custom bills of entry;\n • Certificate of registration with the Pharmaceutical Board of\n Nigeria;\n • Vaccine and inoculation certificate issued by the Federal\n Ministry of Health.\n\n These documents"},{"id":1277,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"• Vaccine and inoculation certificate issued by the Federal\n Ministry of Health.\n\n These documents are required to satisfy exchange control regulations\n in Nigeria.\n\n5.3.4.1 Electronic Form \"M\"(e-Form \"M\")\n This is a form required for the importation of physical goods.\n\n5.3.4.2 Form \"A\"\n This is a form completed by applicants to pay for service\n transactions (invisible trade) such as:\n i. Personal travel and pilgrimage;\n ii. Medical travel;\n iii. Business travel;\n iv. Expatriate travel;\n v. School fees;\n\n 203\n\fTrade Terms and Documentation\n\n vi. Cash gifts;\n vii. Overseas conferences;\n viii. Subscriptions and examination fees, etc.\n\n5.3.4.3 Form NXP\n This is used for commercial exports, including oil exports. It is also\n called exporter’s form.\n\n5.3.4.4 Form NCX\n It is used for non-commercial exports like:\n (a) Gifts and personal effects;\n (b) Trade samples;\n (c) Machinery and equipmen"},{"id":1278,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"rcial exports like:\n (a) Gifts and personal effects;\n (b) Trade samples;\n (c) Machinery and equipment being returned after execution of a\n specific contract;\n (d) Machinery and equipment for replacement;\n (e) Empty containers;\n (f) Items being re-exported.\n\n5.3.4.5 Form TE\n This is used for declaration of export of foreign currency above\n US$10,000 or its equivalent, precious stones, jewellery and works of\n art, including antiquities from Nigeria.\n\n5.3.4.6 Form TM\n This is used for declaration of import of foreign currency above\n US$10,000 or its equivalent, precious stones, jewellery and works of\n art, including antiquities into Nigeria.\n\n Others are:\n (a) Certificate of incorporation;\n (b) Inspection Certificate or Clean Certificate of Inspection (CCI);\n (c) Import Duty Report - This certifies the duty payable on a given\n import transaction;\n (d) Certificate of Registration with Pharma"},{"id":1279,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"tifies the duty payable on a given\n import transaction;\n (d) Certificate of Registration with Pharmaceutical Board of Nigeria\n (for drug importation);\n\n 204\n\f(e) Phytosanitary Certificate issued by the Federal Ministry of\n Agriculture (for importation of plants);\n(f) Firearms licence by the Federal Ministry of Defence (for\n importation of explosives and firearms);\n(g) Vaccines and inoculation certificate issued by the Federal\n Ministry of Health;\n(h) Certificate of Analysis;\n(i) Tally Sheet ‘A’;\n(j) International Veterinary Certificate for animals and animal\n products issued by DVPCS;\n(k) Certificate of Origin (\"CO\") replaced the Combined Certificate\n of Value and Origin( “CCVO”) used under the old FX manual\n effective August 1, 2018;\n(l) Electronic Certificate of Capital Importation (“eCCI”) used for\n the confirmation of inflow of foreign currency or goods;\n(m) Health Certificate for pr"},{"id":1280,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"I”) used for\n the confirmation of inflow of foreign currency or goods;\n(m) Health Certificate for processed and semi-processed food items\n issued by NAFDAC;\n(n) Customs Bill of Entry.\n\nNigeria Import Requirements and Documentation\n\nTo receive clearance for goods imported into Nigeria, traders must\npresent:\n• A Bill of Lading;\n• Commercial Invoice Exit Note;\n• Duly completed Form ‘M’;\n• Packing List;\n• Single Goods Declaration;\n• Product Certificate; and\n• Certificate of Origin.\n\n 205\n\fTrade Terms and Documentation\n\nSUMMARY AND CONCLUSION\nHaving the correct documentation is vital in international trade. Both\nexporters and importers must be familiar with terms of contracts; trade\nterms; trade documents, including insurance, commercial, official and\nfinancial documents; and the consequences of breaking any of the terms and\nconditions.\n\n REVISION QUESTIONS AND ANSWERS\n\nEssay Questions\n\nQuest"},{"id":1281,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"f breaking any of the terms and\nconditions.\n\n REVISION QUESTIONS AND ANSWERS\n\nEssay Questions\n\nQuestion 1\nBanks must examine all relevant documents with reasonable care to\nascertain that they appear on their face value to be in accordance with the\nterms and conditions of the credit-UCP Publication 600.\n\nBanks must verify that the documents received appear to be as listed in the\ncollection order and must immediately advise the party from whom the\ncollection order was received of any document missing - URC Publications\n522.\n\nRequired:\n(i) Name the four major groups/classifications of such documents in\n accordance with the relevant provisions of the Nigerian Exchange\n Control regulations and the articles of the Uniform Rules for Collection\n and the Uniform Customs and Practice.\n(ii) List 2 documents under each group.\n(iii) Briefly explain any four important but common terms and conditions"},{"id":1282,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"cuments under each group.\n(iii) Briefly explain any four important but common terms and conditions\n that can be found on these documents and their relevance to bankers\n and customers in international trade.\n\nAnswer\n(i) Four Major Groups of Documents\n (a) Official documents;\n (b) Commercial/transport documents;\n\n 206\n\f (c) Financial documents; and\n (d) Insurance documents.\n\n(ii) Two Documents under Each Group\n (a) Official Documents\n (i) Packing List; and\n (ii) eForm 'M';\n (b) Commercial/Transport Documents\n (i) Bills of Exchange; and\n (ii) Certificate of Origin.\n (c) Financial Documents\n (i) Bills of Exchange; and\n (ii) Foreign Draft.\n (d) Insurance Documents\n (i) Insurance Certificate; and\n (ii) Insurance Policy.\n\n(iii) Four Important but Common Terms on the Above Documents\n (a) Free on Board (FOB): It means that the invoice price quoted\n by the exporter excludes freight charges, insura"},{"id":1283,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"oard (FOB): It means that the invoice price quoted\n by the exporter excludes freight charges, insurance cost, and\n delivery of goods at the named port of destination, clearing of\n the goods from the port and transporting the goods to the\n importer's warehouse.\n (b) Cost and Freight (C&F): It means the invoice price quoted\n by the exporter includes loading charges and freight to a\n named destination. The importer's responsibilities include the\n following:\n • Arranging for insurance and paying the insurance costs;\n • Arranging for the delivery of the goods at the named\n port of destination;\n • Clearing of the goods from the port, paying port charges\n and other import duties;\n • Transporting the goods to his/her warehouse.\n (c) Cost, Insurance and Freight (CIF): Same as C & F but\n includes insurance costs.\n (d) Franco Domicile: It means the price quoted by the exporter\n\n 207\n\fTrade Terms an"},{"id":1284,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"surance costs.\n (d) Franco Domicile: It means the price quoted by the exporter\n\n 207\n\fTrade Terms and Documentation\n\n includes that of CIF contract plus a further cost of taking\n delivery of the goods at the port of destination, paying port\n charges and all other import duties in the importer’s country,\n and transporting the goods for delivery at the importer’s\n warehouse. The importer is only responsible for taking delivery\n at his warehouse.\n\nQuestion 2\n(a) Distinguish between a Seaway Bill and Bill of Lading.\n(b) State the conditions under which a Seaway Bill may be preferred by a\n merchant to a Bill of Lading.\n(c) State the features of a Seaway Bill in conformity with Article 20 of the\n Uniform Customs and Practice Publication 600.\n\nAnswer\n(a) Attributes of a Bill of Lading\n • It is a title document.\n • It is an evidence of acknowledgment of receipt of goods.\n • It is a transport doc"},{"id":1285,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"title document.\n • It is an evidence of acknowledgment of receipt of goods.\n • It is a transport document.\n • It is an evidence of contract of carriage between the exporter\n and the importer and the shipmaster.\n\n Attributes of a Seaway Bill\n • It is not a negotiable instrument.\n • It is not a title document to the goods. It is not an\n acknowledgement of receipts of goods by the importer.\n\n(b) If the importer is a subsidiary of the exporter (or vice versa)\n • If mutual understanding exists between the parties.\n • If the usage is subject to local regulation of either party.\n • Its use is not as cumbersome and costly as that of bill of lading.\n • If the exporter sends goods on open account.\n\n(c) Features are:\n • The date of the bill;\n • Place of issue;\n • Details of the consignor;\n\n 208\n\f • Details of the goods;\n • Details of the carrying vessel, including the type and date of\n departure;"},{"id":1286,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"etails of the goods;\n • Details of the carrying vessel, including the type and date of\n departure;\n • Carriage contracts term; and\n • Port of embarkation and destination.\n\nQuestion 3\nIncoterms are the necessary technical logjams that obliterate parties' rights\nin international commercial arbitrations.\n(a) Do you agree with this statement and why?\n(b) State your understanding of the relevance of incoterms according to\n the relative provisions of the URC 522.\n(c) State the effects of the following incoterms on parties' duties, liabilities\n and responsibilities in international trade:\n (i) FCA\n (ii) CPT\n (iii) DDP\n (iv) DAP.\n(d) Itemise five incoterms you expect to find on trade documents\n involving merchants located between Mali and Nigeria.\n\nAnswer\n (a) Incoterms are general terms that are usually found on trade\n documents to connote the features and the extent of parties’\n liabilities or"},{"id":1287,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"usually found on trade\n documents to connote the features and the extent of parties’\n liabilities or otherwise. These terms range from common and\n conventional terms and are meant to help merchants located\n in different countries with different trade environments and\n therefore not to obliterate their rights.\n\n (b) Relevance\n (i) The relevance of incoterms is to help merchants; and\n they must be stated on documents.\n (ii) Parties are free to include terms that connote the\n relationship between them.\n (iii) Article 7 (b) of URC 522 states as follows:\n Terms of delivery of documents against:\n\n 209\n\fTrade Terms and Documentation\n\n • Payments and/or acceptance;\n • Other terms and conditions.\n\n (c) (i) FCA (Free Carrier at a named point). Similar to Free on\n Board (FOB).\n Invoice price quoted includes factory price plus\n transportation up to the boarding on ship of goods.\n The importer is res"},{"id":1288,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ncludes factory price plus\n transportation up to the boarding on ship of goods.\n The importer is responsible for Carriage, Insurance and\n Freight (CIF) from the named point.\n (ii) CPT Freight/Carriage paid to a named point is the\n equivalent of cost and freight. It includes FRC plus\n freight but excluding insurance. The importer is\n responsible for insurance from the named point.\n (iii) DDP (Delivered Duty Paid)\n\n Seller: The seller has, in this case, the maximum\n obligation; he is responsible for all transfer charges and\n risks until the merchandise is delivered to the buyer. The\n import customs clearance is also under his charge.\n\n The Incoterms 2020 rules explicitly allow for the\n necessary transport to be performed under a contract of\n carriage or to be arranged by own means (without the\n involvement of a carrier acting as a third party).\n\n Buyer: The buyer picks up the delivery at t"},{"id":1289,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"he\n involvement of a carrier acting as a third party).\n\n Buyer: The buyer picks up the delivery at the\n designated destination and pays the unloading fees. He\n must ask the seller to furnish him with all the\n information required in relation to the security which he\n will need for the export, import and transportation of the\n merchandise until its final destination.\n\n 210\n\f iv) DAP: Delivered at Place (…named place of destination).\n The seller delivers the goods at the buyer’s disposal on\n the arriving means of transport ready for offloading at\n the named destination.\n\n The seller bears all risks involved in bringing the goods\n to the named place. This means delivered at frontier for\n regional trading, and simply that the quoted invoice\n prices include the following: production, packaging,\n freight, carriage and insurance; but the importer is\n responsible for:\n • Delivery at the named po"},{"id":1290,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"freight, carriage and insurance; but the importer is\n responsible for:\n • Delivery at the named port/border;\n • Transportation (local and across border);\n • Insurance (local and border).\n (d) Frontier Trading Incoterms\n EXW\n FOB\n CPT\n CIP\n DAP\n DDP\n DPU.\n\nQuestion 4\n“A bill of lading is an indispensable document in international trade.”\n\nRequired:\n(a) Itemise the characteristics of the Bill of Lading that justifies the above\n statement.\n(b) Give a brief explanation on the following:\n (i) Dirty or clause or foul Bill of Lading.\n (ii) Charter party Bill of Lading.\n (iii) Short form Bill of Lading.\n\n(c) State the requirements of the UCP 600 concerning Bill of Lading.\n\n 211\n\fTrade Terms and Documentation\n\nAnswer\n(a) Bill of Lading is an acknowledgement by the shipmaster that goods\n named are actually on board a named vessel.\n It is the outward evidence of a contract of carriage between the"},{"id":1291,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"actually on board a named vessel.\n It is the outward evidence of a contract of carriage between the\n shipmaster and the shipping company. It is a document of title for the\n goods it describes. It is a negotiable (or quasi-negotiable) instrument.\n(b) (i) Dirty or Clause Bill of Lading: This conveys any statement\n connoting any form of detriment to the goods being carried.\n (ii) Charter Party Bill of Lading: This is a type of Bill of Lading\n that covers shipment by a vessel which has been specifically\n chartered; its routes are subject to the requirements of the\n charterer. Charter party bill of lading is now acceptable but\n subject to the provisions of Articles 25 (a&b) of the UCP 600.\n (iii) Short Form Bill of Lading: This is a bill of lading that does\n not set out in full, conditions under which the goods are being\n carried; but such rules are contained in a separate document\n to which"},{"id":1292,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"er which the goods are being\n carried; but such rules are contained in a separate document\n to which reference is made. Short Form Bill of Lading is\n acceptable provided the credit terms allow it - Article 23 (aiv).\n\n(c) Requirements of UCP 600 as regards Bill of Lading\n (i) Bill of Lading must show the name of the consignee and\n consignor -Article 19aii.\n (ii) Must indicate that the goods have been loaded on board a\n named vessel -Article 19a ii.\n (iii) Must carry the signature or authentication of the carrier or a\n master must be identified as a carrier.\n (iv) Indicate the port of loading and discharge.\n (v) In all other respects, meet the stipulations of the credit.\n (vi) Must appear to contain all the terms and conditions by\n reference to a source document other than the Bill of Lading -\n in case of short form Bill of Lading.\n (vii) Indicate whether transshipment is allowed or not an"},{"id":1293,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"-\n in case of short form Bill of Lading.\n (vii) Indicate whether transshipment is allowed or not and under\n what terms.\n (vii) Indicate that it is a chartered party, where the credit calls for a\n charter party Bill of Lading Article 21a.\n\n 212\n\f (ix) Loading on Board or shipment on a named vessel may be\n indicated by a pre-printed wording -Article 21 aiii.\n (x) Must indicate the number of sets issued, if issued in more than\n one original full set-Article 21.\n (xi) Indicate the latest date of shipment.\n (xii) Indicate the date of loading on Board where the Bill of Lading\n contains the indication \"intended vessel\" or similar qualification\n correlation to the vessel -Article 19.\n\nQuestion 5\nState the documentation requirements for the invisible transactions\naccording to the Central Bank of Nigeria’s foreign exchange requirement.\n a. Business Travel Allowances (BTA).\n b. Conferences, semina"},{"id":1294,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"igeria’s foreign exchange requirement.\n a. Business Travel Allowances (BTA).\n b. Conferences, seminars and training courses.\n c. Royalties.\n d. Management service fees.\n\nAnswer\n (a)\n • Duly completed Form A;\n • Valid international passport;\n • Photocopy of the photo page;\n • Air ticket;\n • Completed questionnaire; and\n • Letter of invitation.\n\n(b) Duly completed Form A\n • A letter of invitation and estimates of expenses incurred by\n the organising body overseas;\n • Passport of the beneficiary;\n • Air ticket; and\n • A letter of authority to travel from the Nigerian organisation\n with the list of the nominees for the conferences/seminar.\n\n 213\n\fTrade Terms and Documentation\n\n(c)\n • Duly completed Form A;\n • Completed agreement;\n • Evidence of payment of tax on royalty; and\n • Audited account for the relevant period.\n\n(d)\n • Duly completed Form A;\n • Copy of management services agreement;"},{"id":1295,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"for the relevant period.\n\n(d)\n • Duly completed Form A;\n • Copy of management services agreement;\n • Cost estimates from the organisation rendering the services;\n • Certificate of satisfactory completion of the job issued by the\n Nigerian employer.\n • Evidence of payment of tax on the amount to be remitted.\n\n MULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTIONS (MCQs) AND ANSWERS\n\n1. Which of the following incoterms excludes seller’s interest on\n insurance payment?\n (a) CIF\n (b) FOB\n (c) FCA\n (d) CFR\n\n2. Which of the following is a document of title?\n (a) Seaway bill\n (b) Linerway bill\n (c) Charter party bill of lading\n (d) Bill of lading (Liner)\n\n3. From a seller’s perspective, the most basic incoterm transaction is:\n (a) Free-on-Board.\n (b) Ex-works.\n (c) Cost and Freight.\n (d) Delivered at terminal.\n\n 214\n\f4. Which of the following will be a requirement under all the different\n INCOTERMS?\n (a) Seller clears re"},{"id":1296,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ich of the following will be a requirement under all the different\n INCOTERMS?\n (a) Seller clears regulatory clearances in their county.\n (b) Seller provides the goods in travel worthy packaging.\n (c) Seller identifies the carrier.\n (d) Seller decides the date of delivery.\n\n5. An exporter sends his goods from Lagos via Apapa to UK. Where does\n the exporter’s responsibility end regarding insurance of goods under\n CFR UK terms?\n (a) Lagos\n (b) Apapa\n (c) UK\n (d) Apapa, as soon as the goods cross the ship’s rail.\n\n6. Exporter is in UK and importer is in Nigeria; which of these Incoterms\n could apply?\n (i) CFR UK (ii) FOB Nigeria (iii) CIF Nigeria.\n (a) Only (i).\n (b) (i) and (iii).\n (c) Only (iii).\n (d) Any of them.\n\n7. The initial invoice that serves as a quotation by the seller to a potential\n buyer is:\n (a) Commercial invoice.\n (b) Pro forma invoice.\n (c) Consular invoice.\n (d) Bidding i"},{"id":1297,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"al\n buyer is:\n (a) Commercial invoice.\n (b) Pro forma invoice.\n (c) Consular invoice.\n (d) Bidding invoice.\n\n8. Form M is used by merchants in Nigeria to:\n (a) Process Personal Travel Allowance.\n (b) Process Visible Imports.\n (c) Process Invisible Export.s\n (d) Process Visible Exports.\n\n 215\n\fTrade Terms and Documentation\n\n9. The seller must deliver the goods to a carrier appointed by the buyer,\n located in the seller’s country and, from there, the buyer arranges\n insurance against damage to the goods in transit. Which Incoterm(s)\n imply(ies) this condition?\n (i) CIF (ii) FOB (iii) FAS (iv) FCA.\n (a) Only (i).\n (b) (i) and (ii).\n (c) (ii), (iii) and (iv).\n (d) All of the above.\n\n10. From the exporter’s point of view, the most favourable incoterm is:\n (a) FCA.\n (b) CPT.\n (c) CIP.\n (d) DAT.\n\nAnswers\n1. b\n2. d\n3. b\n4. b\n5. b\n6. a\n7. b\n8. d\n9. d\n10. b\n\n 216\n\f REFERENCES\n\nCowdell, P. Hyde, D,"},{"id":1298,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"Answers\n1. b\n2. d\n3. b\n4. b\n5. b\n6. a\n7. b\n8. d\n9. d\n10. b\n\n 216\n\f REFERENCES\n\nCowdell, P. Hyde, D, and Watson, A. (2002). Finance of International Trade,\n Financial. World Publishing, UK.\n\nOdekunle, T. (2020). International Trade and Finance, Jaytees Publishers,\n Lagos.\n\nThe International Chamber of Commerce (2020). International Commercial\n Terms 2020.\n\nVarious Examiners’ Reports of The Chartered Institute of Bankers of Nigeria.\n\nEbiz Guide Nigeria-https://books.google.com.ng books.\n\n 217\n\f Trade Financing Facilities\n\nLearning Outcomes:\n\nAt the end of this Chapter, the reader should be able to:\n- Understand different types of financing facilities available to both\n exporters and importers;\n- Understand the features of the various financing facilities;\n- Know the merits and demerits of each of the facilities; and\n- Recommend the appropriate trade financing facilities to both\n exporters"},{"id":1299,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ach of the facilities; and\n- Recommend the appropriate trade financing facilities to both\n exporters and importers.\n\n6.1 INTRODUCTION\n International trade financing is required to carry out international\n trade operations. It is also designed to make it easier for both\n importers/exporters to get the financing they need to make their\n business global.\n Trade finance to both importers and exporters could be pre-shipment\n or post-shipment financing.\n\n6.2 IMPORT FINANCING FACILITIES\n a) Loan or overdraft;\n b) Produce/Merchandise/Hypothecation loan;\n c) Accommodation finance.\n d) Documentary acceptance credit;\n e) Leasing;\n f) Hire purchase.\n\f Explanation of Import Financing Facilities\n\n6.2.1 Loan or Overdraft: This is usually in form of a short- or medium-\n term advance. A written request for loan or overdraft is made by the\n customer to his banker. His credibility is thoroughly assessed by"},{"id":1300,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"loan or overdraft is made by the\n customer to his banker. His credibility is thoroughly assessed by taking\n into consideration the principles of good lending.\n The facility could be in form of secured or unsecured loan or overdraft.\n A limit is approved based on the banker’s overall credit rating.\n Repayment is usually expected from the sales of manufactured goods\n by the importers.\n\n6.2.2 Produce/Merchandise/Hypothecation Loan: This is a special\n loan to the importer which is secured by a charge over the goods for\n which the loan is granted. Steps to be taken by the bank to protect\n the advances are as follows:\n (a) Written request is made by the importer to his banker\n indicating the terms of repayment.\n (b) Approved limit is granted when all lending principles have been\n taken into consideration.\n (c) It will be a condition in the facility that documents of title to\n the imported goo"},{"id":1301,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"sideration.\n (c) It will be a condition in the facility that documents of title to\n the imported goods must pass through the banker for onward\n dispatch to the importer.\n (d) A charge by way of lien on the title documents is created.\n (e) The documents of title will be released to the importer for him\n to take delivery of the imported goods against a trust receipt\n with a letter of pledge. With the trust receipt, the customer\n becomes an agent of the bank and the goods cannot be\n claimed by his trustee in estate in case of his death or\n bankruptcy or liquidation of a corporate customer.\n (f) Where the importer is of undoubted ability with proven\n integrity, the banker may allow him take possession of the\n goods, warehouse, insure and sell while the sales proceeds are\n deposited immediately to liquidate the facility.\n (g) Where the bank is not fully convinced of the importer’s integrity"},{"id":1302,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"to liquidate the facility.\n (g) Where the bank is not fully convinced of the importer’s integrity\n and ability, it will make sure that the goods are warehoused in\n a bonded warehouse, well insured by a reputable insurance\n\n 219\n\fTrade Financing Facilities\n\n company in the bank’s name or with the bank’s interest duly\n noted. The importer will be made to submit insurance\n policy/certificate, warehouse keeper’s receipts and warehouse\n keeper’s warrants.\n (h) Through the submission of the warehouse keeper’s receipt, the\n bank will ensure prompt payment of all warehouse fees and\n charges and the warrants will enable the bank to monitor the\n sales proceeds.\n (i) Buyers will pay directly to the bank account opened specifically\n for the purpose while the bank issues warrants each time sales\n are made until the facility is fully liquidated.\n\n Advantages\n • Simple to arrange.\n • Additional securi"},{"id":1303,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"made until the facility is fully liquidated.\n\n Advantages\n • Simple to arrange.\n • Additional security may not be required for trusted customers.\n • Goods can be easily sold in case of importer’s default, death,\n bankruptcy or liquidation.\n • It may not be as expensive as money market borrowing.\n\n Disadvantages\n • Not suitable for unknown customers.\n • Additional collateral may be required.\n • Suitable for only merchandise transactions.\n\n6.2.3 Accommodation Finance: This is a type of pre-import financing\n which can be used to meet an obligation due under bill of exchange\n accepted under collection or documentary letter of credit. There are\n mainly 2 types of accommodation finance.\n\n i. Bankers’ Acceptance\n (a) This is granted by a bank through a tenor bill of exchange\n to fill the gap in a sight documentary collection.\n It can be offered where a bank is not willing to increase\n its expos"},{"id":1304,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"sight documentary collection.\n It can be offered where a bank is not willing to increase\n its exposure to a customer.\n\n 220\n\f (b) The rate of return is agreed between the bank and the\n investing customers who accept the concept of bankers’\n acceptance when introduced by the bank.\n (c) The borrowing customer draws a bill of exchange on the\n investor for the agreed period.\n (d) The borrowing customer writes a post-dated cheque for\n the bill plus the interest.\n (e) The accepted bill is discounted in the money market or by\n the bank, the proceeds less the discounting charges are\n credited to the importer’s account.\n (f) The bank presents the discounted bill to the investing\n customer where account is debited directly.\n (g) At maturity, the post-dated cheque is presented to the\n importer who pays, and the proceeds are credited to the\n investing customer’s account.\n (h) The discounting bank e"},{"id":1305,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ays, and the proceeds are credited to the\n investing customer’s account.\n (h) The discounting bank earns its commission at discounting,\n while the investor earns the interest for the tenor.\n (i) It would be a direct bank acceptance where the tenor bill\n is drawn on the bank directly and it accepts it, while\n earning both the interest and the commission.\n (j) The liability of the accepting bank to the investor is like a\n guarantee which crystallises only if the importer fails to\n pay at the maturity of the discounted bill.\n\nii. Commercial Papers\n This is a money market instrument used mainly by blue-chip\n companies to raise short-term finance.\n Steps\n (a) A reputable finance company who serves as a broker is\n approached by the importer.\n (b) The finance company raises promissory notes\n denominated in thousands or millions of Naira with rate\n of interest and the maturity date, sold to bank"},{"id":1306,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"minated in thousands or millions of Naira with rate\n of interest and the maturity date, sold to banks, private\n and corporate investors.\n (c) The investors pay the value less the interest.\n\n 221\n\fTrade Financing Facilities\n\n (d) The proceeds are used by the importer to offset financial\n obligations due under the accepted sight bill of\n exchange.\n (e) The importer clears his goods, sells and makes payment\n to the finance company at the maturity of the\n promissory notes.\n (f) At maturity, the commercial papers are presented to the\n finance company for the redemption.\n (g) Where the importer cannot redeem his own obligation,\n the finance company has to pay the investors by\n arranging another paper with similar terms.\n (h) The cost of commercial papers is the investment yield\n on the promissory notes plus charges and fees to the\n finance company.\n\n Advantages of Accommodation Finance\n • Simp"},{"id":1307,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"y notes plus charges and fees to the\n finance company.\n\n Advantages of Accommodation Finance\n • Simple to arrange.\n • Huge sums of money can be raised quickly where the\n importer (company) commands public respect.\n • Requires goodwill rather than tangible collateral.\n • Does not crystallise in the book of the finance house until\n the importer defaults in repayment.\n • More reliable because borrowing is pooled from several\n investors.\n\n Disadvantages\n • Expensive to execute and the shorter tenor may not give\n enough room for the importer to cover costs.\n • Not suitable or open to all importers but to blue-chip\n companies or those with top class-corporate identity.\n\n6.2.4 Documentary Acceptance Credit: If the method of payment\n agreed between the importer and exporter is a documentary letter of\n credit providing for acceptance, a period of credit can be obtained\n from the exporter by seeki"},{"id":1308,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"of\n credit providing for acceptance, a period of credit can be obtained\n from the exporter by seeking an acceptance credit rather than a sight\n credit.\n\n 222\n\f6.2.5 Leasing: An importer, by means of a leasing agreement in his country\n or from a finance house in the exporter’s country, can obtain the use\n of capital assets.\n\n6.2.6 Hire Purchase: An importer may obtain the use of capital assets by\n means of a hire purchase agreement.\n\n6.3 EXPORT FINANCING FACILITIES\n (a) Factoring;\n (b) Forfaiting;\n (c) Leasing;\n (d) Bill discounting;\n (e) Commercial papers;\n (f) Avalising;\n (g) Loan or overdraft;\n (h) Produce/Hypothecation;\n (i) Accommodation finance;\n (j) Hypothecation;\n (k) Acceptance credit facilities;\n (l) Capital market financing;\n (m) Hire purchase;\n (n) Bills negotiation;\n (o) Red-clause L/C;\n (p) Direct credit purchase.\n\n Export Financing Facilities\n6.4 FACTORING\n This is the man"},{"id":1309,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"lause L/C;\n (p) Direct credit purchase.\n\n Export Financing Facilities\n6.4 FACTORING\n This is the management and purchase of book debts as it relates to\n export in the balance sheet of the exporter. It is the selling of trade\n debts for immediate cash by an exporter. It is a contractual agreement\n between the exporter and an export factor that can be the subsidiary\n of a bank. The export factor could offer any of these financial services:\n\n 223\n\fTrade Financing Facilities\n\n - An accounting and debt collection service, which may include\n provision of reliable credit information on foreign buyers.\n A credit insurance service whereby limits might be set for each\n foreign buyer and exporter is allowed to supply goods up to\n these limits and invoices are submitted to the factor. Factor\n buys debts in his own name rather than that of the seller.\n - Provision of short-term finance by advising ca"},{"id":1310,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ts in his own name rather than that of the seller.\n - Provision of short-term finance by advising cash less the factor\n charges.\n - Invoice discounting, whereby the factor agrees to provide\n funds against selected invoices of the exporter at a discount.\n - The discounting fee is usually between 1 and 2 per cent of the\n invoice value and usually also with recourse to the exporter\n while the foreign buyer may not be aware of the involvement\n of an export factor.\n\n Differences between Factoring and Invoice Discounting\n • The factor is not obligated to take over the wholesales ledger\n of the exporter under invoice discounting. The exporter\n selectively discounts some of his trade debts.\n • Invoice discounting may be a \"one-off' offer while factoring\n can be on a regular basis.\n\n Under discounting of book debts, the factor is under a contractual\n obligation to perform at least 2 of the follow"},{"id":1311,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ing of book debts, the factor is under a contractual\n obligation to perform at least 2 of the following functions:\n • Provision of funds;\n • Credit management; or\n • Risk bearing.\n\n Advantages of Factoring to an Exporter\n • Factoring increases the exporter's turnover because it reduces\n the period of his cash operating cycle.\n • There is provision of reliable status enquiry on the buyers,\n thereby eliminating or reducing the possibility of selling to\n unreliable buyers.\n\n 224\n\f • It eliminates the risk of exchange rate fluctuations, especially\n where there is no recourse to the exporter.\n • Capital structure of the exporter may not be affected.\n • It increases efficiency in working capital management.\n\n Disadvantages\n • Factoring costs may be expensive than normal bank facility.\n • It may not be easily arranged for a newcomer into the export\n business.\n • Where it is with recourse to the"},{"id":1312,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"not be easily arranged for a newcomer into the export\n business.\n • Where it is with recourse to the exporter, the risk of bad debt\n may be borne by him.\n • An export factor may be selective of the debts as well as the\n buyer's country.\n\n Steps in Factoring\n a) You perform a service for your customer.\n b) You send your invoice to a factoring company.\n c) You receive a cash advance on your invoice from the factoring\n company.\n d) The factoring company collects full payment from your\n customer.\n e) The factoring company pays you the rest of your invoice\n amount, minus the commission.\n\n6.5 SUPPLY CHAIN FINANCING\n Supply Chain Finance (SCF) refers to the techniques and practices\n used by banks and other financial institutions to manage the capital\n invested into the supply chain and reduce risk for the parties involved.\n\n Whilst some providers of supply chain finance try to dress it up as\n s"},{"id":1313,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"k for the parties involved.\n\n Whilst some providers of supply chain finance try to dress it up as\n something else, supply chain finance is a form of working capital\n finance that provides liquidity in a similar way to an overdraft. The\n main difference is that funds are only used to pay suppliers.\n\n Each financial intervention (financing, risk mitigation or payment) in\n the supply chain is driven by an event or ‘trigger’ in the physical supply\n\n 225\n\fTrade Financing Facilities\n\n chain. Some examples of these events are: Purchase orders, Pre-\n shipment inspections, Despatch/shipment, Invoices raised by the\n seller, Goods accepted by the buyer/entered the buyer’s warehouse.\n\n Supply chain finance is a relatively new way of providing liquidity to\n businesses. But what is it and why do it? How is it different from the\n more traditional approaches of factoring and invoice discounting?\n\n To ac"},{"id":1314,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"is it different from the\n more traditional approaches of factoring and invoice discounting?\n\n To access working capital finance, businesses have traditionally used\n short-term trading assets such as stock or trade receivables as\n security. The most liquid of these assets, from a lender’s perspective,\n is the accounts receivable ledger. They will secure their loan using this\n asset as the primary collateral, or even purchase the complete ledger.\n The lender’s repayment source is the collection of these accounts\n receivable from the end customer (i.e. the debtor).\n\n In broad terms, factoring is transactional, with the lender purchasing\n and then taking over the collection of each receivable. Even\n purchasing just a single invoice is now common via peer-to-peer\n platforms. Invoice discounting, on the other hand, is a more wholesale\n arrangement. This is usually based on selling the complet"},{"id":1315,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"g, on the other hand, is a more wholesale\n arrangement. This is usually based on selling the complete debtor’s\n ledger but allowing the borrower to manage the receivable collection\n themselves.\n\n • Factoring and invoice discounting do work and, perhaps as the\n only show in town, have proved popular. They do however\n have some downsides:\n • The amount of funding available at any point in time can vary\n tremendously. It is dependent on the invoices being funded\n and the lenders’ view on the end customer.\n • Lenders typically earn most of their income through the\n application of fees rather than the interest rate on the funds\n lent. As such, they can have a high fixed cost.\n • They can also be quite invasive for the borrower, with audits,\n reporting and requirements to disclose (non-confidential)\n arrangements to customers.\n\n 226\n\f• For companies with few customer invoices (e.g. retailers),"},{"id":1316,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ial)\n arrangements to customers.\n\n 226\n\f• For companies with few customer invoices (e.g. retailers), or\n those operating in sectors unpopular with lenders, factoring\n and invoice discounting are obviously not an option.\n\nHow does supply chain finance work?\nThere are two broad types of supply chain finance in the market. They\ncan be described as “supplier-initiated” or “buyer-initiated”.\nSupplier-initiated programmes (including reverse factoring) allow\nsuppliers to sign up to the programme and request early payment on\napproved invoices before they are due. The supplier receives the early\npayment from the lender (less the finance fee) and the lender gets\nrepaid by the borrower on the due date.\n\nBuyer-initiated programmes (sometimes called purchase finance or\nsupplier finance) involve the buyer instructing the lender to make\npayments directly to the supplier. These payments can be made earl"},{"id":1317,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"er instructing the lender to make\npayments directly to the supplier. These payments can be made early\nor on the due date. The fee is usually charged to the buyer who repays\nthe lender on the agreed date (may be after the due date).\n\nWhy use supply chain finance?\n• Few companies use supply chain finance for the sole benefit of\n their suppliers or ‘to secure their supply chain’ as it is usually\n put. More common reasons to use supply chain finance include:\n• Where the buyer requires additional working capital to fund\n their growth (or manage seasonal cycles), supply chain finance\n can provide a valuable source of new liquidity, enabling the\n borrower to increase their sales.\n• Some buyers choose to improve their own balance sheet by\n extending supplier payment terms before offering supply chain\n finance.\n• In supply chains where the supplier struggles to access finance\n (or finance is expe"},{"id":1318,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ain\n finance.\n• In supply chains where the supplier struggles to access finance\n (or finance is expensive compared to the buyer), both parties\n can benefit from this credit arbitrage by sharing the savings.\n• Where shortages exist, buyers can gain an advantage by using\n supply chain finance to attract the best suppliers without\n impacting their own working capital.\n\n 227\n\fTrade Financing Facilities\n\n Is reverse factoring supply chain finance?\n Reverse Factoring is a type of supply chain finance, typically practised\n by specialist banks and very large companies. It is called ‘factoring’\n because, in order to avoid this facility being classified as debt on the\n large company’s balance sheet, the bank must actually purchase the\n supplier’s invoice from them. A separate contract between the bank\n and the large borrower covers the repayment obligations which allows\n the lender to offer suppli"},{"id":1319,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"nk\n and the large borrower covers the repayment obligations which allows\n the lender to offer suppliers cheap pricing based on the very high\n credit rating of the borrower once the invoice is approved (hence the\n “reverse” part).\n\n6.6 FORFAITING\n This is the purchase of obligations falling due at some future date and\n arising from delivery of goods and services usually relating to export.\n It is a medium-term financing for the exporter in exchange for bills of\n exchange or promissory notes arising mainly out of goods and services\n exported by him.\n\n Forfaiting is the financial transaction involving the purchase of\n receivables from exporters by a forfaiter. The forfaiter takes on all the\n risks associated with the receivables but earns a margin. The forfaiter\n may also be immunised from certain risks if the transaction involves\n payment by instrument. The forfaiting is a transaction invo"},{"id":1320,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"rtain risks if the transaction involves\n payment by instrument. The forfaiting is a transaction involving the\n sale of one of the firm's transactions. It involves the purchase of future\n payment obligations on a \"without recourse\" basis.\n\n Forfaiting can be applied to a wide range of trade-related and even\n purely financial receivables and payment instruments. Although\n discounted receivables often have maturities over medium terms of 1\n to 3 years, they can be as short as 1 month or as long as 10 years.\n\n Forfaiting is a flexible discounting technique that can be tailored to\n the needs of a wide range of counterparties and domestic and\n international transactions. By purchasing these receivables - which are\n usually guaranteed by the importer's bank - the forfaiter frees the\n\n 228\n\f exporter from credit and from the risk of not receiving payment from\n the importer who purchased the good"},{"id":1321,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ter from credit and from the risk of not receiving payment from\n the importer who purchased the goods on credit. While giving the\n exporter a cash payment, forfaiting allows the importer to buy goods\n for which it cannot immediately pay in full. The receivables, becoming\n a form of debt instrument that can be sold on the secondary market,\n are represented by bills of exchange or promissory notes, which are\n unconditional and easily transferred debt instruments.\n\n Forfaiting is the term generally used to denote the purchase of\n obligations falling due at some future date, arising from deliveries of\n goods and services - mostly export transactions - without recourse to\n any previous holder of the obligation.\n\n The forfaiter will deduct interest [discount] in advance for the whole\n period of credit and disburse the net proceeds immediately. The\n exporter thus virtually converts his credit-b"},{"id":1322,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"credit and disburse the net proceeds immediately. The\n exporter thus virtually converts his credit-based sale into a cash\n transaction. His sole responsibilities are manufacturing and delivery of\n the goods, thus creating a valid payment obligation of the importer.\n\n6.6.1 Characteristics of Forfaiting\n The characteristics of a forfaiting transaction are:\n • 100% financing without recourse to the seller of the debt.\n • The payment obligation is often but not always supported by a\n bank guarantee.\n • The debt is usually evidenced by a legally enforceable and\n transferable payment obligation such as a bill of exchange, a\n promissory note, a letter of credit or a note purchase\n agreement.\n • Transaction values can range from US$100,000 to US$200\n million.\n • Debt instruments are typically denominated in one of the\n world's major currencies, with Euro and US Dollars being the\n most common.\n •"},{"id":1323,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"minated in one of the\n world's major currencies, with Euro and US Dollars being the\n most common.\n • Finance can be arranged on a fixed or floating interest rate\n basis.\n • Credit is extended to the importer for a period ranging between\n\n 229\n\fTrade Financing Facilities\n\n 180 days and seven years.\n • The payment is normally receivable in any major convertible\n currency.\n • A letter of credit or a guarantee is made by a bank, usually in\n the importer's country.\n • The contract can be for either goods or services.\n • At its simplest, the receivables should be evidenced by a\n promissory note, a bill of exchange, a deferred-payment letter\n of credit, or a letter of forfaiting.\n • Discount rate, the interest element, is usually quoted as a\n margin over NIBOR.\n • Days of grace are added to the actual number of days until\n maturity for the purpose of covering the number of days\n normally experi"},{"id":1324,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ctual number of days until\n maturity for the purpose of covering the number of days\n normally experienced in the transfer of payment, applicable to\n the country of risk.\n • Commitment fee is applied from the date the forfaiter is\n committed to undertake the financing, until the date of\n discounting.\n\n The forfaiter fully covers the following risks associated to any\n export:\n (a) Country Risk (Political and Transfer Risk)\n This will absorb the full political risk without recourse or\n residual liability.\n Extraordinary state measures or political incidents like war,\n revolution, invasion or civil unrest could block or disable\n payments.\n There could be also the inability or unwillingness of states or\n other official bodies to effect payment in the currency agreed\n upon, including the risk of moratorium.\n (b) Currency Risk\n Floating exchange rates can have the effect of changing the\n contra"},{"id":1325,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"moratorium.\n (b) Currency Risk\n Floating exchange rates can have the effect of changing the\n contract value by a considerable amount when converted into\n the exporter's own currency, and can lead to a loss for the\n eventual holder of the claim.\n (c) Commercial Risk\n\n 230\n\f This can be referred to as inability or unwillingness of the\n obligor or guarantor to fulfil its obligations on due date.\n (c) Interest Rate Risk\n All forfaiting cost (discount, days of grace, commission] are\n binding and remain unchanged during the whole financing\n period.\n (d) Instant Cash\n We allow you to generate instant cash which relieves your\n balance sheet and improves your liquidity. Your credit sale is\n transformed into a cash sale.\n (e) Flexibility and Simplicity\n Simple documentation is generally achieved even for tailor-\n made financing solutions.\n\n Complete credit administration and collection, including"},{"id":1326,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"en for tailor-\n made financing solutions.\n\n Complete credit administration and collection, including\n relevant costs, will be handled by the forfaiter.\n\n6.6.2 General Aspects of Forfaiting\n Repayments/Amounts\n Most likely, repayment will be by periodic instalments. Where debt is\n evidenced by promissory notes or bills of exchange, a series of drafts\n will be issued, usually at six-month intervals.\n\n Currency\n The debt is normally denominated in US Dollars or any convertible\n currency. Since the cost of forfaiting is, for the most part, determined\n by the forfaiter's funding cost, a weak currency would make such a\n forfaiting transaction just too expensive. It is essential that payments\n be made in effective currency. This means that the debtor may not\n pay in another [e.g. his local) currency. To ensure this, the drafts will\n always carry the effective clause.\n\n Discounting\n The forfaite"},{"id":1327,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ncy. To ensure this, the drafts will\n always carry the effective clause.\n\n Discounting\n The forfaiter will discount the drafts after the exporter has delivered\n the goods to the importer and handed over the required documents\n to the forfaiter.\n\n 231\n\fTrade Financing Facilities\n\n The discount (interest) will be deducted from the face value (nominal)\n of the drafts and the resulting net proceeds will be paid out\n immediately.\n\n Types of Instruments\n Promissory Note/Bill of Exchange\n A great majority of forfeitable obligations take the form of either\n promissory notes issued by the debtor in favour of the beneficiary or\n bills of exchange drawn by the beneficiary on and accepted by the\n debtor. These drafts will generally be guaranteed by a bank Aval or\n a separate bank guarantee of the debtor's bank. The first reason for\n the predominance of these forms of debt instrument is a matter of"},{"id":1328,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"or's bank. The first reason for\n the predominance of these forms of debt instrument is a matter of\n familiarity. Long experience in dealing with such paper has led to\n considerable ease of handling by all parties and generally facilitates\n a quick and uncomplicated transaction.\n\n \"Without Recourse\" Clause\n By transferring the drafts, the exporter also transfers his claim to the\n forfaiter. This is done by an endorsement on the back of the draft, the\n exporter being the endorser and the forfaiter being the endorsee. A\n sample endorsement would read \"Please pay to the order of forfaiter\n without recourse - signed exporter\". This clause excludes the\n endorsee's right of recourse against the previous holder of the draft -\n the main characteristic of forfaiting.\n\n 232\n\f\"Effective/Net of Deduction” Clause\nAll drafts should bear this clause, which ensures that payment may\nonly be effected in th"},{"id":1329,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"on” Clause\nAll drafts should bear this clause, which ensures that payment may\nonly be effected in the currency agreed upon and not in any local\ncurrency. The clause will read: \"effective payment to be made in\nUnited States Dollars only, without deduction for and free of any tax,\nimpost, levy or duty present or future of any nature.\" A draft bearing\nthis clause is issued \"in international format\".\n\nBook Receivables/Letters of Credit\nLetters of credit with deferred payment clause can be forfaited as well.\nHowever, transactions tend to be more complex, since all maturities\nare evidenced by a single document, made out in favour of the\nbeneficiary. The obligation is often not transferable without specific\npermission from the obligor.\n\nForms of Bank Security\nGuarantee and Aval\nAs mentioned above, drafts will generally be guaranteed by a bank\nAval or a separate bank guarantee. The guarantor wil"},{"id":1330,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":", drafts will generally be guaranteed by a bank\nAval or a separate bank guarantee. The guarantor will usually be an\ninternationally active bank resident in the importer's country and able\nto ascertain the importer's creditworthiness firsthand. This security is\nimportant for the exporter as it confirms the client's ability to fulfil his\nobligations. Guarantees and Avals are essentially similar, both being,\nin their simplest form, a promise to pay a certain sum on a given date\nin the event of non-payment by the original debtor. In the case of a\nguarantee, the promise takes the form of a separate document signed\nby the guarantor setting out in full all conditions relating to the\ntransaction, whereas an Aval is affixed and duly signed directly on\neach promissory note or bill of exchange.\n\nAn Aval makes the avalising bank a primary obligor where a guarantee\ndoes not. Besides, an Aval is trans"},{"id":1331,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"val makes the avalising bank a primary obligor where a guarantee\ndoes not. Besides, an Aval is transferable by nature but a guarantee\nmust state that it is unconditional and fully transferable. The simplicity\nand clarity, together with its inherent abstractness and transferability,\nmake an Aval the preferred form of security for forfaiting.\n\n 233\n\fTrade Financing Facilities\n\n Export Risk Insurance\n Claims covered by export credit agencies (ECA], e.g. EXIM Bank, ERG,\n Hermes, may of course also be forfaited. The discount rate would be\n reduced accordingly. Unlike an ECA, the forfaiter will be able to\n purchase the total claim without restriction.\n\n6.6.3 Technical Aspects of Forfaiting\n\n Cost\n Discount Rate\n The claims will be discounted at an all-in discount rate. The rate is\n usually quoted as margin over LIBOR (funding cost) and is made up\n of the following:\n ▪ Cost of covering commerci"},{"id":1332,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"d as margin over LIBOR (funding cost) and is made up\n of the following:\n ▪ Cost of covering commercial, country and interest rate\n risk; and\n ▪ Cost of funds based on the Euromarkets rates (LIBOR).\n\n Grace Days\n Grace days are added to each maturity when calculating interest.\n These will compensate for delays in payment that are common in\n certain countries.\n\n Commitment Fees\n Exporters will often need a forfaiter's firm commitment to purchase a\n claim long before the delivery of goods. A commitment fee will then\n be charged from the day of commitment until disbursement.\n\n Documentation\n As mentioned earlier, documentation is usually simple and\n straightforward.\n The following will apply to a claim evidenced by drafts:\n (a) Promissory Notes/Bills of Exchange in international format\n avalised by a bank;\n (b) Confirmed copy of underlying L/C, including all amendments (if\n any);\n (c) Confir"},{"id":1333,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ed by a bank;\n (b) Confirmed copy of underlying L/C, including all amendments (if\n any);\n (c) Confirmed copy of commercial invoice and shipping documents\n\n 234\n\f (Bill of Lading);\n (d) Confirmation of the authenticity and validity of all signatures\n appearing on the documentation;\n (e) In case the claim will be evidenced by a letter of credit, the\n following will apply:\n (f) Conformed copy of underlying L/C, including all amendments,\n (g) Conformed copy of commercial invoice and shipping\n documents,\n (h) Letter of assignment of proceeds in favour of the forfaiter,\n (i) Notification of assignment to the L/C issuing and advising bank\n as well as their relevant acknowledgement,\n (j) Confirmation by the L/C issuing and advising/confirming bank\n that they accept the forfaiter as the new beneficiary under the\n L/C and that they will pay at maturity directly to him,\n (k) Confirmation by the L/C"},{"id":1334,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"iary under the\n L/C and that they will pay at maturity directly to him,\n (k) Confirmation by the L/C issuing and advising/confirming bank\n that documents under the L/C have been taken up without any\n reserve,\n (l) Confirmation of the authenticity and validity of all signatures\n appearing on the documentation.\n\n Guarantee Obligation of the Exporter\n The exporter is obliged to meet all terms and conditions agreed to in\n the forfaiting agreement, i.e.:\n • to duly fulfil the basic agreement (delivery of forfeited goods);\n • to deliver authentic documents to the forfaiter.\n\n6.7 LEASING\n A \"lease\" is defined as a contract between a lessor and a lessee for\n the hire of a specific asset for a specified period on payment of\n periodic rental. It is a contractual arrangement calling for the lessee\n (user) to pay the lessor (owner) for use of an asset.\n\n An importer by means of a leasing agreement i"},{"id":1335,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"user) to pay the lessor (owner) for use of an asset.\n\n An importer by means of a leasing agreement in his country or from\n a finance house in the exporter's country can obtain the use of capital\n assets.\n\n 235\n\fTrade Financing Facilities\n\n Broadly put, a lease agreement is a contract between two parties: the\n lessor and the lessee. The lessor is the legal owner of the asset; the\n lessee obtains the right to use the asset in return for rental payments.\n\n6.7.1 Terms\n (a) Lessor:\n The party, who owns the equipment, permits the use of the\n same to the other party on payment of a periodical amount.\n (b) Lessee:\n The party who acquires the right to use the equipment for\n which he pays periodically.\n\n6.7.2 Lease Rental\n This is the consideration received by the lessor in respect of a\n transaction and includes:\n (a) Interest on the lessor's investment;\n (b) Charges borne by the lessor, such as r"},{"id":1336,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"and includes:\n (a) Interest on the lessor's investment;\n (b) Charges borne by the lessor, such as repairs, maintenance,\n insurance, etc.;\n (c) Depreciation; and\n (d) Service charges.\n\n6.7.3 Types of Leases\n (a) Financial Lease\n This type of lease, which is for a long period, provides for the\n use of asset during the primary lease period which denotes\n almost the entire life of the asset. The lessor assumes the role\n of a financier and hence services of repairs, maintenance, etc.,\n are not provided by him. The legal title is retained by the lessor\n who has no option but to terminate the lease agreement.\n\n The principal and interest of the lessor is recouped by him\n during the desired payback period in the form of lease rentals.\n The finance lease is also called capital lease, which is a loan in\n disguise. The lessor thus is typically a financial institution and\n does not render specialis"},{"id":1337,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"an in\n disguise. The lessor thus is typically a financial institution and\n does not render specialised service in connection with the\n asset.\n\n 236\n\f(b) Operating Lease\n This is where the asset is not wholly amortised during the non-\n cancellable period, if any, of the lease and where the lessor\n does not rely on its profit on the rentals in the non- cancellable\n period. In this type of lease, the lessor, who bears the cost of\n insurance, machinery, maintenance, repair, etc, is unable to\n realise the full cost of equipment and other incidental charges\n during the initial period of lease. The lessee uses the asset for\n a specified time. The lessor bears the risk of obsolescence and\n incidental risks. Either party to the lease may terminate the\n lease after giving due notice of the same since the asset may\n be leased out to other willing lessees.\n\n(c) Sale and Lease-Back Leasing\n To raise"},{"id":1338,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"e the asset may\n be leased out to other willing lessees.\n\n(c) Sale and Lease-Back Leasing\n To raise funds, a company may sell an asset which belongs to\n her and subsequently lease the asset and make rental payment\n to the buyer who thereby turns to the lessor. This is done to\n avoid capital being tied down with capital-intensive assets.\n\n(d) Sales Aid Lease\n Under this arrangement, the lessor agrees with the\n manufacturer to market his product through his leasing\n operations, in return for which the manufacturer agrees to pay\n him a commission.\n\n(e) Specialised Service Lease\n In this type of agreement, the lessor provides specialised\n personal services in addition to providing its use.\n\n 237\n\fTrade Financing Facilities\n\n (f) Cross-Border Lease\n Lease across the national frontiers is called cross-border\n leasing. A recent development in economic liberalisation,\n cross-border leasing is ga"},{"id":1339,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"cross-border\n leasing. A recent development in economic liberalisation,\n cross-border leasing is gaining greater importance in areas like\n aviation, shipping and other costly assets base which is likely\n to become obsolete due to technological changes.\n\n6.7.4 Merits of Leasing\n (a) The most important merit of leasing is flexibility. The leasing\n company modifies the arrangements to suit the lease\n requirements.\n (b) In the leasing deal, when compared to term loans from financial\n institutions, less documentation is involved.\n (c) It is an alternative source to obtain loan and other facilities from\n financial institutions. That is the reason why banking companies\n and financial institutions are now entering into leasing business\n as this method of finance is more acceptable to manufacturing\n units.\n (d) The full amount (100%) financing for the cost of equipment\n may be made available by"},{"id":1340,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"g\n units.\n (d) The full amount (100%) financing for the cost of equipment\n may be made available by a leasing company, whereas banks\n and other financial institutions may not provide for the same.\n (e) The 'Sale and Lease-Back' arrangement enables the lessee to\n borrow in case of any financial crisis.\n (f) The lessee can avail tax benefits, depending upon his tax status.\n\n6.7.5 Demerits of Leasing\n (a) In leasing, the cost of interest is very high.\n (b) The asset reverts back to the owner on the termination of the\n lease period and the lessor loses his claim on the residual\n value.\n (c) Leasing is not useful in setting up new projects as the rentals\n become payable soon after the acquisition of assets.\n\n The lessor generally leases out assets which are purchased by him\n with the help of bank credit. In the event of a default made by the\n\n 238\n\f lessor in making the payment to the bank, t"},{"id":1341,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"nk credit. In the event of a default made by the\n\n 238\n\f lessor in making the payment to the bank, the asset would be seized\n by the bank which serves as a disadvantage to the lessee.\n\n6.8 BILL DISCOUNTING\n Bills that had been accepted by an importer may be discounted by the\n bank for the exporter. The face value less discount charges is paid to\n the exporter and the bill is kept till maturity when it is presented to\n the acceptor (importer) for payment. The bank's margin or discount\n rate is the difference between the amount paid to the exporter and\n amount collected from the acceptor on due date. Trading or selling a\n bill of exchange prior to the maturity date is done at a value less than\n the par value of the bill. The amount of the discount will depend on\n the amount of time left before the bill matures, and on the perceived\n risk attached to the bill.\n\n While discounting a bill, th"},{"id":1342,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"re the bill matures, and on the perceived\n risk attached to the bill.\n\n While discounting a bill, the bank buys the bill (i.e. Bill of Exchange or\n Promissory Note) before it is due and credits the value of the bill after\n a discount charge to the customer's account. The transaction is\n practically an advance against the security of the bill and the discount\n represents the interest on the advance from the date of purchase of\n the bill until it is due for payment.\n\n Under certain circumstances, the bank may discount a bill of exchange\n instead of negotiating it. The amount the bank advances to the\n exporter also depends on his past record and the reputation of the\n drawee.\n\n Usually, the bank may want some conditions to be fulfilled to be able\n to discount a bill:\n • A bill must be an issuance bill.\n • It must have been accepted and it must bear at least two good\n signatures [e.g. of rep"},{"id":1343,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ance bill.\n • It must have been accepted and it must bear at least two good\n signatures [e.g. of reputable individuals, companies or banks,\n etc.).\n\n 239\n\fTrade Financing Facilities\n\n • The bank will normally only discount trade bills.\n • Where an issuance bill is drawn at a fixed period after sight,\n the bill must be accepted to establish the maturity\n\n The advising or confirming bank will hide the reimbursement\n instruction from the beneficiary so that his bank must present the\n documents to the nominated bank for negotiation in order to obtain\n payment under the documentary collection (DC) terms.\n\n Bills which are financed by the receiving branch, whether drawn under\n a DC or not, are treated as bills receivable by both the remitting\n branch and the receiving branches.\n\n6.8.1 Presenting a Bill\n Bills may be presented to the nominated bank in two ways:\n (a) Bill with Recourse\n We check"},{"id":1344,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"a Bill\n Bills may be presented to the nominated bank in two ways:\n (a) Bill with Recourse\n We check the documents and confirm that they comply with\n the DC terms, and send the bill with the original DC to the\n nominated bank requesting payment. The nominated bank\n needs not recheck the documents and it can claim a refund\n from us in the case of an unspotted discrepancy. We pay our\n customer after receipt of funds from the nominated bank.\n\n (b) Bill without Recourse\n We pass the original DC and unchecked documents to the\n nominated bank on a collection basis, requesting payment. The\n nominated bank has to check the documents in the normal\n way. Usually, we present documents to the nominated bank\n without recourse:\n • When the opening bank is a member of the bank\n nominated for payment, acceptance or negotiation;\n • When the nominated bank has confirmed the DC; or\n • When the nominated ba"},{"id":1345,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"eptance or negotiation;\n • When the nominated bank has confirmed the DC; or\n • When the nominated bank is the drawee.\n\n 240\n\f The remitting bank may finance a good creditworthy exporter\n by purchasing or discounting his collection bills under an\n \"Export Line\". However,\n • If the importer refuses a bill the bank has purchased,\n the bank must be sure of being able to get a refund.\n • The importer must be reliable. The bank usually tries to\n avoid the risk of refusal by keeping in touch with large\n banks.\n • The bank always ensures that when a bill is purchased,\n it is drawn on an approved drawee’s limits.\n\n6.8.2 Steps\n (i) The importer, confirming or advising bank is made to return\n the tenor bill of exchange to the exporter after acceptance by\n the drawee or acceptor based on the clause that would have\n been included in the export trade terms.\n (ii) Discounting facility is asked for by t"},{"id":1346,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"at would have\n been included in the export trade terms.\n (ii) Discounting facility is asked for by the exporter by approaching\n his banker or a discount house.\n (iii) Credibility of parties to the bill is assessed before approval or\n otherwise.\n (iv) Bill (bills) is (are) discounted, while the proceeds less\n discounting charges are made available to the exporter. A\n percentage of the face value of the bill is made available to the\n exporter and not the full face value.\n (v) On due date, bill is presented to the acceptor before payment\n through the international clearing system.\n (vi) Where the bill is returned unpaid, the discounting house will\n have recourse to all parties to the bill but the exporter is held\n primarily liable.\n Advantages\n • It increases the exporter's turnover and also improves cash\n flow position.\n • Bills can be discounted with other financial institutions apart\n fr"},{"id":1347,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"improves cash\n flow position.\n • Bills can be discounted with other financial institutions apart\n from the exporter's bank.\n • Credit can be granted to foreign buyers while operating cycle\n is still maintained by the exporter.\n\n 241\n\fTrade Financing Facilities\n\n Disadvantages\n • Where the importer cannot pay, the discounting institution has\n the recourse to the exporter.\n • Bill discounting might be viewed as a sign of financial distress\n by other creditors because it is an off-balance sheet item.\n • Cost of protesting bill is usually expensive and subject to the\n applicable laws in the importer's country.\n • Bills discounting may not be available to a relatively new\n exporter because it is similar to unsecured lending.\n\n6.8.3 Commercial Papers\n A commercial paper is an unsecured short-term instrument issued by\n a company for financing of accounts receivables, inventories and\n meeting sh"},{"id":1348,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"m instrument issued by\n a company for financing of accounts receivables, inventories and\n meeting short-term liabilities with a fixed maturity. It is a money-\n market security issued (sold) by large corporations to obtain funds to\n meet short-term debt obligations (for example, payroll), and it is\n backed only by an issuing bank or corporation's promise to pay the\n face amount on the maturity date specified on the note. Since it is not\n backed by collateral, only firms with excellent credit ratings from a\n recognised credit rating agency will be able to sell their commercial\n paper at a reasonable price. Commercial paper is usually sold at a\n discount from the face value, and generally carries lower interest\n repayment rates than bonds due to the shorter maturities of\n commercial paper. Typically, the longer the maturity on a note, the\n higher the interest rate the issuing institution pa"},{"id":1349,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"pically, the longer the maturity on a note, the\n higher the interest rate the issuing institution pays. Interest rates\n fluctuate with market conditions, but are typically lower than banks'\n rates.\n\n Commercial paper is a lower-cost alternative to a line of credit with a\n bank. Once a business becomes established, and builds a high credit\n rating, it is often cheaper to draw on a commercial paper than on a\n bank line of credit. Nevertheless, many companies still maintain bank\n lines of credit as a \"back-up\". Banks often charge fees for the amount\n of the line of the credit that does not have a balance because, under\n the capital regulatory regimes set out by the Basel Accords, banks\n\n 242\n\f must anticipate that such unused lines of credit will be drawn upon if\n a company gets into financial distress. They must therefore put aside\n equity capital to account for potential loan losses also"},{"id":1350,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"l distress. They must therefore put aside\n equity capital to account for potential loan losses also on the currently\n unused part of lines of credit, and will usually charge a fee for the cost\n of this equity capital.\n\n6.8.4 Features of a Commercial Paper\n (a) It is a negotiable instrument.\n (b) It is an unsecured instrument as it is not backed up by any assets\n of the company.\n (c) It can be sold by the issuing company, directly to the investors.\n\n Advantages of a Commercial Paper\n (a) It is a cost-effective way of financing working capital. It is\n cheaper than bank loans.\n (b) It is required to be rated. Good rating reduces the cost of\n capital for the company.\n (c) High credit ratings fetch a lower cost of capital.\n (d) It has a wide range of maturity that provides more flexibility.\n (e) It does not create any lien on the asset of the company.\n (f) Tradability of a ommercial paper pro"},{"id":1351,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":") It does not create any lien on the asset of the company.\n (f) Tradability of a ommercial paper provides investors with exit\n options.\n Disadvantages of a Commercial Paper\n A commercial paper suffers from the following demerits:\n • It is available to a few selected blue-chip and profitable\n companies.\n • By issuing commercial papers, the credit available from banks\n may get reduced.\n • Issue of commercial paper is strictly regulated by RBI.\n • Its usage is limited to only blue-chip companies.\n • Issuance of commercial papers brings down bank credit limits.\n • A high degree of control is exercised in the issuance of\n commercial papers.\n • Stand-by credit may become necessary.\n6.9 AVALISING\n\n 243\n\fTrade Financing Facilities\n\n This is the discounting of long-term finance instruments such as bill\n of exchange and promissory notes, particularly for capital goods, in\n the importer's country b"},{"id":1352,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"bill\n of exchange and promissory notes, particularly for capital goods, in\n the importer's country but for the benefit of the exporter. The act of\n having a third party (usually a bank or lending institution) guarantees\n the obligations of a buyer to a seller per the terms of a contract such\n as a promissory note or purchase agreement. The bank, by \"avalising\"\n the document (usually \"by aval\" will be written on the document\n itself), acts as a consignor with the buyer in the transaction. To avalise\n means \"to give one's accord\". It is a rarely used but is an effective\n method of securing the rights of the receiving party in the transaction.\n This is an obligation that a bank will only take on with lucrative\n customers. It is seen as an act of good faith by both parties.\n\n Bill Avalisation is the bank's undertaking on behalf of the importer, to\n honour payments of Bills of Exchange from t"},{"id":1353,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"is the bank's undertaking on behalf of the importer, to\n honour payments of Bills of Exchange from the supplier. This\n undertaking is similar to that of a Letter of Credit and hence provides\n the same comfort to the supplier but with an easier structure for the\n importer. This is applicable to both domestic and international trade.\n\n An \"Avalised\" bill substitutes the bank's risk for the importer’s risk,\n thus providing the exporter with assurance that payment will be met.\n This bill can subsequently be discounted or used to negotiate better\n credit terms; thus it can enhance the trading relationship with the\n importer.\n\n Advantages of Avalising to an Exporter\n • It is suitable for long-term contracts.\n • Credit risk of the exporter is spread because many parties are\n involved.\n • Exporter is able to take full benefits of the principles of time\n value of money.\n • Exporter's turnover and"},{"id":1354,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"is able to take full benefits of the principles of time\n value of money.\n • Exporter's turnover and profitability are improved.\n • Exporter's image is boosted, both locally and internationally.\n Disadvantages\n • It is subject to the stringent exchange control regulations of\n the importer's and exporter's countries.\n\n 244\n\f • Cost of litigation might be highly prohibitive.\n • There is a right of recourse to the exporter and this may\n happen at a time he is not financially prepared.\n\n6.10 LOAN OR OVERDRAFT\n Same as for importer in 6.1.1\n\n6.11 PRODUCE/HYPOTHECATION LOAN\n The flow of document is a bit different from that of the importer.\n Documents relating to the trade are used as lien and the bank collects\n the proceeds of the export to liquidate the finance granted to the\n exporter.\n\n6.11.1 Steps Involved\n • The exporter submits his request, for finance; if granted, he is\n made to sign a"},{"id":1355,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"1 Steps Involved\n • The exporter submits his request, for finance; if granted, he is\n made to sign a letter of undertaking to submit all documents\n to the bank.\n • A limit is approved for the exporter and he is made to submit\n financial documents and all other documents relating to the\n export to the bank for collection, while he forwards goods to\n the importer.\n • The bank will make sure that the exporter adheres strictly to\n the terms of contract between him and the importer.\n • He signs necessary charge forms, including a lieu on the\n documents of title which legally transfer the legal title of the\n goods to the bank.\n • The proceeds of the exports are collected by the bank to\n liquidate the exporter's indebtedness.\n\n6.12 ACCOMMODATION FINANCE\n This is similar to documentary acceptance credit except that\n accommodation finance is a post-shipment finance requiring the\n physical possess"},{"id":1356,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"credit except that\n accommodation finance is a post-shipment finance requiring the\n physical possession of the underlying bills and other shipping\n documents which may have to be deposited with the bank, while the\n bank accepts a bill drawn on it by the exporter.\n (a) The exporter discounts the accommodation bill at a free rate in\n\n 245\n\fTrade Financing Facilities\n\n the money market or with another bank.\n (b) An accommodation bill is a bill of exchange drawn and accepted\n without any underlying commercial contract between the\n drawer and the acceptor to raise finance on the strength of the\n acceptor’s name.\n (c) The underlying bill, i.e. the trade bill drawn on the importer by\n the exporter, should mature before the accommodation bill,\n because this will provide the bank with funds to meet its\n obligations under the accommodation bill.\n (d) Accommodation finance is a type of pre-import f"},{"id":1357,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"its\n obligations under the accommodation bill.\n (d) Accommodation finance is a type of pre-import financing. It can\n be used to meet obligations due under bill of exchange\n accepted under collection or documentary letter of credit.\n\n6.13 BANKER’S ACCEPTANCE\n (a) This is granted by a bank through a tenor bill of exchange to\n fill the gap in a sight documentary collection. It can be offered\n where a bank is not willing to increase its exposure to a\n customer.\n (b) The rate of return is agreed between the bank and the\n investing customers who accept the concept of bankers’\n acceptance when introduced by the bank.\n (c) The borrowing customer draws a bill of exchange on the\n investor for the agreed period.\n (d) The borrowing customer writes a post-dated cheque for the bill\n plus the interest.\n (e) The accepted bill is discounted in the money market or by the\n bank, the proceeds less the disc"},{"id":1358,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"(e) The accepted bill is discounted in the money market or by the\n bank, the proceeds less the discounting charges are credited\n to the importer's account.\n (f) The bank presents the discounted bill to the investing customer\n where account is debited directly.\n (g) At maturity, the post-dated cheque is presented to the importer\n who pays and the proceeds are credited to the investing\n customer's account.\n (h) The discounting bank earns its commission at discounting,\n while the investor earns the interest for the tenor.\n (i) It would be a direct bank acceptance where the tenor bill is\n\n 246\n\f drawn on the bank directly and it accepts it, while earning both\n the interest and the commission.\n (j) The liability of the accepting bank to the investor is like a\n guarantee which crystallises only if the importer fails to pay at\n the maturity of the discounted bill.\n (k) The cost of commercial p"},{"id":1359,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"if the importer fails to pay at\n the maturity of the discounted bill.\n (k) The cost of commercial papers is the investment yield on the\n promissory notes plus charges and fees to the finance\n company.\n\n Advantages of Accommodation Finance\n • It is simple to arrange.\n • Huge sums of money can be raised quickly where the importer\n (company) commands public respect.\n • It requires goodwill rather than tangible collateral.\n • It does not crystallise in the book of the finance house until\n the importer defaults in repayment.\n • It is more reliable because borrowing is pooled from several\n investors.\n\n Disadvantages\n • It is expensive to execute and the shorter tenor may not give\n enough room for the importer to cover costs.\n • It is not suitable or open to all importers but to blue-chip\n companies or those with top class-corporate identity,\n\n6.14 ACCEPTANCE CREDIT FACILITIES\n If the method o"},{"id":1360,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"nies or those with top class-corporate identity,\n\n6.14 ACCEPTANCE CREDIT FACILITIES\n If the method of payment agreed between the importer and exporter\n is a documentary letter of credit providing for acceptance, a period of\n credit can be obtained from the exporter by seeking an acceptance\n credit rather than a sight credit.\n\n 247\n\fTrade Financing Facilities\n\n6.15 CAPITAL MARKET FINANCING\n This could be used where the financing is required for a long term. A\n company that is into importation can decide to go to the capital market\n where the finance needed is long term.\n\n The company can raise fund through any of the following:\n (a) Ordinary Shares;\n (b) Preference shares;\n (c) Debenture stocks, convertible loan stocks, etc.\n\n Advantages\n • It can be used where investment is long-term.\n • A company can raise a very large amount compared to what\n can be got from the money market.\n • The co"},{"id":1361,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"company can raise a very large amount compared to what\n can be got from the money market.\n • The company can also raise funds on international stock\n exchanges to settle obligations due under international\n financial activities.\n • It can boost the company's image and credibility.\n\n Disadvantages\n • It can be very expensive due to various stages and\n requirements of The Securities and Exchange Commission.\n • There is need for public disclosure.\n • Ownership may be diluted.\n\n6.16 HIRE PURCHASE\n An importer may obtain the use of capital assets by means of hire\n purchase agreement. It is the acquisition of assets on credit and\n settlement of which is made through regular instalment payment over\n a period of time in accordance with the contractual agreement.\n\n6.17 BILL NEGOTIATION\n This is a type of finance under which the exporter's bill or collection is\n bought by the bank. Interest and p"},{"id":1362,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"type of finance under which the exporter's bill or collection is\n bought by the bank. Interest and proceeds from the bill are hereafter\n used by the bank to reimburse it. It is the process of transferring legal\n\n 248\n\f title of a holder or an endorser of a bill who holds it till maturity date\n to another party.\n\n6.17.1 Steps\n i. Foreign bills are submitted by the exporter to his banker or any\n other financial institution with request for negotiation.\n ii. Creditworthiness of parties to bills is assessed before\n negotiation.\n iii. Certain percentage of the face value of the bill is advanced to\n the exporter.\n iv. The exporter is charged with negotiation fee and other charges.\n v. Bills are sent to the drawee bank for collection by the exporter's\n bank.\n vi. The exporter's account is credited against the advance on\n receipt of proceeds.\n vii. Bank maintains a right of a holder against all"},{"id":1363,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"d against the advance on\n receipt of proceeds.\n vii. Bank maintains a right of a holder against all parties in case of\n dishonour.\n\n Advantages\n • It is very attractive to banks because of shorter collection and\n credit periods.\n • It is simple and requires lesser credibility.\n • It may increase the exporter's turnover and profitability\n because of increased sales volume.\n • It is a cheap means of obtaining finance.\n\n Disadvantages\n • Where bill is returned unpaid, the negotiating bank has\n recourse to all parties to the bill, including the exporter.\n • It may be viewed negatively by other creditors and bankers\n because it is an off-balance sheet arrangement.\n • Cost of protesting non-payment of bill is highly prohibitive.\n • It is not suitable for small exporters because it is like an\n unsecured lending.\n\n6.18 RED CLAUSE LETTER OF CREDIT\n\n 249\n\fTrade Financing Facilities\n\n See types of"},{"id":1364,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"secured lending.\n\n6.18 RED CLAUSE LETTER OF CREDIT\n\n 249\n\fTrade Financing Facilities\n\n See types of letters of credit in Chapter One.\n\n6.19 DIRECT CREDIT PURCHASES\n Under this arrangement, the exporter, who may be a blue-chip\n company, issues local purchase order with the deserved credit period\n matching the payment of terms of the international order and repays\n the local supplier from the export proceeds. This is common to items\n of raw materials and produce.\n\n6.20 COUNTERTRADE\n Significant development in recent trade transactions involving Third\n World Nations is the increase in the use of barter and countertrade.\n ‘Barter’, the simplest form of trade transaction, is the two-way\n exchange of goods effectuated without the use of currency.\n Countertrade, in comparison, is an agreement between an importing\n nation and a foreign seller requiring that, as a condition for the\n completion of"},{"id":1365,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ween an importing\n nation and a foreign seller requiring that, as a condition for the\n completion of the import transaction, the seller separately purchases\n certain goods from the importing nation. Both are methods of\n effectuating the transfer of goods, services and technologies to\n nations experiencing hard currency shortages or low currency values.\n In response to new economic demands created by constantly\n changing international conditions, Third World Nations are turning\n with greater frequency to these and similar innovative methods of\n structuring their trade transactions in an effort to deal with the ever-\n worsening problem of financing needed imports.\n\n The countertrade transaction consists of a parallel set of obligations\n wherein the parties each undertake to sell goods or technology to the\n other in separate but related transactions. Countertrade can be best\n understood as"},{"id":1366,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"hnology to the\n other in separate but related transactions. Countertrade can be best\n understood as a two-step process. First, the \"seller,\" typically a private\n corporation [the \"first party\"], agrees to present its goods to the\n \"buyer,\" typically a governmental purchasing agency in a Communist\n or Third World country [the \"second party\"] for an agreed purchase\n price in hard currency. Second, the first party then agrees, as a\n condition of the completion of this first transaction, to purchase goods\n supplied by the second party, again in exchange for hard currency. In\n\n 250\n\fessence, the second party agrees to purchase goods from the first\nparty upon the condition that the first party agrees to purchase goods\nfrom the second party. The two obligations are \"linked\" or \"cross-\nreferenced\" to each other by either a provision in one of the two\nagreements or in a separate document (typical"},{"id":1367,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"to each other by either a provision in one of the two\nagreements or in a separate document (typically referred to as a\n\"protocol\"). While the two obligations are often negotiated at the\nsame time and part of the same overall commercial setting, the two\nshould be viewed as distinct transactions with separate contracts and\nseparate currency payments.\n\nTypes of Countertrade\n• Counter purchase;\n• Compensation;\n• Reverse Countertrade;\n• Swap;\n• Commodity Exchange Agreements; and\n• Switch.\n\nAdvantages of Countertrade\nRegardless of the complexity, companies still use countertrade as a\nstrategy for growth because it:\n• Allows for entry into difficult markets.\n• Increases company sales where you might not otherwise have\n business.\n• Overcomes credit difficulties.\n• Allows for disposal of declining or surplus products.\n• Gains competitive advantage over competition (you don’t want\n to lose a mark"},{"id":1368,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"or surplus products.\n• Gains competitive advantage over competition (you don’t want\n to lose a market share as a result of competitors).\n\nDisadvantage of Countertrade\nA disadvantage to countertrade is that the value of a deal - the goods\nbeing exchanged - may be uncertain, causing a significant price\nvolatility.\n\nOther disadvantages include, but are not limited to:\n\n 251\n\fTrade Financing Facilities\n\n • Time-consuming. As in any unconventional tactic, there will be\n haggling over the good trades, so expect a long-drawn-out\n negotiation until all parties are satisfied.\n • Complexity in the nature of the negotiations.\n • Higher transaction costs (including brokerage, for instance).\n Costs can quickly add up, especially while looking for a buyer\n for the goods, commissions to middlemen and so forth.\n • Logistical issues, especially if commodities are involved.\n • Greater uncertainty on the"},{"id":1369,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"forth.\n • Logistical issues, especially if commodities are involved.\n • Greater uncertainty on the value of the goods being traded and\n uncertainty on the quality of the goods.\n\n6.21 CONTINGENT FACILITIES\n A bid bond (also called a tender bond) is issued to ensure that the\n exporter submits realistic bids under the tender process and to protect\n the importer for any loss that might occur if the exporter fails to sign\n the contract. A bid bond also assures the importer that the exporter\n will comply with the terms of the contract in the event that the tender\n is accepted. Bid bonds are usually issued for 2% to 5% of the tender\n amount.\n\n A bid bond is often a condition for the consideration of a bid.\n\n Advance Payment Bond\n This ensures repayment to the importer of an agreed percentage of\n the contract amount (typically 10%-30% of the contract amount) if\n the exporter does not fulfil its"},{"id":1370,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"the contract amount (typically 10%-30% of the contract amount) if\n the exporter does not fulfil its contractual obligations.\n\n Performance Bond\n Safeguards the importer, should the exporter fail to meet its\n contractual obligations. Performance bonds are usually issued for 10%\n to 20% of the contract amount but may be fixed by the local law of\n the importer's country. Obligations under a performance bond could\n concern supply obligations or obligations concerning function and\n quality during the agreed period of the guarantee.\n\n Retention Bond\n\n 252\n\fIn the supply of factory plant, machinery and other capital goods, it is\noften agreed that the buyer may withhold 5%-10% of the contract\namount for a guarantee period, for example 12 months after the plant\nor machine(s) are up and running.\n\nThe exporter may wish to receive the full contract amount before the\nend of the contract period by is"},{"id":1371,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"he exporter may wish to receive the full contract amount before the\nend of the contract period by issuing a retention bond that covers the\namount that would otherwise be withheld.\n\nThe exporter will request its bank to issue a retention bond in favour\nof the buyer. Once the buyer receives the retention bond, he will\ntransfer the amount of the bond value direct to the exporter by\ninternational money transfer.\n\nPayment Guarantee\nA payment guarantee ensures payment to the exporter if the importer\ndoes not fulfil its payment obligations. A payment guarantee can be\nissued in the form of an endorsement on a draft, also known as an\n“aval”. In such cases, Danske Bank guarantees the payment of the\ndraft.\n\nSUMMARY AND CONCLUSION\nInternational Financing Arrangements are very crucial to the growth\nof international trade. Banks and other non-bank financial institutions\nare the channels through which"},{"id":1372,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"international trade. Banks and other non-bank financial institutions\nare the channels through which financing facilities are made available\nto both exporters and importers of goods and services. The facilities\nrange from loan or overdraft to technical ones like factoring, forfaiting,\nbills negotiation, etc. There are, however, more finance options for\nexporters than importers.\n\n 253\n\fTrade Financing Facilities\n\n REVISION QUESTIONS AND ANSWERS\n\nEssay Questions\n\nQuestion 1\n“Forfaiting should not be seen as being in competition with other export\nfinance schemes. It is an extra option for exporters”. - Forfaiting by Barclays.\n a. What is forfaiting?\nb. Describe how it works.\nc. Give at least five benefits which an exporter can derive from forfaiting\n and its disadvantages.\n\nAnswer\n(a) Forfaiting is a method of discounting medium-term financial\n instruments for an exporter by forfaiting house"},{"id":1373,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"ng is a method of discounting medium-term financial\n instruments for an exporter by forfaiting houses (banks, finance\n houses or discount houses) which are usually subsidiaries of\n multinational banks.\n Forfaiting is common on the continent of Europe and normally used\n for export sale involving capital goods where payment will be made\n over a number of years.\n\n(b) How It Works\n i. An exporter of capital goods finds an overseas buyer who\n wants medium-term credit to finance the purchase.\n ii. The buyer must be willing to pay about 15% of the cost at once\n and pay the balance in regular instalments annually or semi-\n annually.\n iii. The buyer will issue series of promissory notes with a final\n maturity date of about five years ahead but for regular\n payments every six months.\n iv. The buyer will be required to find a bank which is willing to\n guarantee (Avalise) the promissory notes or bil"},{"id":1374,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"will be required to find a bank which is willing to\n guarantee (Avalise) the promissory notes or bill of exchange.\n v. The exporter contracts a forfaiter. The forfaiter will want to\n know the amount of the credit involved and the period of time.\n vi. Discounting is normally at a fixed rate, notified by the forfaiter\n to the exporter when the financing arrangement is made.\n\n 254\n\f vii. The exporter will deliver the goods and receive the avalised\n promissory notes or accepted bills. He will now sell them to the\n forfaiter who will purchase them without recourse to the\n exporter and make discounting proceeds (less discounting\n charges) available to the exporter.\n viii. The currencies used in forfaiting can be any of the convertible\n currencies traded on the Eurocurrency markets.\n ix. The forfaiter is repaid gradually, as the notes or bills mature.\n\n(c) Benefits of Forfaiting to an Exporter"},{"id":1375,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"aiter is repaid gradually, as the notes or bills mature.\n\n(c) Benefits of Forfaiting to an Exporter\n i. Debts are converted into cash in the exporter’s books with no\n liabilities, either actual or contingent, being created.\n ii. The exporter's liquidity position is improved because he\n receives cash at once.\n iii. The exporter does not have the exchange risks, because these\n are now borne by the forfaiter.\n iv. There is no interest rate risk, because the rate of discount is\n known from the beginning.\n v. The exporter is relieved of administration problems and costs\n of debts collection.\n vi. It improves the goodwill of the exporter to the importer.\n vii. It allows the exporter to have access to the international\n financial markets financing.\n viii. Forfaiting can be arranged easily.\n\nDisadvantages of Forfaiting to an Exporter\n(i) Costs can be high and there is no interest rate subsidy.\n("},{"id":1376,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"vantages of Forfaiting to an Exporter\n(i) Costs can be high and there is no interest rate subsidy.\n(ii) It may be difficult to find an institution that will be prepared to\n guarantee the importer's liabilities.\n\nQuestion 2\nAs the global economy becomes increasingly competitive, companies are\nrealising that they must be more flexible with customers. To maintain good\nsales is being able to offer international customers better financing terms\nwhich is an important part of any sales package. Since payment from\ncompanies in other countries involves elements of effective monitoring,\n\n 255\n\fTrade Financing Facilities\n\nunlike those faced with domestic companies, credit becomes more of an\nobstacle. With bigger export sales, bad debts may occur which could get to\ncrippling level if not well managed. One of the ways in which this could be\navoided is to engage in international factoring.\n\nRequired:"},{"id":1377,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"One of the ways in which this could be\navoided is to engage in international factoring.\n\nRequired:\n(a) What is International Factoring?\n(b) Identify the three (3) elements of services offered by international\n factoring companies.\n(c) Itemise four major benefits of International Factoring.\n\nAnswer\n(a) International Factoring\n This is a method in which a business owner sells accounts receivable\n at a discount to a third party, known as a factoring company, to raise\n capital.\n i. Factoring is the cash management tool of choice for many\n companies.\n ii. Factoring is not a loan, as it does not create any liability in the\n balance sheet.\n iii. It serves as the credit sales administration of the company that\n engages the services of a factor.\n iv. It provides liquidity to the engaging company.\n v. It is a short-term solution usually for 2yearsor less.\n\n(b) Three Elements of Services by Intern"},{"id":1378,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"v. It is a short-term solution usually for 2yearsor less.\n\n(b) Three Elements of Services by International Factoring\n Companies:\n i. Managing the receivables, where an accounts receivable\n manager handles collection and provides reporting and book-\n keeping services.\n ii. Furnishing credit protection on receivables and establishing\n credit lines.\n iii. Lending against receivables.\n\n(c) Major Benefits of International Factoring:\n i. Factoring can be customised and managed so that it provides\n\n 256\n\f necessary capital when a company needs it.\n ii. The financing does not show up in the balance sheet of the\n company as a debt.\n iii. Factoring is based on the quality of the customer's debtors, not\n the company's credit or business history.\n iv. Factoring provides a line of credit based on sales, not the\n company's net worth.\n v. Unlike in a conventional loan, factoring has no limit for the"},{"id":1379,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"s, not the\n company's net worth.\n v. Unlike in a conventional loan, factoring has no limit for the\n amount of financing.\n\nQuestion 3\nYour customer, Ilodibe Steels Limited, imports iron rods and steel from\nJapan and South Korea and occasionally exports to Jamaica and Guyana. As\na result, it has a domiciliary account, which it uses to finance imports.\nImports have always been invoiced in Pounds Sterling, but your customer's\ncash flow position has suffered because there have been some delays in\nreceiving payment on the dates on export contracts. Ilodibe Steels Limited\nreadily accepts bills of exchange drawn 30 days sight sent directly to it by\nthe foreign sellers, but the funds do not get to it until some 6 weeks after\nthe bills fall due. As regards the South Korean sellers, Ilodibe Steels Limited\nwould usually forward cheques drawn in Sterling to the seller and this takes\nsome time to clea"},{"id":1380,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"mited\nwould usually forward cheques drawn in Sterling to the seller and this takes\nsome time to clear; thus putting additional pressure on the exporter's cash\nflow situation.\n\nThe company's finance director, Mr. Emeka Ayim, calls to see you to find out\nif there is any way in which you can assist the company in solving the\nproblem of its overseas suppliers. Your customers are not willing to arrange\ndocumentary letters of credit but asked you if there is any scheme under\nwhich the bank can assist in trying to cut down the delay before the overseas\nalternative funds.\n\n 257\n\fTrade Financing Facilities\n\nRequired:\nDescription of:\na. The basic method by which the bank can help the customer to make\n payment without unacceptable delays.\nb. List the steps in your suggestion in (i) above.\n\nAnswer\n(a)\ni. Since the customer wants to avoid the transaction cost of\n documentary credits, the bank should"},{"id":1381,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"i. Since the customer wants to avoid the transaction cost of\n documentary credits, the bank should suggest documentary bills for\n collection method supported by a standby letter of credit to Ilodibe\n Steels Limited in which the bank can use its foreign correspondents in\n Japan and South Korea to make payment on the bills.\nii. The suggested method is hypothecation, under which the required\n credit would be made available to Ilodibe Steels Limited while shipping\n documents are passed on the bank and payment made by the\n customer when goods arrive. In the same vein, the foreign sellers\n could be asked to draw series of sight bills for acceptance which they\n could negotiate overseas.\niii. Funds on the customer's domiciliary account should be committed to\n the South Korea import payment and if local currency is needed to\n fund local operations. The balance should be used as collateral for\n b"},{"id":1382,"source":"FINANCE_IN_THE_GLOBAL_MARKET","subject":"Finance in the Global Market","priority":1,"text":"local currency is needed to\n fund local operations. The balance should be used as collateral for\n borrowing.\n\n(b) Hypothecation\n i. The importer must be of good standing and undoubted\n integrity.\n ii. The importer applies to his bank.\n iii. The request must indicate terms of payment.\n iv. Bankers will assess the request and, if found\n suitable/feasible, a marked limit by way of overdraft, loan,\n etc., is set.\n v. Banker will create a charge by way of lien on the title\n document, and make the importer transfer the legal title to\n the document to the bank.\n\n 258\n\fvi. The banker will make it a condition that all documents for the\n export must pass through it for onward dispatch to the\n importer.\nvii. The documents of title, when eventually held by the bank,\n will be released to the importer to enable him take delivery\n of the goods against a trust letter or trust receipt and a letter\n of p"}];
  window.KB = (window.KB || []).concat(chunk);
})();
